\par\par \b \uc2 *S&PGR Affirms Malaysia Ratings; Outlook Stable\b0\par\par\uc2 3,333 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 Juni 2023\par 12:27\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2023, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 27 Jun 2023 06:27 ET  Press Release: S&PGR Affirms Malaysia Ratings; Outlook Stable\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par The following is a press release from S&P Global Ratings: \par  \par OVERVIEW \par      -- As the Malaysian economy recovers from the pandemic-related slowdown, \par we believe its medium-term growth prospects are better than most other \par sovereigns at similar income levels. \par      -- Although its fiscal consolidation plans were delayed, we expect the \par growing economy and conducive policymaking to gradually stabilize government \par finances. \par      -- We affirmed our 'A-/A-2' foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and 'A/A-1' local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  \par sovereign credit ratings on Malaysia. \par      -- The stable outlook reflects our expectations that Malaysia's steady \par growth momentum and strong external position will hold over the next two \par years. \par  \par RATING ACTION \par On June 27, 2023, S&P Global Ratings affirmed its 'A-' long-term and 'A-2' \par short-term foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  sovereign credit ratings on Malaysia. We also \par affirmed our 'A' long-term and 'A-1' short-term local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  ratings on \par Malaysia. \par  \par The outlook on the long-term rating remains stable. \par  \par OUTLOOK \par The stable outlook reflects our expectations that Malaysia's steady growth \par momentum and fiscal policy will allow modest improvements in fiscal \par performance over the next two to three years. \par  \par Downside scenario \par We may lower the ratings if economic growth suffers a prolonged downturn that \par lowers the trend growth in real GDP per capita to levels in line with that of \par peers. Downward rating pressure could also build if political stability in \par Malaysia deteriorates such that policymaking becomes materially less \par predictable. \par  \par Upside scenario \par We may raise the ratings on Malaysia if fiscal outcomes outperform our \par forecasts. Such a scenario may be realized through continuing political \par stability that would narrow deficits sustainably. Consequently, government net \par debt would fall below 60% of GDP or interest payments decline below 10% of \par government revenues. \par  \par RATIONALE \par Our ratings on Malaysia are underpinned by the country's strong external \par position and monetary policy flexibility. In addition, its trend economic \par growth rate is faster than sovereigns of similar income level. \par  \par Although Malaysia's budget deficits remain high, we expect its growth dynamics \par to offset vulnerabilities associated with an elevated government debt stock \par and weak fiscal performance. Political commitment to resume fiscal \par consolidation post-pandemic also exists, in our view. \par  \par Institutional and economic profile: Medium-term growth prospects remain \par healthy; more conducive policymaking environment on abated political risks \par      -- Malaysia's real GDP growth accelerated to 8.7% in 2022, sustained by \par strong exports, high energy prices, and pent-up domestic demand following the \par reopening of the economy. \par      -- Growth in 2023 will moderate to 4% from a high base effect and a \par weaker global growth environment. \par      -- Following the November 2022 elections, the political landscape appears \par to be less fluid with the formation of a new coalition government. \par  \par Malaysia's growth momentum from 2022 has continued into this year, with \par first-quarter GDP rising 5.6% year on year. This was underpinned by a further \par improvement in employment, recovery in tourism activity, and continued capital \par investments on both private and public fronts. Nevertheless, we expect \par full-year economic expansion to moderate to 4% as a weakened global growth \par environment takes hold. \par  \par The Malaysian economy is well-diversified and has a record of resilience \par following periods of adversity. It has a mature electrical and electronics \par (E&E) sector that supplies components and finished products to major global \par players. We forecast the economy will expand on average 4.5% annually over \par 2024-2026. With this, Malaysia's 10-year weighted average per capita GDP \par growth will be 3.6%, which is well above the global median for peers at \par similar income levels. \par  \par We project Malaysia's GDP per capita to be about US$12,500 in 2023, lower than \par most peers' in the same rating category but notably higher than that of most \par of its Southeast Asian neighbors except Singapore and Brunei. \par  \par After the landmark 2018 elections, which saw the end of United Malays National \par Organisation (UMNO) party's 61 years of uninterrupted rule, Malaysian politics \par entered an era of flux and uncertainty. With no single party able to command \par parliamentary majority, the past five years has seen a number of changes in \par government leadership, parties with wide ideological differences forming \par unstable coalitions, and parliamentarians frequently switching camps. \par  \par The general elections in November 2022 resulted in the formation of a new \par coalition government under Pakatan Harapan (PH). \f28 \uc2 \f2 Datuk Seri Anwar Ibrahim\f28 \uc2 \f2  \par became the Prime Minister of Malaysia. He is the fifth in five years. Although \par PH won 82 seats, the party did not reach the simple majority of 112 seats \par needed to form government. As a result, the coalition relies on support from \par partners led by UMNO and includes parties representing the East Malaysian \par states of Sabah and Sarawak. \par  \par Malaysia's institutions have supported generally effective policymaking for a \par long time. We believe the depth of institutional strength mitigates the risk \par of political instability. Uncertainties regarding policymaking have somewhat \par subsided under the new Anwar administration. The anti-party hopping law, which \par was passed in October 2022, appears to be effective so far in preventing \par lawmakers from jumping ship to destabilize the ruling government. \par  \par Flexibility and performance profile: Weak fiscal profile balanced by a solid \par external position \par      -- Deficits will remain elevated in 2023 owing to an expansionary budget \par to support the economy. \par      -- The fiscal position remains weak, but consolidation should resume from \par 2024 with economic recovery well on track and a more stable policy environment. \par      -- Malaysia's strong external metrics remain a key anchor of the ratings. \par  \par Malaysia's budget for 2023 remains expansionary to support growth momentum and \par alleviate inflationary pressures. That said, with the country in endemic mode, \par expenditure needs are likely to be lower now that COVID-19 support measures \par have ended. Hence, we forecast the net indebtedness of Malaysia's general \par government will rise by 4.4% of GDP in 2023, versus a 5.0% increase in 2022, \par on moderately lower general and central government deficits. \par  \par We believe healthy economic growth, alongside fiscal prudence, will be key to \par improving Malaysia's public finances. Net general government debt as a share \par of GDP last year declined to 65.7% from 70.3% in 2021. The double-digit \par nominal GDP growth had outpaced the increase in government indebtedness. \par  \par The pandemic has significantly delayed the government's fiscal consolidation \par plans. With the economy now anchored on a sustained growth path, reduced need \par for fiscal stimuli, and a more conducive policymaking environment, we believe \par the government is in a position to resume its consolidation. We forecast net \par general government debt will stabilize at about 68% of GDP in 2023-2026, with \par the annual change averaging 4.1%. \par  \par Despite the high energy prices last year, fiscal settings did not fully \par benefit. Increased royalties and duties from higher commodity prices are \par largely offset by generous fuel subsidies to the populace. The Malaysian \par government derives around 20% of its total revenue from petroleum-related \par sources. The single largest component of this revenue is the dividend paid by \par state oil company \f28 \uc2 \f2 Petroliam Nasional Bhd\f28 \uc2 \f2 . (\f28 \uc2 \f2 Petronas\f28 \uc2 \f2 ). The company paid an \par exceptionally high dividend of Malaysian ringgit (MYR) 50 billion dividend in \par 2022 and is budgeted to pay MYR40 billion in 2023. \par  \par The PH government replaced the goods and service tax (GST) in June 2018 with a \par sales and services tax, a narrower program that mobilizes less revenue. For \par fiscal consolidation to take hold sustainably, on top of healthy economic \par growth, we believe the government will try to  raise more revenue or reduce \par subsidies or both. Notably, the current administration has begun making \par inroads into a medium-term fiscal framework and enacting a Fiscal \par Responsibility Act. \par  \par The Malaysian government identifies guarantees that it has provided for \par various entities that may be unable to service their debt. We have included \par such committed guarantees in our calculation of general government debt due to \par the high likelihood that they will crystallize on the general government's \par balance sheet. \par  \par These guarantees include but are not limited to those extended to \par government-linked firms Danainfra Nasional Bhd., Prasarana Malaysia, Malaysia \par Rail-link Sdn. Bhd., and Urusharta Jamaah Sdn. Bhd. We estimate the \par government's total committed direct guarantees at 11.5% of GDP. As such, our \par calculation of the general government's net debt is higher by an equivalent \par amount. We also include legacy liabilities associated with state-linked \par investment firm 1MDB. As of end-2022, the government's 1MDB-related liability \par was MYR18.2 billion, and we incorporate it into the general government's \par balance sheet. By the end of 2023, most of the 1MDB liabilities would be \par extinguished with one remaining MYR5 billion (0.3% of GDP) sukuk maturing in \par 2039. \par  \par Committed guarantees have consistently risen in recent years, and we believe \par this will remain the case over the next three years as the government \par continues to support key infrastructure projects and associated firms. But as \par a share of GDP, these committed guarantees should hold steady or decline, in \par our view. \par  \par We believe contingent liabilities to the government from the banking sector is \par limited. Private sector leverage, as measured by total private sector debt \par (including corporate borrowings from the debt capital markets) to GDP, has \par been gradually building in Malaysia since 2008. Some borrowers may be \par vulnerable to prevailing economic difficulties and rising interest rates. \par However, we expect credit risk for the Malaysian banking system to remain \par manageable, supported by banks' prudent lending and underwriting standards. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 27 Jun 2023 06:27 ET \par Press Release: S&PGR Affirms Malaysia Ratings; -2-\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Our Banking Industry Country Risk Assessment for Malaysia is '4' ('1' being \par the strongest assessment and '10' the weakest). \par  \par Malaysia's solid external position remains a rating strength. The country has \par had consistent current account surpluses for more than two decades. We \par forecast the current account surplus will stabilize around 3% of GDP over the \par next three years, driven by a gradual recovery in tourism and continued strong \par demand for Malaysian manufacturing exports will support the country's trade \par accounts. \par  \par Most of Malaysia's external liabilities are denominated in the domestic \par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , while external assets--both official \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves and \par other claims on nonresidents--are denominated in foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . \par  \par The Malaysian economy's external position is subject to some market \par volatility. This includes periods of amplified risk aversion, which could lead \par to nonresidents reducing their holdings of local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  government debt. \par Foreign ownership of Malaysian government securities typically accounts for \par 15%-25% of the total outstanding stock. We estimate Malaysia's ratio of gross \par external financing needs to current account receipts and usable reserves will \par average 91% over 2023-2026. \par  \par The \f28 \uc2 \f2 IMF\f28 \uc2 \f2  classifies \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Malaysia\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign-exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  regime as floating, with \par intermittent \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market. The Malaysian \par ringgit depreciated substantially in 2014-2017 amid the reset in global \par commodity prices. However, pass-through of this depreciation into inflation \par was limited. In part, we believe this reflected relatively well-anchored \par inflation expectations in the country. In our view, the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has \par significant independence, which is unlikely to change in the medium term. \par  \par The consumer price index in Malaysia had increased in 2022 to 3.4%, in line \par with higher global prices and supply-side constraints. However, we expect CPI \par inflation to come in by the end of this year at a modest 2.8%. This is because \par of the government's generous subsidies on fuel and food staples which cap \par prices. \par  \par Malaysia has a deep domestic bond market, which reduces reliance on \par foreign-\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  financing. The country's high share of nonresident holders of \par ringgit-denominated government bonds leaves the country's capital markets \par exposed to potential fund outflows. But the market has generally remained \par stable, with limited impact on the ringgit, since the onset of the pandemic. \par This reflects healthy external demand for Malaysian government debt. \par  \par We estimate Malaysia's reserve coverage will be 4.6 months of current account \par payments at the end of this year. Although lower than historical levels, it \par remains sufficient, and we expect Malaysia's deep capital markets to support \par financial stability. \par  \par KEY STATISTICS \par  \par RELATED CRITERIA \par  \par      -- General Criteria: Environmental, Social, And Governance Principles In \par Credit Ratings, Oct. 10, 2021 \par      -- Criteria | Governments | Sovereigns: Sovereign Rating Methodology, \par Dec. 18, 2017 \par      -- General Criteria: Methodology For Linking Long-Term And Short-Term \par Ratings, April 7, 2017 \par      -- General Criteria: Methodology For Rating Sukuk, Jan. 19, 2015 \par      -- General Criteria: Principles Of Credit Ratings, Feb. 16, 2011 \par      -- General Criteria: Methodology: Criteria For Determining Transfer And \par Convertibility Assessments, May 18, 2009 \par  \par RELATED RESEARCH \par      -- Sovereign Ratings Score Snapshot, 