\par\par \b \uc2 *S&PGR Revises Malaysia Outlk To Stable; Afrms Ratings\b0\par\par\uc2 3,757 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 Juni 2022\par 11:47\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2022, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 27 Jun 2022 05:47 ET  Press Release: S&PGR Revises Malaysia Outlk To Stable; Afrms Ratings\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par The following is a press release from S&P Global Ratings: \par  \par OVERVIEW \par      -- Malaysia is mounting a strong economic recovery from the \par pandemic-driven slowdown and we believe its medium-term growth prospects are \par better than most other sovereigns at similar income levels. \par      -- Though its fiscal consolidation plans were delayed, we expect the \par growth dynamism of the economy and conducive policymaking to gradually \par stabilize government finances. \par      -- We revised our rating outlook on Malaysia to stable from negative. At \par the same time, we affirmed our 'A-/A-2' foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and 'A/A-1' local \par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  sovereign credit ratings on Malaysia. \par      -- The stable outlook reflects our expectations that Malaysia's steady \par growth momentum and strong external position will hold over the next two \par years. \par  \par RATING ACTION \par On June 27, 2022, S&P Global Ratings revised the outlook on its long-term \par sovereign credit ratings on Malaysia to stable from negative. \par  \par At the same time, we affirmed our 'A-' long-term and 'A-2' short-term foreign \par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  sovereign credit ratings on Malaysia. We also affirmed our 'A' \par long-term and 'A-1' short-term local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  ratings on Malaysia. \par  \par OUTLOOK \par The stable outlook reflects our expectations that Malaysia's steady growth \par momentum and strong external position will remain in place over the next two \par years. At the same time, we anticipate the policymaking environment will be \par supportive of restoring fiscal settings to a firmer footing. \par Downside scenario \par We may lower the ratings if economic growth suffers a prolonged downturn that \par lowers the trend growth in real GDP per capita to levels in line with that of \par peers. Downward rating pressure could also build if political stability in \par Malaysia deteriorates such that policymaking becomes materially less \par predictable. \par Upside scenario \par We may raise the ratings on Malaysia if fiscal outcomes outperform our \par forecasts. This would be shown from net debt stock falling below 60% of GDP or \par interest payments less than 10% of general government revenues. \par  \par RATIONALE \par We revised the outlook to stable in recognition of Malaysia's consistently \par strong growth trend that is faster than sovereigns of similar income level. \par Though Malaysia's budget deficits remain high, we expect its growth dynamics \par to offset vulnerabilities associated with its weak fiscal settings. In \par addition, political commitment to resume fiscal consolidation post-pandemic is \par strong, in our view. \par Our affirmed ratings on Malaysia reflect the country's strong external \par position, monetary policy flexibility, and record of supporting sustainable \par economic growth. The country's elevated government debt stock and weak fiscal \par performance temper these strengths. \par Institutional and economic profile: Medium-term growth prospects had \par strengthened with strong external demand and COVID-19 contained; more \par conducive policymaking environment on abated political risks. \par      -- With COVID-19 under control, Malaysia has resumed full economic \par activities and reopened its international borders, supporting recovery this \par year. \par      -- Real GDP growth will accelerate to 6.1% in 2022 and 5.0% in 2023, \par sustained by strong exports, high commodity prices and domestic demand \par following reopening of the economy. \par      -- Political landscape remains fluid but more certainty now on \par policymaking with the government reaching a compromise with the opposition. \par  \par Following Malaysia's uneven recovery last year, we are projecting a strong \par pick up in its GDP growth to hit 6.1% for 2022. Growth was curtailed in 2021 \par (3.1%) by an acute outbreak of the delta variant, which resulted in strict \par countrywide lockdowns and distancing measures. In the first quarter of this \par year, growth surprised on the upside to accelerate to 5% year on year. This \par was driven by solid performance in the manufacturing sector, in particular \par electrical and electronic goods (mainly semiconductors). \par  \par We believe this strong growth momentum will persist in the second half of the \par year. Malaysia is a net energy exporter and major producer of crude palm oil \par (CPO). The country is well positioned to benefit from the high commodity price \par environment. Despite pressures from Russia-Ukraine war and global supply-chain \par disruptions, we envisage the easing of domestic COVID-19 restrictions, \par sustained demand, and strong labor market to contribute to economic recovery. \par  \par The Malaysian economy is well-diversified and has a record of resilience \par following periods of adversity. It has a mature electrical and electronics \par (E&E) sector that supplies components and finished products to major global \par players. We expect E&E, along with the oil and gas sector taking gains from \par high energy prices, to drive strong exports over the next one to two years. We \par forecast the economy will expand on average 4.7% per annum over 2023-2025. \par With this, Malaysia's 10-year weighted average per capita GDP will be close to \par 3%, which is well above the global median for peers at similar income levels. \par  \par We project Malaysia's GDP per capita to be about US$11,900 by end-2022, lower \par than most peers' in the same rating category but notably higher than that of \par most of its Southeast Asian neighbors except Singapore and Brunei. \par  \par Since the collapse of the Pakatan Harapan (PH) coalition in March 2020, the \par political landscape of Malaysia remains fluid. In August 2021, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Muhyiddin \par Yassin\f28 \uc2 \f2   who led as prime minister for 17 months after the PH government broke \par up, lost his majority support due to struggles within his Perikatan Nasional \par (PN) coalition. Amid this political leadership crisis, Ismail Sabri Yakoob of \par the United Malays National Organization (UMNO) was appointed prime minister. \par This marked the third new prime minister appointed since the 2018 elections, \par following a 61-year period of uninterrupted rule under UMNO. \par  \par Malaysia's institutions have supported generally effective policymaking for a \par long time, and we believe their depth of capacity mitigates the risk of \par instability owing to ongoing political developments. Notably, the government \par proactively introduced stimulus measures aimed at supporting the economy as \par the pandemic raged on over the last two years. The Sabri-led administration \par has also gained recognition for successfully combating COVID-19. On the back \par of rapid deployment of vaccines and booster shots, viral caseloads had come \par down significantly. Domestic COVID-19 restrictions began easing since the \par start of the year and on April 1, the country entered endemicity phase with \par the full reopening of borders and resumption of all economic activities. \par  \par Though there remains political fluidity, we believe uncertainties regarding \par policymaking have somewhat subsided. In September 2021, the government signed \par a memorandum of understanding (MOU) with the opposition. The MOU addresses \par bipartisan cooperation on Budget 2022, political stability and long-term \par political reforms. Hence, we expect fewer disruptions to policy directions \par until at least the next general elections (which are due by September 2023). \par  \par Flexibility and performance profile: Weak fiscal profile balanced by a solid \par external position; Malaysia's fiscal consolidation path to resume \par post-pandemic. \par      -- High energy prices have yet to benefit fiscal revenues and deficits \par will remain elevated in 2022 owing to an expansionary budget to restart the \par economy. \par      -- The fiscal position remains weak, but consolidation should resume from \par 2023 with economic recovery well on track and a more stable policy environment. \par      -- Malaysia's strong external metrics remain a key anchor of the ratings. \par  \par Malaysia's budget for 2022 remains expansionary to manage the lingering \par effects of the pandemic and restart the economy. That said, with Malaysia \par entering endemic mode, expenditure needs in areas such as healthcare and \par direct fiscal transfers are likely to be lower. Hence, we forecast the net \par indebtedness of Malaysia's general government will rise by 5.1% of GDP in \par 2022, versus a 6.4% increase in 2021, on moderately lower general and central \par government deficits. \par  \par The pandemic has significantly delayed the government's fiscal consolidation \par plans. With the full reopening of the economy, reduced need for fiscal \par stimuli, and a more conducive policymaking environment, we believe the \par government is committed to resume its consolidation trajectory from next year. \par As a result, we forecast net general government debt will stabilize at about \par 70% of GDP from 2022-2025, with the annual change averaging 4%. \par  \par That said, despite prevailing high energy prices this year, fiscal settings \par have yet to benefit and remain weak. Increased royalties and duties from \par higher commodity prices are largely offset by generous fuel subsidies to the \par populace. Therefore, historically we had observed a general lag effect from \par high crude oil prices to fiscal revenue boost. The Malaysian government \par derives slightly less than 20% of its total revenue from oil-related sources. \par The single largest component of this revenue is the dividend paid by state oil \par company \f28 \uc2 \f2 Petroliam Nasional Bhd\f28 \uc2 \f2  . (\f28 \uc2 \f2 Petronas\f28 \uc2 \f2  ). The company paid a MYR25 billion \par dividend in 2021 and is budgeted to pay the same amount in 2022. In our view, \par upside to fiscal revenues will come on stream in 2023 should \f28 \uc2 \f2 Petronas\f28 \uc2 \f2   \par experience windfall profits this year and declare a higher dividend next. \par  \par The PH government replaced the goods and service tax (GST) in June 2018 with a \par sales and services tax, a narrower program that mobilizes less revenue. For \par fiscal consolidation to take hold on a sustained basis, on top of healthy \par economic growth, we expect the government to make significant efforts to \par restore its revenue-generating framework. Notably, the current administration \par has begun inroads into a medium-term fiscal framework and enacting a Fiscal \par Responsibility Act. \par  \par Malaysia's general government balance sheet also carries contingent risks from \par the country's public enterprises and guarantees. These include legacy \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 27 Jun 2022 05:47 ET \par Press Release: S&PGR Revises Malaysia Outlk To -2-\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par liabilities associated with state-linked investment firm 1MDB. We estimate the \par government's 1MDB-related liabilities at MYR17.5 billion at end 2022 and \par incorporate it into the general government's balance sheet. Come 2023, most of \par the 1MDB liabilities would be extinguished with one remaining MYR5 billion \par (0.3% of GDP) sukuk maturing in 2039. \par  \par The Malaysian government also identifies guarantees that it has provided for \par various entities that may be unable to service their debt. We have included \par such committed guarantees in our calculation of general government debt due to \par the high likelihood that they will crystallize on the general government's \par balance sheet. \par  \par These guarantees include but are not limited to those extended to \par government-linked firms Danainfra Nasional Bhd., Prasarana Malaysia, Malaysia \par Rail-link Sdn. Bhd., and Urusharta Jamaah Sdn. Bhd. We estimate the \par government's total direct liability to these firms at 12.8% of GDP. As such, \par our calculation of the general government's net debt is higher by an \par equivalent amount. \par  \par Committed guarantees excluding the 1MDB obligations have consistently risen in \par recent years, and we believe this will remain the case over the next three \par years as the government continues to support key infrastructure projects and \par associated firms. But as a share of GDP, these committed guarantees should be \par steady, in our view. \par  \par Malaysia's solid external position remains a rating strength. The country's \par current account achieved a strong surplus equivalent to 3.8% of GDP in 2021. \par We envisage a modest increase in the surplus this year to 4% with strong \par exports from E&E and the energy sector. We forecast the current account \par surplus will stabilize above 3.5% of GDP over the next three years as a \par gradual recovery in tourism and continued strong demand for Malaysian \par manufacturing exports will support the country's trade accounts. \par  \par Most of Malaysia's external liabilities are denominated in the domestic \par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , while external assets--both official \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves and \par other claims on nonresidents--are denominated in foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . \par  \par The Malaysian economy's external position is subject to some market \par volatility. This includes periods of amplified risk aversion, which could lead \par to nonresidents reducing their holdings of local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  government debt. \par Foreign ownership of Malaysian government securities typically accounts for \par 15%-25% of the total outstanding stock. We estimate Malaysia's ratio of gross \par external financing needs to current account receipts and usable reserves will \par be about 91.9% in 2022, and gradually fall to about 89.4% by 2025. \par  \par We classify \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Malaysia\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign-exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  regime as floating, with intermittent \par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market. The Malaysian ringgit depreciated \par substantially in 2014-2017 amid the reset in global commodity prices. However, \par pass-through of this depreciation into inflation was limited, burnishing the \par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's inflation management credentials. In our view, the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  \par has significant independence, which is unlikely to change in the medium-term. \par  \par The consumer price index (CPI) in Malaysia has been edging up recently in line \par with higher global prices and supply-side constraints. However, we expect CPI \par inflation to come in by the end of this year at a relatively modest 2.9%. This \par is because of the government's generous subsidies on fuel and food staples \par which cap prices. \par  \par Private sector leverage, as measured by total private sector debt (including \par corporate borrowings from the debt capital markets) to GDP, has been gradually \par building in Malaysia since 2008. Some borrowers may be vulnerable to \par prevailing economic difficulties and rising interest rates, especially in the \par sectors hit hardest by the pandemic. However, we expect credit risk for the \par Malaysian banking system to remain manageable, supported by banks' prudent \par lending and underwriting standards. \par  \par Our Banking Industry Country Risk Assessment for Malaysia is '4' ('1' being \par the strongest assessment and '10' the weakest). \par  \par Malaysia has a deep domestic bond market, which reduces reliance on \par foreign-\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  financing. Malaysia's relatively high share of nonresident \par holders of ringgit-denominated government bonds leaves the country's capital \par markets exposed to potential fund outflows; but the market has generally \par remained stable, with limited impact on the ringgit, since the onset of the \par pandemic. This reflects healthy external demand for Malaysian government debt. \par  \par The retention of Malaysian government securities in the FTSE Russell bond \par index has reduced uncertainty for the Malaysian bond market and the associated \par risks of a deterioration in Malaysia's external settings. We also believe the \par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is committed to a floating exchange rate, despite financial \par market volatility. \par  \par We estimate Malaysia's reserve coverage will be 4.7 months of current account \par payments at the end of this year. Though lower than historical levels, it \par remains sufficient, and we expect Malaysia's deep capital markets to support \par financial stability. Our forecast assumes that reserve coverage will continue \par to decline to 4.3 months through 2025. \par  \par KEY STATISTICS \par  \par RATINGS SCORE SNAPSHOT \par  \par Environmental, social, and governance (ESG) credit factors for this change in \par credit rating/outlook and/or CreditWatch status: \par      -- No ESG credit factor applies. \par  \par RELATED CRITERIA \par  \par      -- General Criteria: Environmental, Social, And Governance Principles In \par Credit Ratings, Oct. 10, 2021 \par      -- Criteria | Governments | Sovereigns: Sovereign Rating Methodology, \par Dec. 18, 2017 \par      -- General Criteria: Methodology For Linking Long-Term And Short-Term \par Ratings, April 7, 2017 \par      -- General Criteria: Methodology For Rating Sukuk, Jan. 19, 2015 \par      -- General Criteria: Principles Of Credit Ratings, Feb. 16, 2011 \par      -- General Criteria: Methodology: Criteria For Determining Transfer And \par Convertibility Assessments, May 18, 2009 \par  \par RELATED RESEARCH \par      -- Sovereign Risk Indicators, April 11, 2022. An interactive version is \par also available at  \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.spratings.com/sri"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.spratings.com/sri }}}\uc2 \f2  \par      -- Global Sovereign Rating Trends: First-Quarter 2022, April 13, 2022 \par      -- Sovereign Ratings Score Snapshot, April 7, 2022 \par      -- Sovereign Debt 2022: Asia-Pacific Central Governments To Borrow US$3.8 \par Trillion, April 6, 2022 \par      -- Sovereign Ratings History, April 5, 2022 \par      -- Credit FAQ: COVID And Conflict Strain Asia-Pacific Sovereigns, March \par 24, 2022 \par      -- Asia-Pacific Sovereign Rating Trends 2022, Jan. 28, 2022 \par      -- Default, Transition, and Recovery: 2020 Annual Sovereign Default And \par Rating Transition Study, April 13, 2021 \par  \par In accordance with our relevant policies and procedures, the Rating Committee \par was composed of analysts that are qualified to vote in the committee, with \par sufficient experience to convey the appropriate level of knowledge and \par understanding of the methodology applicable (see 'Related Criteria And \par Research'). At the onset of the committee, the chair confirmed that the \par information provided to the Rating Committee by the primary analyst had been \par distributed in a timely manner and was sufficient for Committee members to \par make an informed decision. \par  \par After the primary analyst gave opening remarks and explained the \par recommendation, the Committee discussed key rating factors and critical issues \par in accordance with the relevant criteria. Qualitative and quantitative risk \par factors were considered and discussed, looking at track-record and forecasts. \par  \par The committee's assessment of the key rating factors is reflected in the \par Ratings Score Snapshot above. \par  \par The chair ensured every voting member was given the opportunity to articulate \par his/her opinion. The chair or designee reviewed the draft report to ensure \par consistency with the Committee decision. The views and the decision of the \par rating committee are summarized in the above rationale and outlook. The \par weighting of all rating factors is described in the methodology used in this \par rating action (see 'Related Criteria And Research'). \par  \par Certain terms used in this report, particularly certain adjectives used to \par express our view on rating relevant factors, have specific meanings ascribed \par to them in our criteria, and should therefore be read in conjunction with such \par criteria. Please see Ratings Criteria at www.standardandpoors.com for further \par information. Complete ratings information is available to subscribers of \par RatingsDirect at www.capitaliq.com. All ratings affected by this rating action \par can be found on S&P Global Ratings' public website at \par www.standardandpoors.com. Use the Ratings search box located in the left \par column. \par  \par Primary Credit Analyst: YeeFarn Phua, Singapore +  65 6239 6341; \par                         yeefarn.phua@spglobal.com \par Secondary Contact: Andrew Wood, Singapore +  65 6239 6