\par\par \b \uc2 *S&PGR Affirms Malaysia Ratings; Outlook Negative\b0\par\par\uc2 4,065 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 Juni 2021\par 11:03\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2021, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 22 Jun 2021 05:03 ET  Press Release: S&PGR Affirms Malaysia Ratings; Outlook Negative\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par The following is a press release from S&P Global Ratings: \par  \par OVERVIEW \par      -- Malaysia faces continued pressure on its fiscal settings given its \par near-term economic outlook amid a worsened domestic COVID-19 situation and a \par fluid political backdrop. \par      -- The country's solid economic recovery prospects from 2022 onward \par should act as a tailwind for fiscal consolidation. \par      -- We affirmed our 'A-' long-term and 'A-2' short-term foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  \par sovereign credit ratings on Malaysia. We also affirmed our 'A' long-term and \par 'A-1' short-term local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  ratings. The outlook on the long-term rating \par remains negative. \par  \par RATING ACTION \par SINGAPORE (S&P Global Ratings) June 22, 2021--S&P Global Ratings today \par affirmed its 'A-' long-term and 'A-2' short-term sovereign credit ratings on \par Malaysia. We also affirmed our 'A' long-term and 'A-1' short-term local \par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  ratings on Malaysia. The outlook on the long-term ratings remains \par negative. \par  \par OUTLOOK \par The negative outlook reflects our expectation that Malaysia faces heightened \par risks to its fiscal and economic recovery prospects over the next 12-24 months \par related to the COVID-19 pandemic and domestic political uncertainty. \par  \par Downside scenario \par Our ratings on Malaysia could face downward pressure over the next 12-24 \par months if economic growth suffers a deeper or more prolonged downturn than we \par expect, or if we see a weaker commitment to fiscal consolidation. Both these \par situations could result in the faster accumulation of net general government \par debt. Change in net general government debt surpassing 4% on a sustained \par basis, net indebtedness surpassing 80% of GDP, or interest paid by the general \par government exceeding 15% of revenue would indicate such deterioration. \par  \par Downward ratings pressure could also build if political stability in Malaysia \par deteriorates such that policymaking becomes materially less predictable, or if \par the country's external position weakens such that the economy's annual gross \par external financing needs surpass current account receipts plus usable \par reserves. \par  \par Upside scenario \par We may revise the outlook to stable over the next 12-24 months if the \par Malaysian economy grows considerably faster than we forecast, or the policy \par environment becomes more conducive to credible fiscal consolidation. This \par would in turn produce a stronger fiscal performance than we expect, leading to \par a quicker stabilization of government finances. \par  \par RATIONALE \par Our affirmed ratings on Malaysia reflect the country's strong external \par position, monetary policy flexibility, and record of supporting sustainable \par economic growth. The country's elevated government debt stock and evolving \par fiscal policy settings temper these strengths. \par  \par The negative outlook reflects enduring pressures on Malaysia's fiscal and debt \par settings, which have been further undermined by a worsening domestic COVID-19 \par situation this year. Higher fiscal deficits in 2020 and this year, alongside a \par more muted near-term economic recovery, will keep the Malaysian government's \par net indebtedness close to 70% of GDP. Heightened political uncertainty \par compounds the challenges that the government will face in rapidly \par consolidating its finances over the next two to three years. \par  \par Institutional and economic profile: Near-term COVID-19 challenges and \par continued political risk are balanced against healthier medium-term recovery \par prospects \par      -- The serious state of Malaysia's COVID-19 situation and strict \par containment measures will limit the scale of its economic recovery this year. \par      -- Real GDP growth will accelerate to 6.3% in 2022, versus a 4.1% \par forecast this year, supported by vaccine-derived immunity and the gradual \par re-opening of the economy. \par      -- Support for the governing Perikatan Nasional (PN) coalition, which has \par been ruling under a state of emergency since January 2021, is difficult to \par gauge. Malaysia's policymaking is clouded by political uncertainty. \par  \par We have lowered our 2021 real GDP growth forecast for Malaysia to 4.1% from \par 6.2% to incorporate the considerable impact of the tightening of the \par government's Movement Control Order (MCO) since June 1. Though Malaysia's \par severe COVID-19 wave has stabilized over the past two weeks, the broader \par spread of the virus has likely undermined economic activity. \par  \par We expect the majority of the impact to economic activity to be felt in the \par second quarter of 2021. The recovery will likely be gradual in the third \par quarter owing to the cautious reopening that the government has indicated. \par Critically, some economic sectors are allowed to operate with strict safety \par procedures, subject to approval under a positive listing process. As such, \par activity will not be affected to the same extent as during the first MCO in \par the second quarter of 2020. \par  \par We expect Malaysia's economic recovery to gain pace as the government's \par inoculation drive progresses, and targets are met for a broader reopening of \par activities. Near-term targets include a decline in national daily positive \par cases to less than 4,000, improvement in the capacity utilization of key \par healthcare infrastructure, and 10% inoculation of the population. \par  \par The Malaysian economy is well-diversified and has a record of resilience \par following periods of adversity. The country's manufacturing sector is \par well-oriented to take advantage of a global surge in demand for electronics. \par Malaysia has a mature electrical and electronics sector that supplies \par components and finished products to major global players. We expect this \par sector to drive strong exports over the next one to two years, in particular. \par  \par The Malaysian economy is likely to grow at an above-trend rate in 2022 and \par 2023, before settling toward a more sustainable long-term rate of expansion in \par line with its historical performance. \par  \par We project Malaysia's GDP per capita to be about US$11,100 by end-2021, lower \par than most peers' in the same rating category but notably higher than that of \par most of its Southeast Asian neighbors except Singapore and Brunei. \par  \par Malaysia's PN coalition has been in power since March 2020, following the \par collapse of the Pakatan Harapan (PH) coalition. PN is anchored by \par representatives of the Bersatu party, as well as the Barisan Nasional (BN) \par coalition, including key constituent party United Malays National Organization \par (UMNO). This marked Malaysia's second change of government in less than two \par years, following a 61-year period of uninterrupted rule under BN up to 2018. \par  \par Malaysia's institutions have supported generally effective policymaking for a \par long time, and we believe their depth of capacity alleviates the risk of \par instability owing to ongoing political developments. Notably, the government \par proactively introduced stimulus measures aimed at supporting the economy as \par the pandemic worsened in 2020. However, Malaysia's COVID-19 situation has \par worsened considerably this year, and the government is facing pressure from \par various constituencies, including some within the PN coalition itself. \par  \par Fissures within the government, as well as the broader parliament, appear to \par be material, calling into question the longevity and strength of PN's mandate. \par  \par Further changes in government are increasingly likely once the current state \par of emergency, implemented in January of this year, is lifted. \par  \par BN leaders have indicated that the coalition will not work with the broader PN \par coalition, nor key constituent party Bersatu, when the next set of elections \par are held. \par  \par The high level of political uncertainty in Malaysia undermines clarity \par regarding the government's fiscal policy trajectory, and elevates the risk \par that fiscal consolidation targets will not be met over the coming years. \par  \par The Malaysian government has introduced numerous stimulus packages since \par February 2020. The latest "Permerkasa+" package announced prior to \par reinstatement of the heightened MCO measures is worth Malaysian ringgit (MYR) \par 40 billion. This takes the total value of stimulus measures, inclusive of PH's \par first package in February 2020, to MYR380 billion. Direct fiscal injections, \par budgeted at MYR65 billion in November 2020, account for only a fraction of \par this total, but we believe that this component could increase further \par depending on the severity of the pandemic, and concomitant economic \par difficulties. \par  \par Flexibility and performance profile: Malaysia faces a steepening road to \par fiscal consolidation \par      -- Malaysia's fiscal deficit will remain elevated in 2021 owing to a \par weaker economic outlook. \par      -- A higher shortfall this year will make consolidation over the next two \par to three years more challenging as the government balances fiscal restraint \par against the need to support the nascent economic recovery. \par      -- Malaysia's fiscal consolidation also faces policy risks related to the \par country's uncertain political landscape. \par  \par We forecast the net indebtedness of Malaysia's general government will rise by \par 6.6% of GDP in 2021, versus a 6.3% increase in 2020, on slightly higher \par general and central government deficits, and continued accumulation of \par committed guarantees. \par  \par The much wider scale of Malaysia's COVID-19 situation this year will play a \par key role in the government's fiscal performance. The containment measures will \par hit revenue generation, and expenditure needs are likely to be higher than \par programmed in areas such as healthcare and direct fiscal transfers. As a \par result, we forecast net general government debt will average 70% of GDP from \par 2021-2024, with the annual change averaging 4.8%. The general government's \par fiscal deficit this year is likely to be its highest since the global \par financial crisis in 2009. \par  \par The Malaysian government derives slightly less than 20% of its total revenue \par from oil-related sources. The single largest component of this revenue is the \par dividend paid by state oil company \f28 \uc2 \f2 Petroliam Nasional Bhd\f28 \uc2 \f2  . (\f28 \uc2 \f2 Petronas\f28 \uc2 \f2 ). The \par company paid a MYR34 billion dividend in 2020. This reduced the downward \par effect on government revenue from much lower oil prices in 2020. \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 22 Jun 2021 05:03 ET \par Press Release: S&PGR Affirms Malaysia Ratings; -2-\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par However, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Petronas\f28 \uc2 \f2 ' dividend to the government for 2021 is likely to be much \par lower. It is currently budgeted at MYR18 billion, partially reflecting adverse \par business conditions in the previous year. Despite higher prevailing oil \par prices, this will limit upside prospects for Malaysia's revenue growth in \par 2021. \par  \par The PH government's decision to zero-rate Malaysia's goods and services tax \par (GST) in June 2018 weakened the government's revenue generation capacity; GST \par previously contributed nearly 20% to government revenue. We believe the GST \par helped the government to maintain sound fiscal metrics following the 2014-2015 \par global collapse in commodity prices, and considerably reduced the government's \par dependence on commodity revenues. The PH government replaced the GST with a \par sales and services tax, a narrower program that mobilizes less revenue. Amid \par heightened uncertainty around how long the current government will last, as \par well as the pandemic, it remains to be seen what efforts the government can \par make to restore its revenue-generating framework. \par  \par To cap its fiscal deficit at about 6% of GDP this year, the government will \par need to achieve savings through the rationalization of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operational\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and capital \par expenditure. It may also require additional support from some \par government-related entities, while higher commodity prices should help to \par alleviate the impact on government revenues stemming from strict COVID-19 \par containment measures and a slower economic recovery in the second quarter of \par 2021. \par  \par The strong financial performance of companies such as \f28 \uc2 \f2 Petronas\f28 \uc2 \f2  and \f28 \uc2 \f2 Khazanah \par Nasional Bhd\f28 \uc2 \f2  . lends some additional flexibility to the government's fiscal \par profile, though aggregate dividends are likely to be lower than in 2020. \par  \par Malaysia's general government balance sheet also carries contingent risks from \par the country's public enterprises and guarantees. These include legacy \par liabilities associated with state-linked investment firm 1MDB. We estimate the \par government's 1MDB-related liabilities at MYR31 billion and incorporate it into \par the general government's balance sheet. \par  \par The Malaysian government also identifies guarantees that it has provided for \par various entities that may be unable to service their debt. We have included \par such committed guarantees in our calculation of general government debt due to \par the high likelihood that they will crystallize on the general government's \par balance sheet. \par  \par These guarantees include but are not limited to those extended to \par government-linked firms Danainfra Nasional Bhd., Prasarana Malaysia, Malaysia \par Rail-link Sdn. Bhd., and SRC International Sdn. Bhd. We estimate the \par government's total direct liability to these firms on a net basis at more than \par 10% of GDP. As such, our calculation of the general government's net debt is \par higher by an equivalent amount. \par  \par Committed guarantees excluding the 1MDB obligations have consistently risen in \par recent years, and we believe this will remain the case over the next three \par years as the government continues to support key infrastructure projects and \par associated firms. \par  \par Malaysia's solid external position remains a rating strength. The country's \par current account achieved a solid surplus equivalent to 4.4% of GDP in 2020. We \par envisage a moderate decline in the surplus this year as imports, income \par accounts, and transfers begin to normalize. We forecast the current account \par surplus will stabilize modestly above 3% of GDP over the next three years as a \par gradual recovery in tourism and continued strong demand for Malaysian \par manufacturing exports will support the country's trade accounts. \par  \par Most of Malaysia's external liabilities are denominated in the domestic \par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , while external assets--both official \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves and \par other claims on nonresidents--are denominated in foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . \par  \par The Malaysian economy's external liquidity requirements, on a gross basis, are \par considerable; its external liquidity is therefore subject to market \par volatility. This includes periods of amplified risk aversion, which could lead \par to nonresidents reducing their holdings of local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  government debt. \par Foreign ownership of Malaysian government securities typically account for \par 15%-25% of the total outstanding stock. We estimate Malaysia's ratio of gross \par external financing needs to current account receipts and usable reserves will \par be about 93.9% in 2021, and gradually fall to about 92.6% by 2024. \par  \par We classify \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Malaysia\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign-exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  regime as floating, with intermittent \par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market. The Malaysian ringgit depreciated \par substantially in 2014-2017 amid the reset in global commodity prices. However, \par pass-through of this depreciation into inflation was limited, burnishing the \par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's inflation management credentials. \par  \par The consumer price index (CPI) in Malaysia has been edging up recently in line \par with higher global prices and supply-side constraints. We expect CPI inflation \par to moderate to 3.3% by the end of this year. In our view, the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has \par significant independence, which is unlikely to change under the current \par government. \par  \par Although high household debt remains a risk for the banking system and the \par broader economy, it has been gradually declining relative to GDP. We believe \par risks are contained by banks' strong capitalization and a generally sound \par regulatory framework. Household financial assets are also ample. \par  \par Private sector leverage, as measured by total private sector debt (including \par corporate borrowings from the debt capital markets) to GDP, has been gradually \par building in Malaysia since 2008. Some borrowers may be vulnerable to \par prevailing economic difficulties, especially in the sectors hit hardest by \par pandemic restrictions. However, we expect credit risk for the Malaysian \par banking system to remain manageable, supported by banks' prudent lending and \par underwriting standards. \par  \par Banks' exposure to segments that are most at risk amid the weakened economy is \par manageable, in our view. These segments include lower-income households, \par micro, small, and midsize enterprises, and some corporate sectors that are \par most vulnerable to COVID-19, such as commercial property developers and \par hospitality. \par  \par Our Banking Industry Country Risk Assessment for Malaysia is '4' ('1' being \par the strongest assessment and '10' the weakest). \par  \par Malaysia has a deep domestic bond market, which reduces reliance on \par foreign-\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  financing. Although Malaysia's relatively high share of \par nonresident holders of ringgit-denominated government bonds leaves the \par country's capital markets exposed to potential fund outflows, the market has \par generally remained stable, with limited impact on the ringgit, since the onset \par of the pandemic. This reflects healthy external demand for Malaysian \par government debt. \par  \par The retention of Malaysian government securities in the FTSE Russell bond \par index has reduced uncertainty for the Malaysian bond market and the associated \par risks of a deterioration in Malaysia's external settings. We also believe the \par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is committed to a floating exchange rate, despite financial \par market volatility. \par  \par The \f28 \uc2 \f2 Malaysian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2   has in recent years typically maintained a sizable \par amount of reserves sold forward; we subtract this quantum under our assessment \par of the bank's useable reserves, which excludes items not readily available for \par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . \par  \par We estimate Malaysia's reserve coverage will be 4.7 months of current account \par payments at the end of this year. Although this level remains sufficient, it \par is near a multi-year low. While Malaysia's deep capital markets should support \par financial stability, our forecast assumes that reserve coverage will continue \par to decline to nearly 4.4 months through 2024. \par  \par KEY STATISTICS \par  \par RELATED CRITERIA \par  \par      -- Criteria | Governments | Sovereigns: Sovereign Rating Methodology, \par Dec. 18, 2017 \par      -- General Criteria: Methodology For Linking Long-Term And Short-Term \par Ratings, April 7, 2017 \par      -- General Criteria: Principles Of Credit Ratings, Feb. 16, 2011 \par      -- General Criteria: Methodology: Criteria For Determining Transfer And \par Convertibility Assessments, May 18, 2009 \par  \par RELATED RESEARCH \par      -- \par 48424964&From=SNP_CRS", June 9, 2021 \par      -- \par 48355612&From=SNP_CRS", June 2, 2021 \par      -- \par 47843618&From=SNP_CRS", April 12, 2021 \par      -- \par 47844104&From=SNP_CRS", April 12, 2021. An interactive version is also \par available at  \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.spratings.com/sri"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.spratings.com/sri }}}\uc2 \f2  \par      -- \par 47821055&From=SNP_CRS", April 9, 2021 \par      -- \par 47477944&From=SNP_CRS", March 2, 2021 \par      -- \par 47208134&From=SNP_CRS", Feb. 2, 2021 \par      -- \par 47143508&From=SNP_CRS", Jan. 28, 2021 \par  \par In accordance with our 