\par\par \b \uc2 *S&PGR Revises Malaysia Outlook To Neg; Ratings Affirmed\b0\par\par\uc2 4,097 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 Juni 2020\par 12:13\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2020, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 26 Jun 2020 06:13 ET  Press Release: S&PGR Revises Malaysia Outlook To Neg; Ratings Affirmed\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par The following is a press release from S&P Global Ratings: \par  \par OVERVIEW \par      -- The economic impact of the COVID-19 pandemic, combined with depressed \par oil prices and fiscal stimulus, will weaken the Malaysian government's debt \par position for the next few years. \par      -- We are revising our outlook on Malaysia to negative to reflect \par additional downside risk to the government's fiscal metrics, owing to the weak \par global economic climate and heightened policy uncertainty. \par      -- We are affirming our 'A-' long-term and 'A-2' short-term foreign \par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  sovereign credit ratings on Malaysia. We are also affirming our 'A' \par long-term and 'A-1' short-term local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  ratings. \par      -- Our ratings on Malaysia balance the country's strong external \par position, monetary policy flexibility, and track record of supporting \par sustainable economic growth, against its elevated government debt stock, and \par evolving fiscal policy settings. \par  \par RATING ACTION \par On June 26, 2020, S&P Global Ratings revised its outlook on Malaysia's \par long-term ratings to negative from stable. We affirmed our 'A-' long-term and \par 'A-2' short-term foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  sovereign credit ratings, as well as our 'A' \par long-term and 'A-1' short-term local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  ratings on Malaysia. \par  \par OUTLOOK \par The negative outlook reflects our expectation that Malaysia faces heightened \par risks of a further deterioration in its fiscal settings, related to the \par COVID-19 pandemic, over the next 24 months. \par  \par Downside scenario \par Our ratings could face downward pressure if economic growth suffers a deeper \par or more prolonged downturn than we currently expect, or if we see a weaker \par commitment to fiscal consolidation, either of which could result in the faster \par accumulation of net general government debt. Downward ratings pressure would \par arise if Malaysia's annual change in net general government debt surpasses 4% \par on a sustained basis, or interest paid by the general government exceeds 15% \par of revenue. Downward ratings pressure could also build if political stability \par deteriorates such that policymaking becomes materially less predictable. \par  \par Upside scenario \par We may revise the outlook to stable over the next 24 months if the economy \par grows considerably faster than we forecast and, in turn, produces a stronger \par fiscal performance than we expect, leading to a quicker improvement in \par government finances. \par  \par RATIONALE \par We revised the outlook to negative to reflect pressures on Malaysia's fiscal \par and debt settings, which have been exacerbated by the COVID-19 pandemic. \par Higher fiscal deficits this year and next, alongside a forecast contraction in \par real GDP this year, will likely lead to the Malaysian government's net \par indebtedness surpassing 60% of GDP. Against an unforgiving global \par macroeconomic backdrop, heightened domestic political uncertainty is adding \par downside risks to the government's fiscal position. We weigh these risks \par against Malaysia's strong external position, sound long-term growth prospects, \par and well-established monetary policy credibility. \par  \par Institutional and economic profile: The economy faces a steep downturn in \par 2020, and will likely stabilize from 2021 onward \par      -- We forecast a 2% contraction in the Malaysian economy in 2020, \par reflecting the deep effects of the pandemic. \par      -- The economy will likely begin to stabilize thereafter, and we forecast \par a strong recovery to 7.5% growth in 2021. \par      -- Malaysia faces heightened political uncertainty owing to marginal \par support for the ruling Perikatan Nasional coalition in parliament. \par  \par We forecast real GDP growth contracting by 2% in 2020 as COVID-19 hits \par Malaysia's trade flows, investment, and private consumption. Strict domestic \par containment measures against the virus were introduced in March, and have been \par gradually lifted since May. Although the measures appear to have been \par successful in preventing a much more serious local epidemic, they will have \par significantly curtailed economic activity in second-quarter 2020, and it will \par take some time for the economy to recover from the lockdown. Like many \par economies around the world, this will be Malaysia's worst economic performance \par in many years--underscoring the deep effects of the global economic and health \par crisis. There could be additional downside risk to this forecast should the \par pandemic last longer than expected, or if the global economy does not \par meaningfully recovery in 2021. \par  \par Nevertheless, the Malaysian economy is well-diversified and has a long track \par record of resilience following periods of adversity. In 2021, we expect a \par strong rebound in growth largely on base effects. Over the medium term, we \par expect the Malaysian economy to return to its trend growth rate of 4%-5% \par annually, with a slightly stronger performance in 2022 as lost output is \par further recovered. \par  \par We project Malaysia's GDP per capita to be about US$10,400 by end-2020, lower \par than most peers' in the same rating category but notably higher than most of \par its Southeast Asian peers except Singapore and Brunei. We forecast Malaysia's \par real GDP per capita growth to average approximately 2.8% per year over \par 2020-2023. \par  \par Malaysia's Perikatan Nasional (PN) coalition government came to power in early \par March, following the collapse of the Pakatan Harapan coalition. PN is anchored \par by representatives of the Barisan Nasional (BN) coalition, including key \par constituent party UMNO, as well as the Malaysian United Indigenous Party \par (PPBM), which crossed the aisle from the PH government to form PN. This marks \par Malaysia's second change of government in two years, following a 61-year \par period of uninterrupted rule under BN up to 2018. \par  \par Malaysia's institutions have supported generally effective policymaking for a \par long time and we believe their depth of capacity will help to alleviate the \par risk of instability owing to ongoing political developments. Notably, the \par government proactively introduced stimulus measures aimed at supporting the \par economy as the COVID-19 pandemic worsened in recent months. \par  \par Nevertheless, marginal support for PN in parliament has led to challenges by \par prominent members of the PH opposition, including former Prime Minister \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Mahathir Mohamed\f28 \uc2 \f2 , as well as opposition leader \f28 \uc2 \f2 Anwar Ibrahim\f28 \uc2 \f2  . Uncertainty \par regarding the level of support for either coalition could result in further \par changes in government, or snap polls, thereby undermining the predictability \par of policymaking at a crucial juncture, in our view. In particular, this \par undermines clarity regarding the government's fiscal policy trajectory, and \par elevates the risk that fiscal consolidation targets are not met over the \par coming years. \par  \par To try and mitigate the severe economic impact of the pandemic the PN \par government has expanded the stimulus measures first introduced by the PH \par government in February. The total value of stimulus measures, inclusive of \par PH's first package in February, is now about Malaysian ringgit (MYR) 295 \par billion (20.3% of GDP). Only MYR45 billion (3.0% of GDP) of the package is \par expected to be borne directly by the government, largely comprising measures \par that aim to support employment and protect the most vulnerable segments of \par society. Other measures not borne by the government include a roughly MYR100 \par billion, six-month moratorium on repayments of loans to banks by households \par and businesses, overseen by \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank Negara Malaysia\f28 \uc2 \f2   (\f28 \uc2 \f2 BNM\f28 \uc2 \f2 ), as well as a scheme to \par provide as much as MYR50 billion in government guarantees on lending to firms \par facing working capital difficulties. The latter scheme will be administered \par through government-linked company Danajamin Nasional Bhd. \par  \par Flexibility and performance profile: The higher debt stock and deficits \par reflect a weaker fiscal profile \par      -- Malaysia's fiscal deficit will rise to 6% of GDP this year, owing to \par fiscal stimulus measures and weaker revenue generation. \par      -- Net general government indebtedness will surpass 60% of GDP on a \par sustained basis, as a result of higher fiscal deficits and lower nominal GDP \par growth. \par      -- Malaysia's fiscal consolidation trajectory could be affected by \par political uncertainty. \par  \par We have revised our general government deficit forecast up to 6.0% of GDP in \par 2020, versus our previous expectation of 4.1%. Greater expenditure on stimulus \par measures, in combination with weaker revenue generation against the backdrop \par of a shrinking economy, are the key contributors to the higher deficit. As a \par result of these evolving dynamics, we now forecast net general government debt \par will average 62.8% from 2020-2023, with the annual change in net general \par government debt averaging 3.9%. \par  \par The Malaysian government derives slightly less than 20% of its total revenue \par from oil-related sources. The single largest component of this revenue is the \par dividend paid to the government by state oil company \f28 \uc2 \f2 Petroliam Nasional Bhd\f28 \uc2 \f2  . \par (\f28 \uc2 \f2 PETRONAS\f28 \uc2 \f2 ), which we expect to be maintained at MYR24 billion, as allocated in \par the government's original 2020 budget. This dampens the potential downward \par effect on revenue from much lower oil prices. However, future budgets may see \par less support from \f28 \uc2 \f2 Petronas\f28 \uc2 \f2  in the form of its annual dividend transfer, if oil \par prices remain lower for longer. \par  \par The former PH government's decision to zero-rate Malaysia's goods and services \par tax (GST) in June 2018 has weakened revenue generation capacity; the GST was \par previously responsible for nearly 20% of government revenue. We believe the \par GST was instrumental in the government's ability to maintain sound fiscal \par metrics following the 2014-2015 global collapse in commodity prices, and that \par it considerably reduced the government's dependence on commodity revenues. The \par PH government replaced the GST with a sales and services tax, a narrower \par program that mobilizes significantly less revenue. Amid heightened uncertainty \par around how long the current government will last, as well as the pandemic, it \par remains to be seen what efforts the government can make to restore its \par revenue-generating framework over time, in our view. \par  \par In order to cap its fiscal deficit at 6% of GDP this year, the PN government \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 26 Jun 2020 06:13 ET \par Press Release: S&PGR Revises Malaysia Outlook To -2-\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par will need to achieve savings through the rationalization of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operational\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and \par capital expenditure items, as well as through additional support from some \par GLCs, including \f28 \uc2 \f2 Petronas\f28 \uc2 \f2 , KWAP, and \f28 \uc2 \f2 Khazanah\f28 \uc2 \f2 . The strong financial performance \par of these firms lends some additional flexibility to the government's fiscal \par profile. Still, the government's targeted fiscal deficit will likely be its \par highest since the global financial crisis in 2009. \par  \par In view of these developing dynamics, we forecast that the general \par government's net debt stock will rise sustainably above 60% of GDP. We expect \par net debt to GDP to average 63.4% through 2023, and to fall to 61.1% by the end \par of our forecast period, from a high of 66.1% this year. The rise in the \par government's debt stock is also likely to require Malaysia to revise its \par long-standing fiscal rule of limiting central government debt stock to less \par than 55% of GDP. We expect officials to raise this ceiling in the near future, \par as government \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  could otherwise be affected if the debt stock were to \par breach that level. \par  \par Malaysia's general government fiscal position also carries contingent risks \par from its public enterprises and financial sector. These include guarantees on \par debts and letters of support (including the US$3 billion letter of support for \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 1Malaysia Development Bhd\f28 \uc2 \f2  . [1MDB], which we regard as a direct financial \par obligation of the government). \par  \par The former PH government announced that it was committed to provide support \par for government guarantees for various entities that may be unable to service \par their debts. We have included such committed guarantees in our calculation of \par general government debt, due to the high likelihood that they will crystallize \par on the general government's balance sheet. \par  \par These guarantees include but are not limited to those extended to \par government-linked firms Danainfra, Prasarana, Malaysia Rail-link Sdn. Bhd. \par (MRL), SRC International Sdn. Bhd., and 1MDB. We estimate the government's \par total direct liability to these firms on a net basis at more than 10% of GDP. \par As such, our calculation of the general government's net debt is higher by an \par equivalent amount. We note that some of these state-linked vehicles carry \par significant assets which, if liquidated, will likely be used to repay maturing \par debt. We expect committed government guarantees to gradually decline over the \par next three-to-four years assuming that the government does not undertake \par significant new off-budget capital expenditure projects. \par  \par Malaysia's solid external position remains a ratings strength. Its current \par account remains in modest surplus at about 3.4% of GDP in 2019. We envisage a \par moderate decline in the current account surplus this year, to 1.9% of GDP, \par largely on a deterioration in Malaysia's terms of trade. However, we expect \par the surplus to stabilize above 2% of GDP from 2021 onward as global trade \par conditions normalize. \par  \par Most of Malaysia's external liabilities are denominated in the domestic \par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , while external assets, both official \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign-exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves and \par other claims on nonresidents, are denominated in foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . \par  \par The Malaysian economy's external liquidity requirements, on a gross basis, are \par considerable; its external liquidity is therefore subject to some degree of \par market volatility. This includes acute bouts of risk aversion, which could \par lead to nonresidents reducing their holdings of local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  government \par debt. Foreign ownership of Malaysian government securities typically account \par for 15%-25% of the total outstanding stock. We estimate Malaysia's ratio of \par gross external financing needs to current account receipts and usable reserves \par at about 96.3% in 2020 although we forecast this will gradually fall to about \par 93% by 2023. \par  \par We classify \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Malaysia\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign-exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  regime as floating, with intermittent \par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market. The Malaysian ringgit depreciated \par substantially in 2014-2017 amid the reset in global commodity prices; \par pass-through of this depreciation into inflation was limited, however, \par burnishing the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's inflation management credentials. More recently, \par consumer price inflation has been very low partly because of the zero-rating \par of GST in 2018. In our view, \f28 \uc2 \f2 BNM\f28 \uc2 \f2  has significant independence and we do not \par expect this to change under the current government. \par  \par Malaysia has a deep domestic bond market, which reduces its reliance on \par foreign-\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  financing--although the development of this market has led to \par somewhat greater volatility in nonresident capital flows. \f28 \uc2 \f2 BNM\f28 \uc2 \f2  has made some \par policy adjustments over recent years. Most notably, these include the stricter \par enforcement of a ban on offshore forward trading in the ringgit and a decrease \par in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign-exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  amounts exporters are allowed to hold. These policies may \par have contributed to a sell-off in nonresident holdings of ringgit-denominated \par government securities, particularly in early 2017. The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  actively \par supported dollar liquidity conditions in the local market during this period, \par however. This reflects the sufficiency of its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves as \par well as the efficacy of its policy levers. \par  \par Although high household debt levels remain a risk for the banking system and \par the broader economy, relative to GDP these levels are gradually declining. We \par believe risks are contained by strong capitalization and a generally sound \par regulatory framework. Household financial assets are also ample. \par  \par Private sector leverage, as measured by total private sector debt (including \par corporate borrowings from the debt capital markets) to GDP, has been gradually \par building since 2008 and could exacerbate the effects of the downturn. Overall, \par however, we expect the credit risk of Malaysian banks to remain manageable, \par supported by prudent lending and underwriting standards. Banks' exposure to \par the lending segments most at risk to the weakened economy--including lower \par income households and commercial property developers--is manageable in our \par view. Our base case is for an incremental increase in credit losses and \par impaired loans owing to the economic downturn. Our Banking Industry Country \par Risk Assessment for Malaysia is '4' ('1' being the strongest assessment and \par '10' the weakest). \par  \par Malaysia's relatively high share of nonresident holders of ringgit-denominated \par government bonds leaves the country's capital markets exposed to the potential \par for a sudden fund outflow. Nonresident holders of ringgit-denominated \par government bonds have previously pared their holdings of these bonds over \par short periods, but the market has generally remained stable with limited \par impact on the ringgit. This position carries additional vulnerability to the \par outstanding decision by FTSE Russell regarding the potential exclusion of \par Malaysian government securities from its bond index. We do not anticipate a \par material deterioration in Malaysia's external settings, either way the final \par decision goes. We also believe the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is committed to a floating \par exchange rate, despite financial market volatility. \par  \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 BNM\f28 \uc2 \f2  in recent years has typically maintained a sizable amount of reserves sold \par forward; we subtract this quantum under our assessment of the bank's useable \par reserves, which excludes items not readily available for \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . \par  \par Malaysia's reserve coverage stood at an estimated 4.6 months of current \par account payments at the end of 2019. Although this level remains sufficient, \par it is near a multi-year low. While Malaysia's deep capital markets should \par provide support for financial stability, our forecast assumes that reserve \par coverage will continue to decline through 2023 to nearly 4.0 months. \par  \par Environmental, social, and governance (ESG) credit factors for this credit \par rating change: \par      -- Governance \par      -- Health and Safety \par  \par KEY STATISTICS \par  \par RELATED CRITERIA \par      -- Criteria | Governments | Sovereigns: Sovereign Rating Methodology, \par Dec. 18, 2017 \par      -- General Criteria: Methodology For Linking Long-Term And Short-Term \par Ratings, April 7, 2017 \par      -- General Criteria: Use Of CreditWatch And Outlooks, Sept. 14, 2009 \par      -- General Criteria: Methodology: Criteria For Determining Transfer And \par Convertibility Assessments, May 18, 2009 \par  \par RELATED RESEARCH \par      -- Sovereign Ratings History, June 9, 2020 \par      -- Sovereign Ratings List, June 9, 2020 \par      -- Sovereign Ratings Score Snapshot, June 3, 2020 \par      -- 2019 Annual Sovereign Default And Rating Transition Study, May 19, 2020 \par      -- Sovereign Risk Indicators, April 25, 2020. A free interactive version \par is available at " \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.spratings.com/sri"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.spratings.com/sri }}}\uc2 \f2 " \par      -- Sovereign Ratings And The Effects Of The COVID-19 Pandemic, April 17, \par 2020 \par  \par In accordance with our relevant policies and procedures, the Rating Committee \par was composed of analysts that are qualified to vote in the committee, with \par sufficient experience to convey the appropriate level of knowledge and \par understanding of the methodology applicable (see 'Related Criteria And \par Research'). At the onset of the committee, the chair confirmed that the \par information provided to the Rating Committee by the primary analyst had been \par distributed in a timely manner and was sufficient for Committee members to \par make an informed decision. \par  \par After the primary analyst gave opening remarks and explained the \par recommendation, the Committee discussed key rating factors and critical issues \par in accordance with the relevant criteria. Qualitative and quantitative risk \par factors were considered and discussed, looking at track-record and forecasts. \par  \par The committee's assessment of the key rating factors is reflected in the \par Ratings Score Snapshot above. \par  \par The chair ensured every voting member was given the opportunity to articulate \par his/her opinion. The chair or designee reviewed the draft report to ensure \par consistency with the Committee decision. The views and the decision of the \par rating committee are summarized in the above rationale and outlook. The \par weighting of all rating factors is described in the methodology used in this \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 26 Jun 2020 06:13 ET \par Press Release: S&PGR Revises Malaysia Outlook To -3-\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par rating action (see 'Related Criteria And Research'). \par  \par Certain terms used in this report, particularly certain adjectives used to \par express our view on rating relevant factors, have specific meanings ascribed \par to them in our criteria, and should therefore be read in conjunction with such \par criteria. Please see Ratings Criteria at \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.standardandpoors.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 www.standardandpoors.com}}}\uc2 \f2  for further \par information. Complete ratings information is available to subscribers of \par RatingsDirect at \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.capitaliq.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 www.capitaliq.com}}}\uc2 \f2 . All ratings affected by this rating action \par can be found on S&P Global Ratings' public website at \par \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.standardandpoors.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 www.standardandpoors.com}}}\uc2 \f2 . Use the Ratings search box located in the left \par column. \par  \par Primary Credit Analyst: Andrew Wood, Singapore +  65 6239 6315; \par                         andrew.wood@spglobal.com \par Secondary Contact: YeeFarn Phua, Singapore (65) 6239-6341; \par                    yeefarn.phua@spglobal.com \par  \par  \par The article with statistical information is available in full \par at \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.standardandpoors.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 www.standardandpoors.com}}}\uc2 \f2 . Registration is free. To access the full article, \par select "Ratings Actions" in the left navigation under Ratings Resources, \par then choose the Press Releases tab. \par  \par  \par  \par No content (including ratings, credit-related analyses and data, valuations, \par model, software, or other application or output therefrom) or any part thereof \par (Content) may be modified, reverse engineered, reproduced, or distributed in \par any form by any means, or stored in a database or retrieval system, without \par the prior written permission of Standard & Poor