\par\par \b \uc2 *\uc2 Fitch\uc2  Affirms Malaysia at 'A-'; Outlook Stable\b0\par\par\uc2 2,801 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 August 2018\par 11:12\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2018, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 14 Aug 2018 05:12 ET  Press Release: \uc2 Fitch\uc2  Affirms Malaysia at 'A-'; Outlook Stable\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par The following is a press release from \f28 \uc2 \f2 Fitch Ratings\f28 \uc2 \f2  : \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 Fitch Ratings\uc2 -Hong Kong-14 August 2018: \uc2 Fitch Ratings\uc2  has affirmed Malaysia's Long-Term Foreign-\b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  Issuer Default Rating (IDR) at 'A-' with a Stable Outlook.\par \par \uc2 A full list of rating actions is at the end of this rating action commentary.\par \par \uc2 KEY RATING DRIVERS\par \par \uc2 - Malaysia's Long-Term IDRs are supported by solid economic growth and a net external creditor position built up from a record of current-account surpluses. These strengths are offset by elevated government and private sector debt, and low per capita income and \uc2 World Bank\uc2  governance scores relative to rating peers. The affirmation not only takes into consideration measures such as the rollback of the goods and services tax (GST), but also the stated intention to reduce fiscal deficits and improve governance. The agency has raised its estimate of central government debt at end-2017 to around 65% of GDP, from 50.8%, following the government's recognition that it will need to service a large share of explicitly guaranteed debt. This estimate may be further revised as more details become available.\par \par \uc2 The Pakatan Harapan coalition, which won the May 2018 election, has moved ahead on many of its key election promises, notably repealing the GST. It also plans to continue fuel subsidies in place since early 2018, although they will be made more targeted. \uc2 Fitch\uc2  views these measures as negative for the credit profile. However, the government aims to implement offsetting fiscal measures and has indicated its intention to contain the central government deficit. There are risks to achieving this target, such as lower-than-Fitch-expected growth - which could limit room for expenditure cutbacks - as well as delays in implementing planned revenue measures.\par \par \uc2 The authorities expect to meet the original budget deficit target of 2.8% of GDP for 2018 through offsetting measures, including a review of large infrastructure projects and the reintroduction of the sales and services tax with full-year revenue of 1.6% of GDP that had been replaced by the GST (full-year revenue of 3% of GDP) under the previous government. The authorities also expect additional oil revenue of 0.4% of GDP due to higher global oil prices and dividends from government-linked enterprises providing an additional 0.3% of GDP in revenue. On the expenditure side, the authorities plan to cut \b \highlight22\uc2 operational\b0 \highlight\uc2  and development spending by MYR10 billion (0.7% of GDP) in 2018.\par \par \uc2 We expect the deficit to continue falling to around 2.5% of GDP by 2020 under our baseline assumptions through a combination of subsidy rationalisation, further capital spending cuts, new revenue measures and better tax compliance. Central government debt is likely to decline to around 59% of GDP by 2020, although the decline could be more rapid if the government chooses to sell off public assets and use the proceeds for debt reduction. More details on some of these measures could be announced in the budget, which is due on 2 November. However, there are also upside risks to debt as a part of contingent liabilities related to public-private partnerships of 14.9% of GDP may migrate to the sovereign balance sheet.\par \par \uc2 Malaysia's structural indicators, such as GDP per capita, standards of human development and governance remain below the 'A' category median. One of Pakatan Harapan coalition's notable campaign promises was a royal commission inquiry into recent corruption scandals, such as that surrounding the state investment fund, \uc2 1Malaysia Development Bhd\uc2  . The investigation could help raise governance standards over time, although improvements may take time to eventuate. Malaysia ranked in the 61st percentile on the \uc2 World Bank\uc2  governance indicator, compared with the 'A' category historic median's 75th percentile.\par \par \uc2 We expect GDP growth to slow to 5.2% in 2018, 4.8% in 2019 and 4.6% in 2020, from 5.9% in 2017, as the government seeks to constrain recurrent spending in line with its narrower revenue base. In addition, public capital spending is being held back by a review of large infrastructure projects and exports are likely to moderate from slowing external demand. However, Malaysia's average GDP growth for the five years to 2018 will remain above peer medians. Downside risks to our growth projections could materialise from accelerated spending cuts, disruption to capital projects or slowing investment in the event of prolonged policy and political uncertainty.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Malaysia\b0 \highlight\uc2  (\uc2 BNM\uc2 ) to smooth \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  volatility in the midst of large capital outflows between April and June 2018 contributed to a decline in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves of about USD5 billion, to USD105 billion, at end June-2018. We expect \uc2 BNM\uc2  to limit its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and allow sufficient \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  flexibility to preserve \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves at a reasonably healthy level of around five months of current external payments. Although short-term external debt is high, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves continue to cover over 100% of this debt.\par \par \uc2 We expect Malaysia's current account surplus to remain between 3% - 4% of GDP between 2018-2020, supported by higher oil-related exports and slightly slower import growth, which should counter our forecast moderation in exports of electronics. Imports are likely to slow from the new government's review of major infrastructure projects. However, given Malaysia's high degree of trade openness, it remains vulnerable to negative external developments, such as rising trade protectionism. The country is also vulnerable to shifts in external investor sentiment due to high levels of short-term external debt and foreign holdings of government debt, especially against the backdrop of rising US interest rates. Foreign holdings of treasury bills, Malaysian government securities and government investment issues were around 28% of total central government debt in 1Q18.\par \par \uc2 Banking soundness indicators remain steady, with a system-wide common equity Tier 1 ratio of 12.7% and liquidity coverage ratio of 140% continuing to indicate adequate capital and liquidity buffers. Pockets of risk remain within certain over-supplied property sub-segments, highly leveraged lower-income households and high corporate debt. However, household credit growth has eased over the last few years, and we expect the banking sector to be resilient in case of a moderate correction in the property market.\par \par \uc2 SOVEREIGN RATING MODEL (SRM) and QUALITATIVE OVERLAY (QO)\par \par \uc2 - \uc2 Fitch\uc2 's proprietary SRM assigns Malaysia a score equivalent to a rating of 'BBB+' on the Long-Term Foreign-\b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  IDR scale.\par \par \uc2 In accordance with its rating criteria, \uc2 Fitch\uc2 's sovereign rating committee decided not to adopt the score indicated by the SRM as the starting point for its analysis, because the SRM output has migrated from 'A-' to 'BBB+', but in our view this is potentially a temporary deterioration.\par \par \uc2 Fitch\uc2 's SRM is the agency's proprietary multiple regression rating model that employs 18 variables based on three-year centred averages, including one year of forecasts, to produce a score equivalent to a Long-Term Foreign-\b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  IDR. \uc2 Fitch\uc2 's QO is a forward-looking qualitative framework designed to allow for adjustment to the SRM output to assign the final rating, reflecting factors within our criteria that are not fully quantifiable and/or not fully reflected in the SRM.\par \par \uc2 RATING SENSITIVITIES\par \par \uc2 - The main factors that, individually or collectively, could trigger a positive rating action are:\par \par \uc2 - Narrowing of structural weaknesses relative to peers, including per capita GDP and governance standards.\par \par \uc2 - - Material reduction in government debt ratios or contingent liabilities, accompanied by improved transparency.\par \par \uc2 The main factors that, individually or collectively, could trigger negative rating action are:\par \par \uc2 - Increase in the debt-to-GDP ratio, for example, from a failure to reduce the fiscal deficit or from crystallisation of additional contingent liabilities.\par \par \uc2 - - External financing pressures that strain macroeconomic stability.\par \par \uc2 - - Instability in the financial system, possibly triggered by excessive credit growth and a rise in household debt.\par \par \uc2 KEY ASSUMPTIONS\par \par \uc2 - - The global economy performs broadly in line with \uc2 Fitch\uc2 's global economic outlook.\par \par \uc2 The full list of rating actions is as follows:\par \par \uc2 Long-Term Foreign-\b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  IDR affirmed at 'A-'; Outlook Stable\par \par \uc2 - Long-Term Local-\b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  IDR affirmed at 'A-'; Outlook Stable\par \par \uc2 - Short-Term Foreign-\b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  IDR affirmed at 'F1'\par \par \uc2 - Short-Term Local-\b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  IDR affirmed at 'F1'\par \par \uc2 - Country Ceiling affirmed at 'A'\par \par \uc2 - Issue ratings on long-term senior unsecured local-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  bonds affirmed at 'A-'\par \par \uc2 - Issue ratings on short-term senior unsecured local-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  bonds affirmed at 'F1'\par \par \uc2 - Issue ratings on Malaysia's global sukuk trust certificates issued by Malaysia Sukuk Global Berhad affirmed at 'A-'\par \par \uc2 Contact:\par \par \uc2 Primary Analyst\par \par \uc2 - Sagarika Chandra\par \par \uc2 - Associate Director\par \par \uc2 - +852 2263 9921\par \par \uc2 - Fitch (Hong Kong) Limited\par \par \uc2 - 19/F Man Yee Building\par \par \uc2 - 68 Des Voeux Road Central, Hong Kong\par \par \uc2 Secondary Analyst\par \par \uc2 - \uc2 Stephen Schwartz\par \par \uc2 - Senior Director\par \par \uc2 - +852 2263 9938\par \par \uc2 Committee Chairperson\par \par \uc2 - Jan Friederich\par \par \uc2 - Senior Director\par \par \uc2 - +852 2263 9910\par \par \uc2 Media Relations: Wai-Lun Wan, Hong Kong, Tel: +852 2263 9935 , Email: wailun.wan@fitchratings.com.\par \par \uc2 Additional information is available on {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.fitchratings.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 www.fitchratings.com}}}\par \par \uc2 Applicable Criteria\par \par \uc2 - Country Ceilings Criteria (pub. 19 Jul 2018)\par \par \uc2 - {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.fitchratings.com/site/re/10037793"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://www.fitchratings.com/site/re/10037793 }}}\par \par \uc2 - Sovereign Rating Criteria (pub. 19 Jul 2018)\par \par \uc2 - {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.fitchratings.com/site/re/10037181"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://www.fitchratings.com/site/re/10037181 }}}\par \par \uc2 - Sukuk Rating Criteria