\par\par \b \uc2 BREAKINGVIEWS-Fund-scandal fallout keeps Malaysia on its toes\b0\par\par\uc2 605 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 Januar 2017\par 02:44\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2017 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 (The author is a Reuters Breakingviews columnist. The opinions expressed are her own.)\par \par \uc2 By Una Galani\par \par \uc2 HONG KONG, Jan 23 (Reuters Breakingviews) - Malaysia is still struggling to put its sovereign-fund scandal behind it. Prime Minister \uc2 Najib Razak\uc2  was not named in lawsuits filed last year by the \uc2 U.S. Department of Justice\uc2  after billions disappeared from \uc2 1Malaysia Development Berhad\uc2 , and he has deftly avoided a legal tangle on his home turf. Nonetheless, the fallout is keeping Malaysia's leader on his toes.\par \par \uc2 The scandal at the fund Najib championed has left a dark mark on sentiment around the country. Money from the fund found its way into financing the movie "The Wolf of Wall Street" and allegedly into Najib's personal bank account, something he denies. But mud sticks. One recent survey by FT Confidential Research found 71.5 percent of respondents had a negative view of the prime minister. Malaysia must hold an election by August 2018 at latest, but even with the redistricting of constituencies, it will be a slog to win more votes than in 2013 which saw the worst-ever performance for the Barisan Nasional ruling coalition.\par \par \uc2 The fallout is also playing out in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Since November 2014 the ringgit has slumped by over one-third against the U.S. dollar, three times more than the Philippine peso, Indonesian rupiah, Singaporean dollar and Thai baht. That frustrates Malaysia's middle class, many of whom aspire for the country to become more like Singapore; they complain that even visiting Thailand has become expensive. The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's slide has become a stick used by the opposition to bash the government.\par \par \uc2 The ringgit's weakness matters because voters care, and because foreigners own nearly half of Malaysia's government bonds. That's unfortunate because Najib has otherwise done a decent job of putting the economy on a solid footing. The government expects Malaysia could grow as much as 5 percent next year, and the country continues down the path of fiscal consolidation with the recent introduction of a goods and services tax, which is unpopular but has helped reduce the government's dependence on oil revenue.\par \par \uc2 As a small nation, surrounded by mostly larger, faster-growing neighbours, against a backdrop of rising U.S. rates, Malaysia needs to move on from 1MDB, but governance clearly matters to investors and possibly voters. Najib may have dodged a bullet abroad - but the struggle at home continues.\par \par \uc2 CONTEXT NEWS\par \par \uc2 - The Malaysian ringgit has fallen almost 34 percent against the U.S. dollar since November 2014 to trade around 4.4 per dollar, more than three times the declines posted by the Philippine peso, Indonesian rupiah, Singaporean dollar and Thai baht over the same period.\par \par \uc2 - In November, \uc2 Bank Negara Malaysia\uc2  took action to try stop \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders overseas driving the ringgit lower. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  insisted banks sign a commitment to cease trading the ringgit on the offshore non-deliverable forward market. It was one of several measures taken by \uc2 Bank Negara\uc2  to try to stem the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's recent slide.\par \par \uc2 - \b \highlight22\uc2 Malaysia\b0 \highlight\uc2  has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the onshore market to support the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  reported that as of Dec. 30, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves fell to $94.6 billion, equivalent to 8.8 months of retained imports. Reserves stood at $96.4 billion a month earlier.\par \par \uc2 - For previous columns by the author, Reuters customers can click on\par \par \uc2 - SIGN UP FOR BREAKINGVIEWS EMAIL ALERTS: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://bit.ly/BVsubscribe"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://bit.ly/BVsubscribe}}}\par \par \uc2 (On \uc2 Twitter{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/ugalani"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://twitter.com/ugalani}}}\uc2  Editing by Pete Sweeney and Kate Duguid)\par \par \uc2 Released: 2017-1-26T01:44:32.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020170123ed1n002s1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
