\par\par \b \uc2 Bank Negara Malaysia\uc2  To Hold Rates Through 2017? -- Barron's Blog\b0\par\par\uc2 By Shuli Ren \par \uc2 351 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 November 2016\par 08:51\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2016, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 As expected, \uc2 Malaysia's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  kept its policy rate unchanged at 3%, amid worries that the Malaysian ringgit would weaken further if its benchmark policy rate is cut.\par \par \uc2 Bank Negara \b \highlight22\uc2 Malaysia\b0 \highlight\uc2  has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets last week to support the ringgit, and warned foreign investors not to short ringgit forwards sparking fears of another capital control. The ringgit is the biggest loser among emerging Asia \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  this month, down by more than 5% against the U.S. dollar and is now not far off the Asian Financial Crisis lows.\par \par \uc2 The street expects BNM to cut rates again in 2017. But \uc2 Capital Economics\uc2  believes this expectation needs to be adjusted. Analyst Krystal Tan wrote:\par \par \uc2 There are fears that Donald Trump's reflationary policies could lead to a rise in US inflation and trigger the Fed to hike interest rates aggressively, leading to a pull-back of capital flows from emerging markets. The need to roll-over Malaysia's large stock of short term external debt (which is the equivalent to around 28% of GDP) makes the ringgit vulnerable during periods of global risk aversion. Malaysia's relatively high level of foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  debt, estimated at close to 40% of GDP, means it won't want to risk sharp falls in the ringgit.\par \par \uc2 The tone of today's statement suggests that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is in little hurry to adjust monetary policy settings any time soon, and there was no mention of the possible introduction of capital controls which had recently been mooted in the press. With inflation set to remain low, we expect interest rates in Malaysia to remain on hold through 2017.\par \par \uc2 Indeed, analysts changed their November rate cut expectations quite recently amid ringgit's weakness.\par \par \uc2 The ringgit fell another 0.5% to 4.438 per dollar today. Year-to-date, the \uc2 iShares MSCI Malaysia ETF\uc2  (EWM) fell 2.1%. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  More at Barron's Asia Stocks to Watch blog, \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://blogs.barrons.com/asiastocks/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://blogs.barrons.com/asiastocks/}}}\par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 November 23, 2016 02:51 ET (07:51 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020161123ecbn000iu\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
