\par\par \b \uc2 Malaysia, Indonesia markets roiled as investors scramble for hedge on Trump\b0\par\par\uc2 617 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 November 2016\par 05:31\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2016 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 JAKARTA, Nov 11 (Reuters) - Emerging markets in Southeast Asia were slammed on Friday as the stunning upset of Donald Trump's presidential win in the United States reverberated around the world, with \b \highlight22\uc2 Malaysia\b0 \highlight\uc2  and Indonesian central banks \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to try to stem the flow of money out of stocks and bonds.\par \par \par \par \uc2 The latest selloff was triggered by markets recalibrating their expectations of a Trump presidency on broad economic policy, with a consensus that his policies will be inflationary and push U.S. rates up driving investors out of emerging markets and into dollar-based assets.\par \par \par \par \uc2 Yields on benchmark 10-year Treasuries have spiked 41 basis points in the past two days as investors scrambled to readjust their positions.\par \par \par \par \uc2 Emerging markets in Asia are particularly vulnerable to hot money outflows, and deep uncertainty over how broad U.S. and international policy will ultimately play out under Trump has unsettled investors.\par \par \par \par \uc2 On Friday's Asian session, the differential between the onshore spot rate in the Malaysian ringgit and the offshore NDF rate spread hit its widest since 2009.\par \par \par \par \uc2 Ringgit one-month non-deliverable forwards (NDFs)  plunged to 4.5280 per dollar, while spot ringgit stood at 4.2670. As a result, the dollar/ringgit's NDFs premium over the dollar/ringgit spot widened to 0.2610, the widest since at least April 2008, according to Reuters data.\par \par \par \par \uc2 The subdued spot rate belied the drama because \uc2 Bank Negara Malaysia\uc2  was acting to stem any panic, traders said.\par \par \par \par \uc2 Malaysia's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor \uc2 Muhammad Ibrahim\uc2  told reporters on Friday the ringgit should not be priced out of sync with fundamentals, and that it has a responsibility to tell banks to take temporary measures to calm the market\par \par \par \par \uc2 "We don't want to be dictated by factors that have nothing to do with the country's fundamentals," Ibrahim said.\par \par \par \par \uc2 Traders in Kuala Lumpur said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had told them not to quote offshore rates and was approving large ringgits sell orders on a one-off basis in a bid to keep a lid on things. The tactic seemed to work with onshore trade reportedly very thin.\par \par \par \par \uc2 However, yields on Malaysian government bonds told another story. Yields on 10-years have widened 22 basis points since Wednesday, while those on 20-year and 30 year bonds have widened 21 basis points and 10 basis points respectively over the same period.\par \par \par \par \uc2 Almost 40 percent of Malaysian government bonds are in foreign hands.\par \par \par \par \uc2 Malaysian stocks were down almost one percent.\par \par \par \par \uc2 Indonesian markets also dived in early trade. Indonesia markets have enjoyed relatively high inflows into it stocks and bonds markets in the past few months, making them vulnerable to hot money outflows at times of uncertainty.\par \par \par \par \uc2 The rupiah fell as much as 2.7 percent, while Jakarta Composite Index  fell as much as 3.2 percent to its lowest since Sept 16.\par \par \par \par \uc2 Bank Indonesia\uc2  sold dollars to stabilise the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , traders said, but it still fell to a four-month low.\par \par \par \par \uc2 Yield of Indonesia's 10-year government bonds jumped on Friday to 7.462 percent from 7.417 percent. Foreigners own 38.4 percent of outstanding Indonesian government bonds.\par \par \par \par \uc2 "Trump will pick up government spending, which may have (the Fed) raise interest rates faster-than-expected to tame inflation," saggid Taye Shim, a strategist with Daewoo Securities in Jakarta.\par \par \par \par \uc2 Philippine stocks were also caught in the selloff with the main index tumbling more than 2.5 percent. The Philippines peso, however, was steady at 48.99.\par \par \par \par \uc2 ($1 = 13,490 rupiah)   (Reporting by Fransiska Nangoy and Jongwoo Cheon; Writing by Nachum Kaplan; Editing by Shri Navaratnam)\par \par \uc2 Released: 2016-11-14T04:31:24.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020161111ecbb007k9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
