\par\par \b \uc2 SGD/MYR Crosses 3.00 Hurdle as Ringgit Worst Hit in Asia -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 201 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 August 2015\par 04:56\par \uc2 Dow Jones Newswires Chinese (English)\par \uc2 RTNW\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2015, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 0235 GMT [Dow Jones] One Singapore dollar now buys slightly more than 3 Malaysian ringgit, setting a record high for the SGD/MYR cross-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  exchange rate. While all Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  have fallen Monday due to global risk aversion sending investors toward safe haven assets such as gold and the U.S. dollar, the Malaysian ringgit is by far the worst affected in the region. The ringgit is down 2.0% against the U.S. dollar today, whereas other Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  are mostly down by less than 0.5%. Traders are losing faith in the \uc2 Malaysia \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's ability to stop the ringgit's slide. Data released Friday showed foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves fell again, evidence that \uc2 Bank Negara \b \highlight22\uc2 Malaysia\b0 \highlight\uc2  has still been \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market by selling U.S. dollars - yet the ringgit continues to depreciate to new 17-year lows on a daily basis. SGD/MYR is now 3.0045 from its Friday close of 2.9620, implying a 1.4% fall of the ringgit versus the Singapore dollar. (ewen.chew@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 Editor: MNG\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 24-08-15 0256GMT\par \par \uc2 Dokument RTNW000020150824eb8o0004c\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
