\par\par \b \uc2 Malaysia Ringgit Only Tad Weaker,  \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank \highlight\uc2  Suspected -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 204 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 August 2015\par 04:58\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2015, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 0258 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] Malaysia's ringgit has weakened, albeit just slightly, after China's abrupt move to devalue the yuan Tuesday. The 2% devaluation of the yuan has sent other USD/Asia pairs sharply higher, but USD/MYR is only up marginally to imply a 0.2% fall for the ringgit, fueling speculation that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is capping the pair to prevent further \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  weakness. By comparison, the Singapore dollar fell as much as 0.9% to the U.S. dollar. \uc2 Bank Negara \b \highlight22\uc2 Malaysia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's suspected \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to limit \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  depreciation is raising concerns as foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves have already been depleted to five-year lows, as confirmed by data released Friday. Keeping the USD/MYR capped by selling U.S. dollars on the spot market will further shrink Malaysia's foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves and could raise alarm bells at credit rating agencies if reserves fall to dangerously low levels. This could spark a vicious cycle of ringgit depreciation.\par \par \uc2  USD/MYR is now 3.9400 from its Monday close of 3.9330. (ewen.chew@wsj.com) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: JFN \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 August 10, 2015 22:58 ET (02:58 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020150811eb8b0008v\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
