\par\par \b \uc2 EM ASIA  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2 -Asia  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  falls as U.S. rate hike views swing back to September\b0\par\par\uc2 951 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 August 2015\par 08:00\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2015 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par * Ringgit at fresh 17-year low; Malaysia bonds fall\par     * Solid Malaysia June exports data not helps much\par     * Won hits 3-year low; \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  caution grows\par \par  (Adds text, updates prices)\par     By Jongwoo Cheon\par     SINGAPORE, Aug 5 (Reuters) - Most emerging Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par slid on Wednesday after Atlanta Federal Reserve President Dennis\par Lockhart expressed support for a September U.S. interest rate\par hike.\par     Despite an unexpected increase in June exports, the\par Malaysian ringgit fell to levels last seen during the\par 1997-98 Asian financial crisis, when policymakers pegged it to\par the dollar.\par     Foreign banks continued to sell the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , traders said.\par     Offshore funds pushed the South Korean won to its\par weakest in more than three years.\par     The Singapore dollar also slid to a near five-month\par low. Indonesia's rupiah stayed around a 17-year trough,\par although it pared some of losses after the second-quarter\par economic growth data. \par     Lockhart, a voter this year on the Federal Open Market\par Committee, told the Wall Street Journal that it would take\par "significant deterioration" in the U.S. economy for him to not\par support a rate hike in September. He is considered a centrist on\par the committee. \par     After the comments, the U.S. dollar rose to a near\par four-month peak against a basket of six major \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and\par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Treasury\f28 \uc2 \f2  yields advanced. Other emerging market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  such\par as Latin American units slumped.\par     The remarks raised the odds on the margin that the Fed will\par start its policy tightening lift-off in September, said Andy Ji,\par Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist for \f28 \uc2 \f2 Commonwealth Bank of Australia\f28 \uc2 \f2  in\par Singapore.\par     Investors are looking for clues on the timing of the Fed's\par rate hike from U.S. nonfarm payrolls data due on Friday, but the\par indicator is unlikely to dent expectations that rates will rise\par by year-end, Ji said.\par     "The view of a lift-off this year is still intact. A hike\par will happen. If not in September, it will be October, November\par or December."\par     That will keep hurting emerging Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , especially\par the ringgit, the Singapore dollar and the Taiwan dollar\par , which are more correlated with higher U.S. rates, he\par added.\par     \par     WON\par     The won lost as much as 0.8 percent to 1,175.1 per dollar,\par its weakest since June 2012.\par     The South Korean \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  pared earlier losses on caution\par over possible \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  authorities\par to support it.\par     \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves fell in July to a three-month low,\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  data showed earlier, as some traders suspected the\par authorities of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to stem the won's weakness.\par  \par     Last month, the won lost 4.7 percent against the dollar, the\par largest monthly slide since September 2011, according to Thomson\par Reuters data.\par     Still, the won is likely to stay weaker ahead of a key U.S.\par jobs data on Friday. It may weaken to 1,183.8-1,185.6, lows of\par May-June 2012, analysts said.\par     \par     RINGGIT\par     The ringgit slid 0.6 percent to 3.8780 per dollar, its\par weakest since Sept. 2 1998, a day before the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  was pegged\par at 3.8000. The peg remained in place until 2005.\par     The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  came under further pressure from dollar demand\par linked daily fixing.\par     The five-year government bond yield rose to 3.658\par percent, its highest since June 30.\par     The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is unlikely to rebound as opposition parties\par and activists keep up pressure on Prime Minister \f28 \uc2 \f2 Najib Razak\f28 \uc2 \f2 \par over corruption allegations swirling around indebted state-fund\par 1MDB, analysts said.\par     While June trade data was better than expected, a survey\par earlier in the week showed manufacturing activity contracted\par again in July under pressure from weak domestic demand.\par     "The fundamentals are being overshadowed by domestic\par political developments that have soured sentiment towards\par Malaysian assets," said Khoon Goh, senior \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist for ANZ\par in a research note.\par     ANZ revised its year-end ringgit target to 3.9500.\par     Caution grew over the prospect of further \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by\par the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to prop up the worst-performing Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par so far this year, but doubts also lingered over how heavily \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank\par Negara \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Malaysia\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  would \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  with \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves\par falling.\par     \par     SINGAPORE DOLLAR\par     The Singapore dollar fell as much as 0.4 percent to 1.3872\par per the U.S. dollar, its weakest since March 20.\par     The city-state's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is seen testing 1.3900 as it\par cleared a chart support at a chart support of 1.3815, analysts\par said.\par     \par     \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CURRENCIES\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  VS U.S. DOLLAR\par     Change on the day at 0530 GMT\par   \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid   Previous day    Pct Move\par   Japan yen       124.36         124.38       +0.02\par   Sing dlr        1.3851         1.3811       -0.29\par   Taiwan dlr      31.644         31.726       +0.26\par   Korean won     1172.70        1165.50       -0.61\par   Baht             35.08          35.00       -0.23\par   Peso             45.75          45.61       -0.31\par   Rupiah        13500.00       13483.00       -0.13\par   Rupee            63.86          63.75       -0.18\par   Ringgit         3.8750         3.8535       -0.55\par   Yuan            6.2097         6.2096       -0.00\par  \par   Change so far in 2015\par   \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid  End prev year    Pct Move\par   Japan yen       124.36         119.66       -3.78\par   Sing dlr        1.3851         1.3260       -4.27\par   Taiwan dlr      31.644         31.718       +0.23\par   Korean won     1172.70        1099.30       -6.26\par   Baht             35.08          32.90       -6.21\par   Peso             45.75          44.72       -2.25\par   Rupiah        13500.00       12380.00       -8.30\par   Rupee  