\par\par \b \uc2 EM ASIA  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2 -Ringgit slides to pre-peg levels on commodity rout, RBA helps Asia  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 653 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 August 2015\par 08:51\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2015 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par * \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Malaysia\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  stocks, bond prices down\par     * Doubts over "heavy" \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  lingers\par     * Singapore dollar, won turn firmer\par \par  (Adds text, updates prices)\par     SINGAPORE, Aug 4 (Reuters) - Malaysia's ringgit slumped on\par Tuesday, returning to the pre-peg levels seen during the Asian\par financial crisis, as falling commodity prices hurt confidence\par further.\par     Most emerging Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  turned firmer on a rise in\par the Australian dollar after the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Reserve Bank of\par Australia\f28 \uc2 \f2  (RBA) toned down calls for a further fall in the local\par unit. \par     The ringgit lost as much as 0.4 percent to 3.8685 per\par dollar, its weakest since Sept. 2 1998, a day before the\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  was pegged at 3.8000.\par     Malaysia's stocks lost 1.1 percent, underperforming\par Southeast Asian equities. Most government bond prices also fell.\par     Sentiment on the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's financial assets has been\par already hurt by the political pressure on Prime Minister Najib\par Razak as a result of the scandal over indebted state-fund 1MDB.\par     Traders were wary of possible \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central\par bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to support the worst-performing Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  this year.\par     But doubts also lingered over how aggressively \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank Negara\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Malaysia\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  would \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to prop up the ringgit given its\par depleting ammunition. In the first half of July alone,\par Malaysia's international reserves were down $5 billion.\par  \par     The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  was on Monday spotted \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the\par market, but it did not defend specific levels, traders said.\par     On Tuesday, the ringgit pared earlier losses, tracking a\par rebound in regional \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , traders said.\par     The ringgit lost 1.3 percent against the dollar in July when\par the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  was spotted heavily \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , traders said.\par     That compared with a 4.7 percent loss in South Korea's won\par  and a 4.0 percent slide in Thailand's baht.\par     A strong won is a headache for South Korea as the yen's\par weakness helps Japanese exporters. Thailand's top policymakers\par have said a weaker baht is better than a rate cut for the\par economy.\par     \par     RBA HELPS ASIA \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par     Most emerging Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  turned firmer after the\par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Reserve Bank of Australia\f28 \uc2 \f2  dropped a reference to a further\par decline being necessary, saying only that the Aussie was\par adjusting to weak commodity prices.\par     Singapore's dollar reversed earlier losses after\par falling to 1.3809 per U.S. dollar, its weakest since March 23.\par     The city-state's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  earlier came under pressure on\par concerns about a slowing economy. Activity in Singapore\par factories including the key electronics sector contracted in\par July, a survey showed on Monday. \par     The won also ended the local session firmer as traders\par covered short positions on the Australian dollar's strength.\par     \par     \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CURRENCIES\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  VS U.S. DOLLAR\par     Change on the day at 0600 GMT\par   \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid   Previous day    Pct Move\par   Japan yen       123.96         124.03       +0.06\par   Sing dlr        1.3775         1.3783       +0.06\par   Taiwan dlr      31.640         31.705       +0.21\par   Korean won     1164.76        1165.90       +0.10\par   Baht             35.05          35.06       +0.03\par   Peso             45.76          45.66       -0.21\par   Rupiah        13490.00       13495.00       +0.04\par   Rupee            63.93          64.04       +0.18\par   Ringgit         3.8570         3.8530       -0.10\par   Yuan            6.2094         6.2097       +0.00\par  \par   Change so far in 2015\par   \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid  End prev year    Pct Move\par   Japan yen       123.96         119.66       -3.47\par   Sing dlr        1.3775         1.3260       -3.74\par   Taiwan dlr      31.640         31.718       +0.25\par   Korean won     1164.76        1099.30       -5.62\par   Baht             35.05          32.90       -6.13\par   Peso             45.76          44.72       -2.26\par   Rupiah        13490.00       12380.00       -8.23\par   Rupee            63.93          63.03       -1.40\par   Ringgit         3.8570         3.4965       -9.35\par   Yuan            6.2094         6.2040       -0.09\par  \par  (Reporting by Jongwoo Cheon; Editing by Shri Navaratnam)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Released: 2015-8-4T07:5