\par\par \b \uc2 EM ASIA  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2 -Asia  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  make weak gains, ringgit falls to pre-peg levels\b0\par\par\uc2 969 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 August 2015\par 08:32\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2015 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par * \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Malaysia\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  c.bank spotted \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , but sporadically\par     * \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Malaysia FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves data on Friday closely eyed\par     * Baht, won gain on short-covering\par \par  (Adds text, updates prices)\par     By Jongwoo Cheon\par     SINGAPORE, Aug 3 (Reuters) - Most emerging Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par rose on Monday after weak U.S. wage growth clouded views on when\par U.S. interest rates were likely to rise, but the outlook for\par them was gloomy given the growing concerns over China's slowing\par economy.\par     The Malaysian ringgit's woes worsened due to the\par political pressures on Prime Minister \f28 \uc2 \f2 Najib Razak\f28 \uc2 \f2  as a result of\par the scandal over indebted state-fund 1MDB. There were some\par doubts over how heavily \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank Negara \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Malaysia\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  would \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to\par prop up the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  if foreign reserves dwindled too fast.\par     On Monday, with the ringgit hit a level unseen since the day\par before it was pegged at 3.80 per dollar in early September 1998,\par  as the then government of prime minister Mahathir Mohamad put a\par floor under the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to stop it falling further during the \par     Thailand's baht and South Korea's won \par led gains among regional \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  as investors cut dollar\par holdings after data on Friday showed U.S. labour costs in the\par second quarter reported their smallest growth in 33 years.\par  \par     The regional \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  recovery rebound lost momentum after\par a private survey showed China's factory activity shrank more\par than initially estimated in July, dampening hopes that the\par world's second-largest economy was stabilising. \par     Also, despite the weak U.S. wage growth, expectations for a\par rise in U.S. rates later this year remained intact, leaving\par emerging Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  vulnerable, traders and analysts said.\par     "Given the strong NFP figures over the last many months and\par the strong preliminary Q2 GDP, we still expect first hike in\par September," said Amy Yuan Zhuang, a senior analyst at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Nordea\f28 \uc2 \f2  in\par Singapore.\par     Investors are awaiting U.S. nonfarm payrolls data due on\par Friday. U.S. employers were expected to have added 222,000 jobs\par last month, compared to 223,000 increases in the previous month,\par according to Reuters' survey of economists. June's\par payroll growth ran well above the average for the first five\par months. \par     U.S. economy grew at a 2.3 percent annual rate, faster than\par a 0.6 percent expansion in the previous three months, on solid\par consumer spending. \par     "Given the strong NFP figures over the last many months and\par the strong preliminary Q2 GDP, we still expect first hike in\par September," said Amy Yuan Zhuang, a senior analyst at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Nordea\f28 \uc2 \f2  in\par Singapore.\par     "Yellen made it very clear that a hike this year is almost\par certain, unless something really surprises on the downside."\par     Last month, Fed Chair \f28 \uc2 \f2 Janet Yellen\f28 \uc2 \f2  said the U.S. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central\par bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will likely hike interest rates if the world's top economy\par grows as expected.\par     \par     RINGGIT\par     The ringgit slid 0.7 percent to 3.8500 per dollar. That was\par its weakest since Sept. 2, 1998, when the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  traded\par between 3.79 and 4.00 to the dollar, on the day before it was\par pegged. The ringgit was taken off the peg in 2005, but its\par return to those levels has given Malaysians a sharp reminder of\par hard times.\par     Trading was thin on Monday, as foreign banks sold the\par ringgit. Foreign reserves data due on Friday will be closely\par watched after losing $5 billion in the first half of June.\par  \par     "The reserves data may gain increasing importance to the\par market as a signal of nervousness from policy makers," said\par Sacha Tihanyi, senior \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist for Scotiabank in a\par research note.\par     The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  was on Monday spotted \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to\par support the worst-performing Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  so far this year,\par though it did not appear to be defending any specific levels,\par traders said. \par     Some government bond prices fell with the five-year\par government bond yield rising to 3.619 percent, its\par highest since July 9.\par     \par     BAHT\par     The baht gained as traders covered short positions ahead of\par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Thailand's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's monetary policy meeting on Wednesday.\par     The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has barely moved since data showed July\par consumer prices fell for a seventh straight month.\par  \par     Most economist expect \f28 \uc2 \f2 the Bank of Thailand\f28 \uc2 \f2  to leave its\par benchmark interest rate unchanged for now. \par     \par     WON\par     The won advanced as weak U.S. wage data prompted stop-loss\par dollar selling.\par     Offshore funds bought the South Korean \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to cover\par short positions, seeing its recent weakness as excessive,\par traders said.\par     The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  lost 4.7 percent against the dollar in July,\par the largest monthly loss since September 2011, Thomson Reuters\par data showed.\par     \par     \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CURRENCIES\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  VS U.S. DOLLAR\par     Change on the day at 0530 GMT\par   \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid   Previous day    Pct Move\par   Japan yen       124.04         123.89       -0.12\par   Sing dlr        1.3749         1.3723       -0.19\par   Taiwan dlr      31.591         31.682       +0.29\par   Korean won     1166.20        1170.00       +0.33\par   Baht             35.01          35.18       +0.49\par   Peso             45.66          45.74       +0.19\par   Rupiah        13505.00       13525.00       +0.15\par   Rupee            63.97          64.13       +0.26\par   Ringgit         3.8460         3.8245       -0.56\par   Yuan            6.2097         6.2097       +0.00\par  \par   Change so far in 2015\par   \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid  End prev year    Pct Move\par   Japan yen       124.04         119.66       -3.54\par   Sing dlr        1.3749         1.3260       -3.56\par   Taiwan dlr      31.591         31.718       +0.40\par   Korean won     1166.20        1099.30       -5.74\par   Baht             35.01          32.90       -6.03\par   Peso             45.66          44.72       -2.05\par   Rupiah        13505.00     