\par\par \b \uc2 Malaysia: Forget About 1MDB Scandal, Ringgit Poses A Bigger Risk -- Barron's Blog\b0\par\par\uc2 By Shuli Ren   \par \uc2 344 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 Juli 2015\par 06:42\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2015, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 Political uncertainty in Malaysia is on the rise.\par \par \uc2 Prime Minister \uc2 Najib Razak\uc2  on Tuesday sacked deputy prime minister Muhyiddin Yassin after Yassin publicly called on Razak to explain the scandal around state investment company \uc2 1Malaysia Development Bhd\uc2  (1MDB). The Prime Minister was accused of taking money from the fund, a story broken by The Wall Street Journal. Yassin on Wednesday said he hoped the investigation into the 1MDB scandal would continue.\par \par \uc2 While it is interesting political theater, "1MDB's financial woes are unlikely to have a big direct impact on the economy," wrote Capital Economics. "Consumers, at least, appear far more concerned about the effects of the recent introduction of the general sales tax on their spending power than any fall-out from 1MDB."\par \par \uc2 Where the Malaysian ringgit is heading likely poses a far bigger threat to the Malaysian economy than the 1MDB scandal. The ringgit this week has been hovering around 3.80 per U.S. dollar, its lowest level in almost 17 years.\par \par \uc2 A weak ringgit is worrisome because despite a weakening economy, the Malaysian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is reluctant to cut rates. The Bank kept its policy rate on hold at 3.25% in its July meeting. Further, "foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves fell sharply in the first half of July, suggesting that BNM may have \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," wrote the research firm.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Malaysia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's economy likely slowed further in the second-half. Industrial production expanded only 4.3% from a year ago in April and May, down from 6.5% in the first-quarter. Exports declined further, by 7.8%, versus a 2.5% fall in the first-quarter.\par \par \uc2 The FTSE Bursa Malaysia KL Index has fallen 8.8% from its late-April high. The U.S. dollar-denominated \uc2 iShares MSCI Malaysia ETF\uc2  ( EWM) has retreated 16.5%. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  More at Barron's Asia Stocks to Watch blog, \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://blogs.barrons.com/asiastocks/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://blogs.barrons.com/asiastocks/}}}\par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 July 30, 2015 00:42 ET (04:42 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020150730eb7u000db\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
