\par\par \b \uc2 Malaysia's  \highlight22\uc2 Foreign-Exchange \highlight\uc2  Reserves Likely to Fall Below $100 Billion: UOB -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 223 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 Juli 2015\par 13:59\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2015, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 1159 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] Malaysia's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves are likely to fall below $100 billion by end-July, UOB says, citing heavy capital outflows. This view comes after the reserves fell by $5 billion to $100.5 billion, the lowest since 2010, as at mid-July compared with $105.5 billion at June 30. Foreigners were net sellers of Malaysian equities with an outflow of 1 billion ringgit in the first half of July. Foreigners have been net sellers of Malaysian equities for 11 straight weeks. The fall in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves in \b \highlight22\uc2 Malaysia\b0 \highlight\uc2  in July reflects \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to defend the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and smoothen out excessive volatility that could otherwise further dent investor and domestic sentiment reserves, UOB adds. The USD/MYR fell 0.9% to 3.8065 as at mid-July from 3.773 at end-June. Since January, the ringgit is the worst-performing \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , down 8% to 3.8076.\par \par \uc2  But due to the likely \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the ringgit broke above 3.80 in early July and continues to linger in the range of 3.77 and 3.8090 against USD. (saurabh.chaturvedi@wsj.com; \uc2 Twitter\uc2 : @journosaurabh) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: AAP \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 July 24, 2015 07:59 ET (11:59 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020150724eb7o001b7\par }\page {\p