\par\par \b \uc2 Singapore Dollar Falls From Record High Vs Malaysia Ringgit -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 191 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 Juli 2015\par 04:17\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2015, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 0217 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] The Singapore dollar, which hit a record high versus Malaysia's ringgit early July, has retreated by 1.8% versus the ringgit over the last three weeks. The Singapore dollar has weakened due to broad U.S. dollar strength - which has also hurt other Asia \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  - but the ringgit has been kept stable by suspected \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Onshore sources say that \uc2 Bank Negara \b \highlight22\uc2 Malaysia\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been selling U.S. dollars in the spot market around the 3.8070-3.8080 level, preventing the ringgit from depreciating any further. The ringgit has fallen sharply in recent months, hitting a 10-year low and dropping below the 3.8000 level at which it was pegged against U.S. dollar from 1998-2005. Aside from expectations of a U.S. rate increase, the ringgit outlook has dimmed due to the possibility of political instability ahead. SGD/MYR is now 2.7743 versus its Monday close of 2.7765. (ewen.chew@wsj.com) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: MNG \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 July 20, 2015 22:17 ET (02:17 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020150721eb7l0001r\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
