\par\par \b \uc2 CORRECTED-Malaysian  \highlight22\uc2 central bank \highlight\uc2  defends ailing ringgit amid political storm\b0\par\par\uc2 722 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 Juli 2015\par 12:40\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2015 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 (Corrects penultimate paragraph to show foreigners were net buyers not sellers, changes wording at start of last paragraph)\par \par \uc2 By Jongwoo Cheon\par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE, July 7 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Malaysia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  for a third successive trading day on Tuesday to support the ringgit \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , traders said, as corruption allegations swirled round Prime Minister \uc2 Najib Razak\uc2 , raising doubts over his grip on power.\par \par \uc2 Thanks to \uc2 Bank Negara\uc2 's damage limitation, the ringgit has lost only 0.8 percent since the political storm broke on Friday, but it has weakened past an old \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  peg of 3.80 per dollar, fixed in September 1998 during the Asian financial crisis and scrapped in 2005.\par \par \uc2 The ringgit closed at 3.8060 on Tuesday, helped off a low of 3.8100 by the heaviest day for \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  so far as foreign investors sold Malaysian assets.\par \par \uc2 A scandal over the $11.6 billion of debt wracked up by state fund 1MDB had been bubbling for months, with Malaysia's longest serving leader, former prime minister Mahathir Mohamed, making repeated calls for Najib to step down while asking where the money had gone.\par \par \uc2 But a Wall Street Journal report on Friday sent shockwaves through Malaysia and financial markets by alleging nearly $700 million was transferred into bank accounts believed to belong to Najib.\par \par \uc2 Reuters is unable to verify the report. Najib has denied taking any money from the debt-laden state fund or any other entity for personal gain.\par \par \uc2 "The political drama is unlikely to be fixed overnight. It will continue and put the ringgit under pressure," said Lee Jin-yang, a macro analyst at \uc2 Aberdeen Asset Management\uc2  in Singapore.\par \par \uc2 "The ringgit is likely to face additional headwinds from broader U.S. dollar strength and weak oil price as well," Lee said.\par \par \uc2 He, like many other traders, did not see any quick reversal of fortunes for the ringgit.\par \par \uc2 The 3.8000 level remains a strong psychological reference point for Malaysians, even though the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  brought the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  off the peg 10 years ago.\par \par \uc2 Technical analysts have a more dispassionate perspective, and they see the ringgit gathering chart support between 3.8000 and 3.8200 - marking the 38.2 percent Fibonacci retracement of its depreciation from 1978 to 1998.\par \par \uc2 If the support level is breached, the ringgit may weaken to 3.9060, the 50 percent retracement of its appreciation from 1998 to 2011, they said.\par \par \uc2 Political risk had remained a background factor until recently, but the ringgit was already weighed down by worries about the impact of weak global prices on its oil and gas earnings.\par \par \uc2 It is Asia's weakest performing \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  this year, having fallen 8.1 percent against the dollar.\par \par \uc2 Yet last week, \uc2 Fitch Ratings\uc2  somewhat unexpectedly opted to switch its outlook on the rating to "stable" from "negative".\par \par \uc2 Fitch had been the most pessimistic of the three major international credit ratings agencies on Malaysia, and there had been fears of a possible downgrade.\par \par \uc2 Instead, Fitch said its improved outlook "reflects the strong economic fundamentals and the sound financial position of the country".\par \par \uc2 Moody's Investors Service\uc2 , said on Monday that 1MDB did not pose a systemic risk to Malaysia's economy, banks, or government finances.\par \par \uc2 The government's fiscal deficit is improving. Inflation is benign, and economic growth, despite slower exports, was a respectable 5.6 percent in the first quarter.\par \par \uc2 Moreover, the worries about the weak crude price have been overdone, as Malaysia sells very little oil, but is the world's second largest exporter of gas, and gas prices have come down less steeply.\par \par \uc2 But the country and its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  could suffer from investors' growing aversion to risk as the Greek debt crisis rumbles on and China's stock market tumbles.\par \par \uc2 Foreigners are major investors in Malaysian government ringgit bonds. They bought a net 28.7 billion ringgit ($7.54 billion) worth of the government bonds in the first six months of the year, according to \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  data.\par \par \uc2 If they were to start dumping bonds heavily, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may have to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  more intensively in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, traders said. ($1 = 3.8040 ringgit) (Additional reporting by Reuters \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  Analyst Krishna Kumar in SYDNEY and Yantoultra Ngui in KUALA LUMPUR; Editing by Simon Cameron-Moore) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2015-7-8T11:40:20.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020150707eb7700axo\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
