\par\par \b \uc2 EM ASIA  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2 -China stock rout hits EM Asian  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2 ; Malaysia cbank supports ringgit for 4th day\b0\par\par\uc2 843 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 Juli 2015\par 07:50\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2015 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par * Malaysian stocks, bond prices fall\par     * Won at 2-year low, foreigners keep selling S.Korea stocks\par     * Thailand's baht near 6-year low\par \par  (Adds text, updates prices)\par     By Jongwoo Cheon\par     SINGAPORE, July 8 (Reuters) - Most emerging Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par lost ground on Wednesday as a  rout in China's equity markets\par and Greece's festering debt crisis hurt investors' appetite for\par riskier assets.\par     Traders said \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Malaysia\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  was spotted \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets for the fourth straight session to stem \par pressure on the ringgit as political tensions mounted\par over Prime Minister \f28 \uc2 \f2 Najib Razak\f28 \uc2 \f2  and debt-laden state fund 1MDB.\par     The ringgit was little changed by midday, while Malaysian\par stocks and bonds fell.\par     The South Korean won hit a two-year low against\par the dollar as foreign investors kept selling Seoul shares\par . Against the yen, the won slid to its weakest\par in more than three months as the Japanese \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  rose on\par safe-haven seekers.\par     Thailand's baht fell to as low as 34.051 per\par dollar, its weakest since September 2009.\par     Chinese stocks tumbled again despite a fresh round of\par government rescue measures. One regulatory official said\par investors were in the grip of "panic", boosting fears of a\par possible financial crisis in the world's second-largest\par economy. \par     "China's continuous efforts have not eased the panic\par sentiment. That increased worries about the economy," said Jeong\par My-young, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Samsung\f28 \uc2 \f2  Futures' research head in Seoul.\par     "Investors had hoped China's economic stimulus in the first\par half could take effect from the third quarter. But the hope\par waned as a series of steps failed to lift stocks."\par     China is a major export market for many emerging Asian\par countries and regional \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  have been under pressure from\par the sluggish Chinese economy.\par     \par     RINGGIT\par     The ringgit barely moved as the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  was seen\par keeping the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  firmer than 3.8100 per dollar. On Tuesday,\par it weakened past the old \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  peg of 3.80 per dollar, fixed\par in September 1998 during the Asian financial crisis and scrapped\par in 2005.\par     Still, the Malaysian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is expected to remain weak as \par stocks and government bond prices slid.\par     Prime Minister Najib has instructed his lawyers to consider\par action against the Wall Street Journal (WSJ), which reported\par that investigators probing 1MDB had traced nearly $700 million\par to bank accounts in his name. \par     Najib has denied taking any money from 1MDB or any other\par entity for personal gain.\par     Kuala Lumpur shares fell 0.8 percent. The 10-year\par , 5-year and 3-year government\par bond yields rose.\par     \par     WON\par     The won lost as much as 0.8 percent to 1,139.2 per dollar,\par its weakest since July 10 2013, as offshore funds sold the\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par     Seoul shares fell 0.9 percent as foreign investors\par were set to become net sellers in the main exchange for a fourth\par consecutive session. They unloaded 319.2 billion won ($281.1\par million) worth of stocks after dumping a combined net 400.0\par billion won in the previous three sessions, according to the\par Korea Exchange.\par     The South Korean \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  recovered some earlier losses\par ahead of a psychological support at 1,140, analysts said. It has\par a chart support line at 1,142.7, the 50 percent Fibonacci\par retracement level of its appreciation from 2010 to 2014, they\par added.\par     Caution also grew over possible \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign\par exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  authorities to stem volatility in the won, traders\par said.\par     That came as the won slid to the yen as much as 0.9 percent\par to 9.3338, its weakest since March 26.\par     The won's strength against the yen has been a headache for\par South Korea's policy makers as local exporters compete against\par Japanese rivals in overseas markets.\par     \par     \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CURRENCIES\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  VS U.S. DOLLAR\par     Change on the day at 0510 GMT\par   \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid   Previous day    Pct Move\par   Japan yen       122.02         122.56       +0.44\par   Sing dlr        1.3556         1.3540       -0.12\par   Taiwan dlr      31.022         31.162       +0.45\par   Korean won     1135.05        1130.20       -0.43\par   Baht             33.99          33.93       -0.18\par   Peso             45.23          45.18       -0.11\par   Rupiah        13332.00       13325.00       -0.05\par   Rupee            63.53          63.46       -