\par\par \b \uc2 Nikkei +1.4% Midday; Rebound Broad but Thin -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,652 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 Juli 2015\par 05:13\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2015, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 0313 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] The Nikkei extends its early gains, closing the morning session up 1.4% at 20384.79. Volume is average, however, totaling just 1.1 billion shares, reflecting a lack of enthusiasm. "Recent dips in the stock market have been punctuated afterwards by a slew of bargain-buying, but today investors are cautious," says an equity trading director at a foreign brokerage. "There is some exchange-traded-fund buying, but most of the orders from overseas are on the sell side; European investors look poised to sell into any rallies at this point." Among Nikkei leaders, \uc2 KDDI\uc2  (9433.TO) is up 2.1% at Y2,962 and SoftBank (9984.TO) is up 1.1% at Y7,136, after positive 1Q earnings previews from brokerages. (bradford.frischkorn@wsj.com) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: TAG \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 0307 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] Sport-utility vehicles and entry-level premium cars are the bright spots in the otherwise lackluster Chinese auto market, says Bernstein Research analyst Robin Zhu. Registration data show that China's new car registrations fell 6.3% year-on-year in May, with 21 of 31 provinces and municipalities on the mainland showing negative growth, says Mr. Zhu. Still, the SUV and entry-level premium segments continue to rise, with SUV registrations up 21.4% year-on-year in May while registrations of entry-level premium cars are up 9.7%. New-car registrations, considered a proxy for auto sales, underline retail demand. (rose.yu@wsj.com) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: ALP \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0307 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] USD/MYR has come off its 16.5-year high of 3.8150 notched earlier Tuesday, as punters take profit on long-USD positions on speculation that the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Malaysia central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  may be \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to buoy the ringgit. Onshore sources said that agents of \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank Negara Malaysia\f28 \uc2 \f2  may have sold U.S. dollars at 3.8150 and thus capped USD/MYR. The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  typically \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervenes\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the market when the ringgit is deemed to have fallen or gained excessively within a short span of time, in order to quell the move, rather than to reverse it. The \f28 \uc2 \f2 Malaysia \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  chief last week said that the ringgit's weakness is temporary and due to external factors. The ringgit is the worst performer among Asia \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  this year, down 8.4%, and weaker than when it was pegged at 3.8000 to the U.S. dollar in the aftermath of the Asian financial crisis. USD/MYR is now 3.8060 versus its Monday close of 3.8070. (ewen.chew@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: CNG \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0303 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] It is a mixed day for Asian stocks Tuesday, but Singapore's FTSE Straits Times Index manages to eke out a slight gain, trading 0.1% higher from its previous close, last at 3337.60 mid-morning. Investors remain cautious in global markets as they await some progress between Greece and its creditors on whether new bailout terms can be found, while Chinese markets remain volatile, putting traders on edge. In Singapore, Ascendas REIT (A17U.SG) rises 1.2% and \f28 \uc2 \f2 Singapore Exchange\f28 \uc2 \f2  (S68.SG) gains 1.5%. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Triyards Holdings\f28 \uc2 \f2  (RC5.SG) rises 3.8% after publishing financial results. Noble Group (N21.SG), which announced an independent probe into its accounting, early Tuesday, falls 0.7%. Large-cap losers are led by \f28 \uc2 \f2 Jardine Cycle & Carriage\f28 \uc2 \f2  (C07.SG), which falls another 2.4% to its lowest level in five years, following an extended slump after a rights issue. (jake.watts@wsj.com; @jmwatts_) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: ALP \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0301 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] China stocks opened lower Tuesday--sentiment remains sluggish and margin-call pressure acute, say analysts. The benchmark Shanghai Composite Index loses 1.5% to 3719.70, with nearly 850 companies losing and less than 100 gaining. "The pressure from margin calls remain heavy," said Qian Qimin, analyst at Shenyin Wanguo Securities. The China market is diverging, with reams of government support and money "the government is facing a dilemma as to whether save the blue-chip stocks or the smaller caps. Injecting funds into smaller cap stocks may blow the bubble bigger." Over 750 A-share listed firms in Shanghai and Shenzhen suspended trading by July 7, which analysts say are due to fear of shrinking market value as a result of panic selloff. (Yifan.xie@dowjones.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: ALP \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0235 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Traders say an auction of 10-year CPI-linked JGBs is likely to draw smooth demand despite a decline in inflation expectations among market participants. "It's hard to think that it will be a strong auction, but with stable investors such as pensions, there's no concern of it coming in really weak," says \f28 \uc2 \f2 Mitsubishi UFJ Morgan Stanley Securities\f28 \uc2 \f2  strategist Katsutoshi Inadome. He notes some negative factors for the auction, including CPI-linkers looking expensive as market expectations for inflation to pick up have faded. The finance ministry will issue Y500 billion of the bonds in a reopen of the 0.1%-coupon No. 20 issue. The result is due at 0345 GMT.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (eleanor.warnock@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: MNG \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0232 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Tocom RSS3 rubber futures are flat in slow, rangebound trade even amid sharp losses in Shanghai rubber. A trader in Singapore says Tocom is still holding up amid sentiment that rubber is oversold in Shanghai and manipulated by volatility in the broader equity markets. Benchmark December rubber trades Y0.1 higher at Y215.4/kg, after moving in the narrow Y213.4-Y216.0/kg range. (huileng.tan@wsj.com; \f28 \uc2 \f2 Twitter\f28 \uc2 \f2 : @huileng_tan) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: MNG \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0230 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Taiwan shares rise 0.5% to 9304.16, in tandem with gains in most Asian markets. A local trader says investors have calmed over the Greek debt situation; some of them expect central banks around the world to keep their monetary policies accommodative for as long as needed, which will be positive for equities. Although the mood has improved slightly, volume remains moderate and heavyweights are trading mixed, indicating that the market lacks direction, the trader says. TSMC (2330.TW) rises 0.7% to NT$141.50, Hon Hai (2317.TW) gains 1.1% to NT$97.40 and \f28 \uc2 \f2 Eva Airways\f28 \uc2 \f2  (2618.TW) is up 4.2% at NT$22.55. Largan (3008.TW), however, is down 3.2% at NT$3,515.00. (aries.poon@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: TAG \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0219 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] ANZ's June quarter quarterly survey of business opinion suggests softening in the New Zealand outlook, "but does not point to an economy on its knees," says ANZ chief economist Cameron Bagrie. Overall "the economy is experiencing a deceleration in growth and not a full-blown downturn," he underscores. He adds, however, with growth momentum slowing, and inflation pressures remaining benign "it is a backdrop consistent with additional monetary policy action." He still expects the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to cut the rate in July, September and next March, bringing it to 2.5%. (rebecca.howard@wsj.com; \f28 \uc2 \f2 Twitter\f28 \uc2 \f2 : @FarroHoward) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0217 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] USD/IDR opens lower Tuesday, implying a stronger rupiah versus the U.S. dollar, despite broad greenback strength in Asia. DBS Group Research said Monday that foreign investors are still piling into Indonesia's stocks and bonds - which is positive for the rupiah. Underlying U.S. dollar strength stemming from euro weakness and U.S. rate hike expectations has been depressing the rupiah this year. The rupiah is the second-worst performer in Asia this year, down 7.3% versus the greenback. If USD/IDR closes Tuesday above 13,350 it would activate the bullish chart signal of the Bollinger uptrend channel and could motivate speculative U.S. dollar-buying that might lead the pair toward the 13,440 top of the weekly Bollinger uptrend channel. USD/IDR is now 13,310 versus its Monday close of 13,348. (ewen.chew@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: MNG \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0215 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Shares of South Korea's two biggest carriers--\f28 \uc2 \f2 Korean Air\f28 \uc2 \f2  (003490.SE) and \f28 \uc2 \f2 Asiana Airlines\f28 \uc2 \f2  (020560.SE)--surge, taking cues from a sharp fall in global oil prices over Greece's rejection of debt bailout terms and China's stock market woes. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Asiana\f28 \uc2 \f2  is up 5.9% at 6,680 won, outperforming the broader market's 0.1% fall. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Korean Air\f28 \uc2 \f2  rises 4% to 42,850 won. "Oil price falls are good news to airlines. Subsiding fears about the outbreak of Middle East Respiratory Syndrome in the country will also provide a boost to the shares, which have fallen significantly in recent weeks," says Meritz Securities analyst Kim Seung-chul. U.S. crude futures fell almost 8% on Monday over concerns about the Greek crisis. The sharp oil-price fall nudges oil refiners to move lower. \f28 \uc2 \f2 S-Oil Corp\f28 \uc2 \f2 . (010950.SE) is down 2.2% at 61,200 won. \f28 \uc2 \f2 SK Innovation\f28 \uc2 \f2  (096770.SE) sheds 1.8% at 109,000 won. (in-soo.nam@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0213 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] BNZ lowers its NZD/USD forecasts as not only have dairy prices and business confidence declined, but the global outlook has also become much more uncertain. "NZD suffers badly during sagas that prompt risk aversion, such as Greece's possible exit from the euro-zone, and China's equity market rout," says BNZ \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Strategist Raiko Shareef. "If sustained, the latter has potential to significantly affect NZ's trade flows, not just through dairy, but also through tourism," he says. As a result, BNZ now expects the Kiwi to be at US$0.6500 by end 2015 and US$0.6400 by end 2016 versus a prior forecast of US$0.6800 and US$0.6600, respectively. The pair is currently trading at 0.6675. (rebecca.howard@wsj.com; @FarroHoward) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: MNG \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 July 06, 2015 23:13 ET (03:13 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020150707eb77000eb\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
