\par\par \b \uc2 Malaysia  \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank \highlight\uc2  Suspected of Supporting Ringgit -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 200 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 Juli 2015\par 05:07\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2015, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 0307 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] USD/MYR has come off its 16.5-year high of 3.8150 notched earlier Tuesday, as punters take profit on long-USD positions on speculation that the \b \highlight22\uc2 Malaysia central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may be \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to buoy the ringgit. Onshore sources said that agents of \uc2 Bank Negara Malaysia\uc2  may have sold U.S. dollars at 3.8150 and thus capped USD/MYR. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  typically \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market when the ringgit is deemed to have fallen or gained excessively within a short span of time, in order to quell the move, rather than to reverse it. The \uc2 Malaysia \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chief last week said that the ringgit's weakness is temporary and due to external factors. The ringgit is the worst performer among Asia \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  this year, down 8.4%, and weaker than when it was pegged at 3.8000 to the U.S. dollar in the aftermath of the Asian financial crisis. USD/MYR is now 3.8060 versus its Monday close of 3.8070. (ewen.chew@wsj.com) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: CNG \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 July 06, 2015 23:07 ET (03:07 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020150707eb770008s\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
