\par\par \b \uc2 Ringgit Revisits Its Past as 3.8000 Appears on Horizon -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,546 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2015\par 05:19\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2015, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 0418 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] Malaysia's ringgit briefly traded at 3.7000 to the U.S. dollar Tuesday, says a trader, for the first time since March 2009. The new 6-year low has evoked memories of the ringgit's pre-2005 pegged value of 3.8000 to the U.S. dollar. The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  peg was abolished in July 2005 - the same day China announced its own liberalisation of the yuan. But could the ringgit again trade at its old pegged level? The daily and weekly USD/MYR charts are bullish, with the respective Bollinger uptrend channels guiding the way to 3.7360 - the top of the weekly uptrend channel. But ringgit traders say the \b \highlight22\uc2 Malaysia central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the spot market to cap USD/MYR around 3.7000, so the rise to 3.7360 - and possibly 3.8000 in the medium term - could be a slow grind over the next few weeks. USD/MYR is now 3.6950 from its Monday close of 3.6770. (ewen.chew@wsj.com) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: MNG \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 0415 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] The USD hit fresh multiyear highs against the EUR and the JPY as market participants bet on the continuing divergence in monetary and economic conditions in the U.S., the eurozone and Japan. "It looks like investors are buying the dollar in advance of the FOMC meeting scheduled next week," said \uc2 Mizuho Securities\b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist Kenji Yoshii. While there was no specific buying incentive, the USD gained traction as option-related stop-loss buying orders kicked in, Yoshii says. The EUR/USD, now at 1.0804, briefly hit 1.0785, its lowest since September 2003, while the USD/JPY, now at 121.81, touched 122.04, its highest since July 2007.(hiroyuki.kachi@wsj.com) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: PJK \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0407 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] India's rupee falls Tuesday to a 2-month low of 62.80 as the greenback climbs broadly on continued hedging of U.S. dollar shorts against the possibility of a sooner-than-forecast U.S. interest rate rise. The USD/INR pair may be eyeing a break of the 63.00 round-figure trading barrier - which if breached could trigger stoploss-related USD-buying. The rupee has been falling since the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Reserve Bank of India\f28 \uc2 \f2  cut its benchmark lending rates last week in a surprise move. The reduced yield on the rupee, coupled with suspected USD/INR buying by the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the aftermath of the rate decision, has generated bullish USD/INR pressures. USD/INR is now 62.78 from its Monday close of 62.62. (ewen.chew@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: MNG \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0406 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] USD/KRW has reached a new 1.5-year high of 1.123.3 after stoploss orders were triggered on a break of the 1,120 round-figure trading barrier. The pair may face stronger opposition at the 1,124.0 monthly Ichimoku Cloud resistance zone. If that barrier holds by this month's closing, the chart suggests a pullback may occur. USD/KRW has spiked on the rally in lead indicator USD/JPY to a 7.5-year high of 122.02. Fundamentally, the prospect of a sooner-than-forecast U.S. interest rate rise - following Friday's impressive jobs report - has been driving the greenback higher. USD/KRW is now 1,122.5 from its Monday close of 1,112.1, implying a 0.9% fall of the South Korean won versus the U.S. dollar. (ewen.chew@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: MNG \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0356 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Japanese investors should think about making the 20- or 30-year JGB the benchmark issue as the BOJ's massive bond buying has sapped liquidity from the market for shorter maturities, says NLI Research Institute researcher Katsuyuki Tokushima. He notes times when the current benchmark, the 10-year JGB, didn't even trade. "The market should recognize it's highly likely the 10-year JGB yield isn't an appropriate gauge for long-term interest rates at the moment." He says yields on 20- and 30-year JGBs, which the BOJ has a harder time buying under its easing program, are likely a better measure of "the market's dynamism and likely more appropriate as yield benchmarks." (eleanor.warnock@wsj.com) (eleanor.warnock@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: PJK \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0349 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] The USD/TWD rises, tracking the USD's gains against most \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , says a local trader. Weak local stocks are also weighing on the local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . "Buying from foreign investors is quite active, so the greenback is still likely to advance further," a trader says. The pair is at 31.553, higher than 31.475 traded before the local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's suspected \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Monday, which lifted it to 31.520 at the close of onshore trading session, traders say. The Taiex is down 0.3%. (fanny.liu@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0348 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] The New Zealand dollar could fall further after news of an anonymous threat to contaminate dairy baby formula and other formulas with pest control sodium fluoroacetate, otherwise known as 1080. "Even though the police report signalled no evidence of contamination, the perception that a major NZ produce could become contaminated by poison by either a group or by blackmail from an individual will of course be of great concern," says OM Financial Senior Advisor Stuart Ive. The news saw the pair fall below 0.7300 "as the market absorbs the news and the potential detrimental impact to the New Zealand Dairy brand," says Ive. The pair is at 0.7282. (rebecca.howard@wsj.com; Twitter: @FarroHoward) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: DWT \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0343 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] The upturn in China's February consumer price inflation on the back of higher food prices will likely prove short-lived, with CPI growth slipping below 1% in the coming months, Capital Economics says. The research house adds in a note to clients that the 1.4% rise in February suggests downward pressure on broader prices has begun to ease. The CPI rose 0.8% in January, a five-year low. A rebound in February food inflation, to 2.4% on year from 1.1%, was largely responsible for the pick-up in the headline inflation rate. This was mostly the result of the timing of Chinese Lunar New Year. Vegetable and fruit prices rose sharply during much of the month, as is usual ahead of and during the holiday. Downward pressure was still seen in producer prices, which sank 4.8% year on year in February against a 4.3% fall in January. This partly reflected global oil and other commodities prices. (william.kazer@dowjones.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: PAB \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0336 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] \f28 \uc2 \f2 China's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  appears poised to cut the net supply of cash in the financial system this week, after providing a modest amount of short-term loans to lenders Tuesday. \f28 \uc2 \f2 The People's Bank of China\f28 \uc2 \f2  earlier offered CNY35 billion ($5.59 billion) of reverse-repurchase agreements, a popular interbank lending tool, in its twice-a-week money-market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operation\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . Despite the fund injection, a total of CNY113 billion of the so-called reverse repos will mature this week, returning cash to the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . The \f28 \uc2 \f2 PBOC\f28 \uc2 \f2  is widely expected to offer a similar amount of reverse repos later this week, ensuring that it will drain liquidity from the system for the third consecutive week following its massive pump-priming before the Lunar New Year holiday last month. (hong.shen@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KBC \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0334 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] NZD/USD under pressure after New Zealand government says it has upped security and testing after an anonymous threat was made to contaminate dairy baby formula and other formulas with pest control poison sodium fluoroacetate, otherwise known as 1080. "Whilst there is a possibility that the threat is a hoax, we must treat the threat seriously and a priority investigation is underway," said Mike Clement, deputy commissioner of police. The pair is at 0.7289. (rebecca.howard@wsj.com; Twitter: @FarroHoward) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: ALP \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0318 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] A survey showing weakness in Australian business confidence remains worrying, says Felicity Emmett, economist at \f28 \uc2 \f2 ANZ Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2 . Political instability and uncertainty about the economic outlook are feeding into weaker business confidence, she adds. "Moreover, lower interest rates do not seem to be helping. While rate cuts can lower the cost of capital, without a pick-up in animal spirits among business "the recovery is likely to continue to disappoint," she says. (james.glynn@wsj.com; Twitter: @JamesGlynnWSJ) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0307 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Hedge funds broadly posted strong performance in February according to institutional investment tracker \f28 \uc2 \f2 eVestment\f28 \uc2 \f2 , a win for an industry facing increased investor grumbling of late on its lackluster returns. "Every major hedge fund strategy posted positive returns for the month of February, with activist funds leading the industry and managed futures lagging, but still positive," \f28 \uc2 \f2 eVestment\f28 \uc2 \f2  says. Activist funds that push for changes at companies were up an average 5.4% in February as rising U.S. stocks bolstered company valuations. (mia.lamar@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 March 10, 2015 00:19 ET (04:19 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020150310eb3a000cr\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
