\par\par \b \uc2 Peso Stable After Three-Day Papal Closure of Markets -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,823 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 Januar 2015\par 02:43\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2015, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 0143 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] The peso is stable at around 44.72 versus its last close of 44.68 as Philippine financial markets reopen after the three-day closure due to the Pope's visit. USD/PHP is below the base of the daily Ichimoku Cloud support zone--a mild bearish chart signal--but is inching higher and away from the Bollinger downtrend channel at 44.61. A range of 44.61-44.77 formed by the Bollinger downtrend channel and the 20-day Bollinger mid resistance may trap the pair on an intraday basis. Although the greenback is stronger on escalating risk aversion in Asia, the U.S. dollar may struggle to rally while yields on \uc2 U.S. government\uc2  bonds stay below 2.00%. (ewen.chew@wsj.com) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 0142 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] USD/KRW is turning higher as risk aversion brews, lifting the safe haven U.S. dollar in Asia and threatening to end the bearish technical bias which was established Monday. A Tuesday close above 1,084 would nullify the daily Bollinger downtrend channel and place USD/KRW back inside the daily Ichimoku Cloud consolidation zone. Risk appetite is poor following a sharp fall in Chinese equities on Monday which could be exacerbated by a likely weak China 4Q gross domestic product report due at 0200 GMT. But sustained upside for USD/KRW may be fleeting while the yield on the benchmark U.S. 10-year Treasury note remains below 2.00%. USD/KRW is now 1,084.0 from its Monday close of 1,078. (ewen.chew@wsj.com) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0131 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] USD/IDR is inching higher and out of its consolidation range of 12,550-12,650 as risk aversion looms before China's gross domestic product data due at 0200 GMT. A weak reading might exacerbate investor anxiety sparked by China's stock market crash on Monday. A disappointing number below the consensus forecast of 7.2% could trigger more risk aversion-related U.S. dollar-buying. The greenback is likely to stay up at least until Thursday when the \f28 \uc2 \f2 European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is expected to announce another easing of monetary policy that would depress the euro. Fundamentally though, the rupiah could look more attractive to investors in the medium term as the government seeks to channel budgetary savings from its fuel-subsidy cut into infrastructure development. USD/IDR is now 12,665 from its Monday close of 12,617. (ewen.chew@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0114 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] EUR/GBP is likely to trade in a higher range Tuesday as the cross extends its rebound from the near-seven-year low of 0.7593, hit Friday. The daily chart is mixed as MACD is bearish: five- and 15-day moving averages are declining. But stochastics have turned bullish at oversold levels while a bullish-engulfing candlestick pattern was completed Monday. Resistance at 0.7684 (Monday's high, tested this morning). A breach would expose upside to 0.7746 (Thursday's high). Support is found at 0.7614 (Monday's low); a breach would tilt the near-term view negative, targeting 0.7593 (Friday's low), then 0.7403 (Feb. 14, 2008 low). Spot EUR/GBP is at 0.7680. (jerry.tan@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0045 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Malaysia's ringgit is a tad weaker at around 3.5730 from its Monday close of 3.5700 to the U.S. dollar, but could re-enter the daily Bollinger uptrend channel at 3.5760 as crude-oil prices remained soft overnight - a negative factor for the oil-sensitive ringgit. There is a growing fear that Malaysia's exports and current account balance will deteriorate sharply if oil prices stay depressed near 5.5-year lows. The six-month drop in oil prices has triggered a response from the Malaysia government, which will announce at around 0200 GMT Tuesday measures to deal with the fall in the oil prices. An altered budget and a lowered growth forecast are expected. Some traders think the worst-case scenario, based on Prime Minister \f28 \uc2 \f2 Najib Razak\f28 \uc2 \f2 's hint at "\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ," is the implementation of capital controls - which the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Malaysia central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  late last week dismissed. (ewen.chew@wsj.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0043 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] USD/CAD is likely to consolidate ahead of China 4Q GDP data (forecast +7.2% on-year) due at 0200 GMT. The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  pair is undermined by loonie demand on a buoyant CAD/JPY cross amid strong Tokyo stocks (Japan's Nikkei Average was recently up 1.03% at 17,188). But USD/CAD downside is limited by bullish USD sentiment and weaker oil prices (\f28 \uc2 \f2 Nymex\f28 \uc2 \f2  crude last down $1.23 at $47.46/bbl on Globex). Daily chart is mixed as MACD bullish, but stochastics turned bearish at overbought levels. Support at 1.1930 (Monday's low); breach would expose downside to 1.1893 (hourly chart), then to 1.1799-1.1792 band (Thursday's low-Jan. 8 low). Resistance at 1.1985 (Monday's high); breach would tilt near-term view positive, exposing upside to 1.2045 (Friday's five-and-a-half year high), then to 1.2200 (76.4% Fibonacci retracement of decline from March 9, 2009 high of 1.3063 to July 26, 2011 low of 0.9403). Spot USD/CAD is at 1.1952. (jerry.tan@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0039 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] USD/CHF is likely to trade in a higher range Tuesday. The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  pair is supported by negative Swiss interest rates; franc sales on cross trades versus major \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ; threat of SNB CHF-selling \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ; bullish USD sentiment. However, daily chart is still negative-biased as MACD and slow stochastic indicators bearish. Resistance at 0.8800 (Monday's high), then at 0.8812 (Friday's high); breach would expose upside to 0.9139 (hourly chart, near 61.8% Fibonacci retracement of decline from Wednesday's high of 1.0240 to Thursday's low of 0.7360). Support at 0.8548 (Monday's low), then at 0.8452 (hourly chart); breach would expose downside to 0.8350 (Friday's low). Spot USD/CHF is at 0.8790. (jerry.tan@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0037 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] GBP/USD is likely to trade in a lower range Tuesday. The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  pair is undermined by sterling sales on a rebounding EUR/GBP cross and bullish USD sentiment. But GBP/USD losses are tempered by sterling demand on GBP/JPY cross amid stronger Tokyo stocks (Japan's Nikkei Average was recently up 0.84% at 17,157). Daily chart is negative-biased as MACD and slow stochastic indicators bearish. Support at 1.5073 (Friday's low); breach would target 1.5032-1.5026 band (Jan. 8 low-July 15, 2013 low), then psychological 1.5000 line and 1.4812 (July 9, 2013 swing low). Resistance at 1.5179 (Monday's high); breach would temper negative near-term view, exposing upside to 1.5234 (Friday's high). Spot GBP/USD is at 1.5083. (jerry.tan@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0035 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] NZD/USD is likely to range-trade ahead of China 4Q GDP data (forecast +7.2% on-year) due at 0200 GMT. The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  pair is undermined by bullish USD sentiment. But NZD/USD downside is limited by Kiwi demand on NZD/JPY cross amid gains in Tokyo stocks (Japan's Nikkei Average was recently up 0.68% at 17,129). Data focus Tuesday also on 1200 GMT NZ GlobalDairyTrade auction. Daily chart is mixed as MACD in bullish mode, but stochastics neutral, five- and 15-day moving averages meandering sideways. Support at 0.7744 (Friday's low); breach would target 0.7701 (Thursday's low), then 0.7688-0.7679 band (Wednesday's low-Jan. 6 low). Resistance at 0.7808 (Monday's high); breach would tilt near-term view positive, exposing upside to 0.7856 (Friday's high), then 0.7872 (100-day moving average). Spot NZD/USD is at 0.7761. (jerry.tan@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0033 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] AUD/USD is likely to consolidate Tuesday. The China-sensitive Aussie is vulnerable to China 4Q GDP data (forecast +7.2% on-year) due at 0200 GMT. AUD/USD weighed by positive USD sentiment, but downside limited by Aussie demand on AUD/JPY cross amid positive Tokyo stocks (Japan's Nikkei Average was recently up 0.58% at 17,113). Daily chart is still positive-biased as MACD and stochastics bullish, five-day moving average above 15-day moving average and advancing. Support at 0.8166 (Friday's low); breach would target 0.8130 (Thursday's low), then 0.8066 (Wednesday's low)). Resistance at 0.8243 (Monday's high); breach would tilt near-term view positive, targeting 0.8256 (Friday's high), then 0.8295 (Thursday's high). Spot AUD/USD is at 0.8191. (jerry.tan@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1858 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ]--EUR/JPY is likely to trade in a higher range Tuesday. The cross is supported by positive risk sentiment as European stocks gained Monday (Stoxx Europe 600 rose 0.2% to 353.18, its strongest close since early January 2008) on prospects for further stimulus measures from the \f28 \uc2 \f2 European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . But risk sentiment can turn negative if 0200 GMT China 4Q GDP comes in below the +7.2% on-year consensus forecast. Daily chart is mixed as MACD bearish, five- and 15-day moving averages declining; but stochastics turning bullish at oversold levels. Resistance at 136.81 (Monday's high); breach would expose upside to 138.78-138.88 band (Thursday's high-Wednesday's high). Support at 135.16 (Monday's low); breach would tilt near-term view negative, targeting 134.70 (Friday's low), then 134.11 (Oct. 16 swing low). Spot EUR/JPY is at 136.42. (jerry.tan@wsj.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0005 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Oil Search's (OSH.AU) announcement that its gas-export venture in Papua New Guinea has won government support to develop the P'nyang gas resource to support a project expansion is a "small positive", says \f28 \uc2 \f2 Citigroup\f28 \uc2 \f2 , which says challenges still loom. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Citigroup\f28 \uc2 \f2  notes Oil Search and partners including \f28 \uc2 \f2 Exxon Mobil\f28 \uc2 \f2  (XOM) will likely try to develop lower-cost gas resources such as Hides first, potentially using P'nyang as back-fill. "While the market appears to be willing to pay full value for LNG expansion given its low position on the cost curve, we still see risks associated with capex, project delays, and LNG marketing which we don't believe the market is pricing in." \f28 \uc2 \f2 Citigroup\f28 \uc2 \f2  has a neutral call on Oil Search and A$7.26 price target. (ross.kelly@wsj.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 January 19, 2015 20:43 ET (01:43 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020150120eb1k00018\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205