\par\par \b \uc2 1MDB Crisis Won't Wound Malaysia Government: Eurasia Group -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 2,806 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 Januar 2015\par 08:38\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2015, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 0738 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] The Malaysian government will not be significantly damaged by an ongoing crisis at 1Malaysia Development, Eurasia Group reckons. The state-owned fund, which is known as 1MDB and is charged with developing the economy, reportedly missed a payment in December to a group of Malaysian banks on a $560 million loan, raising questions about its management. But Prime Minister \uc2 Najib Razak\uc2 's administration will benefit from the fact that the issue "is unlikely to resonate with the majority of voters in the country," writes analyst Ambika Ahuja. She notes that she expects the government to arrange an extension of loans for the fund. Looking at the year ahead, Malaysia--a net oil exporter--will suffer from falling global oil prices, but Najib is likely to move forward with fiscal reforms regardless. The implementation of a goods-and-services tax is expected in April. (newley.purnell@wsj.com; Twitter: @Newley)\par \par \uc2 Corrections & Amplifications\par \par \uc2 This snippet was corrected at 04:13 a.m. EST because the fund was misstated as 1MBD in the headline. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par   \par Editor: KLH \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0732 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] A non-binding opinion on the legality of the ECB's Outright Monetary Transactions (OMTs) from the advocate general at the \f28 \uc2 \f2 European Court of Justice\f28 \uc2 \f2 , which will be announced at 0830 GMT, and could affect the structure of any ECB bond purchase programme. However it will be several months before the final ruling is made, according to analysts at Helaba, by which time the ECB will probably have taken action with many analysts looking for fresh stimulus details at the Jan. 22 ECB meeting. (nick.cawley@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par markettalk@wsj.com \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0731 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] The slump in the euro-denominated price of Brent, and the lack of any Christmas-associated price rises for food, manufactured goods and services should result in the national measure of French consumer prices falling 0.1%, both on the month and on he year, says \f28 \uc2 \f2 HSBC\f28 \uc2 \f2 . If so this would be the first negative yearly reading since October 2009. The bank forecasts HICP at flat on-year in December from +0.4% in November. Data due 0745 GMT. gary.stride@wsj.com \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par markettalk@wsj.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0656 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] China may not be green enough for \f28 \uc2 \f2 Tesla Motors\f28 \uc2 \f2  (TSLA), says equity research firm JL Warren Capital after the electric-car company said its China sales fell last quarter. "China still lacks a popular green movement like in the West," the firm said, adding that its survey of Tesla buyers there show they bought the car because it is trendy or cool rather than for environmental reasons. That puts it in competition with other brands and with other types of luxury products, JL Warren says. The firm also cited signs of a gap between the number of Teslas imported to China and the number registered to drive, as well as turnover in Tesla's China management. (carlos.tejada@wsj.com; Twitter: @CRTejada) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0637 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] As oil markets tumble, U.S. power generators are usually pretty quick to switch their energy supply to natural gas from coal, says \f28 \uc2 \f2 Whitehaven Coal\f28 \uc2 \f2  (WHC.AU) chief executive \f28 \uc2 \f2 Paul Flynn\f28 \uc2 \f2 . But, he says, Asia's thermal-coal market doesn't face the same pressures. "The price of gas is a lot higher in Asia" and would have to fall much more sharply here to displace coal as the cheapest energy commodity, thereby prompting substitution, says Flynn. "And I wouldn't expect to see that happen in the short term," he says. Asian benchmark gas prices are around three times higher than U.S. natural gas. Flynn also doesn't think surplus U.S. coal will make its way into Asia--which would add to an oversupply and weigh on prices--even with declining freight costs. "It is just not a viable alternative," he says. (rhiannon.hoyle@wsj.com; Twitter: @RhiannonHoyle) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0633 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] There's been winners and losers from the sliding Australian dollar. For \f28 \uc2 \f2 Whitehaven Coal\f28 \uc2 \f2  (WHC.AU), which exports coal to countries including Japan, it's undoubtedly eased some of the pressure of weak energy markets, according to chief executive \f28 \uc2 \f2 Paul Flynn\f28 \uc2 \f2 . "For us, it is a benefit," says Flynn. "Australian-dollar coal is much better off than it was six-to-12 months ago," he says. Exactly how much of a boost to earnings it's providing will become clear when the miner reports its half-year results later this month. To be sure, Citi earlier Wednesday cautioned that depreciating exporter \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  may lower the cost of production, thus lowering the natural price support level for coal, and Flynn agrees it could prolong an oversupply in the market. However, he argues the surplus isn't as big as it used to be and that Whitehaven is better equipped to deal with soft prices after sharply lowering its operating costs. (rhiannon.hoyle@wsj.com; Twitter: @RhiannonHoyle) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0610 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Malaysia's ringgit has chalked up a 5.5-year low of 3.5950 to the U.S. dollar as the price of Brent crude oil fell 1.1% to $46.08 a barrel. The Malaysian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has lost 2.8/% against the dollar this year. In the last two months of 2014, the ringgit depreciated 5.9% due to oil prices collapsing. The USD/MYR psychological trading barrier of 3.6000, if breached, could trigger stoploss orders to sell more ringgit. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Malaysia\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  might \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to prevent a sharp fall in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , but according to one onshore trader, this seems less likely given \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank Negara Malaysia\f28 \uc2 \f2  has not been active in the spot market recently. The USD/MYR is now at 3.5950 versus its Tuesday close of 3.5920. (ewen.chew@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: FLS \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0557 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] The Hong Kong government's plans to start talks with joint venture partner \f28 \uc2 \f2 Walt Disney\f28 \uc2 \f2  (DIS) on the Phase Two expansion of \f28 \uc2 \f2 Hong Kong Disneyland\f28 \uc2 \f2  come just months before the scheduled opening of \f28 \uc2 \f2 Disney\f28 \uc2 \f2 's Shanghai theme park, which will be bigger and will draw tourists away from the Hong Kong resort. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Hong Kong Disneyland\f28 \uc2 \f2 , which opened in 2005, remains the smallest of \f28 \uc2 \f2 Disney\f28 \uc2 \f2 's global theme parks, and struggled in its early years partly because attendance was lower than expected. It turned its first profit in fiscal 2012, as newer attractions helped boost attendance. A large plot of reclaimed land adjacent to the theme park--that's been earmarked for Disneyland's expansion--still sits vacant. (jeffrey.ng@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0556 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] The USD/JPY falls below 117, briefly touching 116.99 on EBS amid Nikkei weakness (now down 1.8%) and tumbling copper prices, says a Japanese bank dealer. "There are no fresh cues but (the pair) also lacks buying incentives," says the dealer. Focus is now on whether the pair can stay above 117. If the pair falls well below that mark, as possible option-related stop loss orders just below 117 kick in, there may be no downside support until 115.50, the dealer says. The USD/JPY is now at 117.10. (hiroyuki.kachi@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: PJK \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The name of South Korean President Park Geun-hye was misspelled. "South Korea's Employment Goal Difficult To Meet: HI Investment -- Market Talk," at 0122 GMT, misspelled the name as Park Guen-hye.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0525 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] The plunge in copper prices in Asia is "red meat to Aussie dollar bears," says Sean Callow, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Westpac\f28 \uc2 \f2 . Callow says there is a significant risk that the Australian dollar will extend its losses to below US$0.8000 soon. Pair now US$0.8091. (james.glynn@wsj.com; Twitter @JamesGlynnWSJ) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0524 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] The Aussie's stubbornness relative to falling copper prices in the North American time zone, gave way in Asia as copper continued to plummet, says David Forrester, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Macquarie Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2 . While copper is a small weight in Australia's commodity export basket (about 2%) it punches above this weight when it comes to influencing the Australian dollar, given its importance as an indicator for global commodity demand from global manufacturing and construction activity. (james.glynn@wsj.com; Twitter @JamesGlynnWSJ) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 14 Jan 2015 04:07 ET \par Correction to 1Malaysia Development Article\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1Malaysia Development, a state-owned fund, is also known as 1MDB. "1MBD Crisis Won't Wound Malaysia Government: Eurasia Group -- Market Talk," at 0738 GMT, incorrectly referred to the fund as 1MBD in the headline.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0901 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Tumbling oil prices and spare capacity in the Indian economy should keep wholesale inflation subdued in the coming months, says Shilan Shah, an economist at Capital Economics. That would strengthen the case for the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to loosen monetary policy despite wholesale inflation becoming less important, he adds. "Today's data provide more evidence of subdued price pressures and bolster the case for the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Reserve Bank of India\f28 \uc2 \f2  to begin loosening policy over the coming months." India's official data showed wholesale prices in December edged 0.11% higher from a year earlier, after remaining unchanged in November. (anant.kala@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0858 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ]--The ECB appears be getting a favorable opinion from the \f28 \uc2 \f2 European Court of Justice\f28 \uc2 \f2 's advocate general regarding its OMT program. The opinion said the ECB has "broad discretion" when implementing monetary policy and that the program is "necessary." There's a lot of legalese in this, but nothing that would appear to handcuff the ECB in launching a QE program later this month. (brian.blackstone@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par markettalk@wsj.com \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0858 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ]--ECB president \f28 \uc2 \f2 Mario Draghi\f28 \uc2 \f2 's comments in a German press interview support market expectations that the ECB will launch QE at its Jan. 22 meeting. In an interview published by Germany's Die Zeit newspaper, Mr. Draghi said the ECB is united in its determination to deliver on its 2% inflation target, and that it doesn't have many options left to achieve this. brian.blackstone@wsj.com \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par markettalk@wsj.com \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0855 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ]--The seeming certainty of a new record low borrowing cost at Germany's launch of a new 10-year Bund might lead to weak demand. Should this be the case, there's no need to worry, says ING Bank strategist Padhraic Garvey. German auctions with a lower amount of bids than the offer size are not unusual. "Initiations can struggle in terms of bid/offer but we'd not read much into this should that be the case," Mr. Garvey says. Grey market levels indicated a yield for the new Bund at 0.51%. Results of the EUR5 billion February 2025 Bund auction are due at 1030 GMT. (emese.bartha@wsj.com; @EmeseBartha) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par markettalk@wsj.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0849 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Japan's economy, which fell into a recession in the second and third quarters of 2014, is likely to see a recovery in 2015, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Nomura Securities\f28 \uc2 \f2  says. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Tomo Kinoshita\f28 \uc2 \f2 , the brokerage's chief economist, says private consumer spending should rise steadily on the back of a decline in oil prices and the rise in real income from higher nominal wages and the sales tax hike delay. He also says private investment is likely to rebound from the 2014 fourth quarter. Exports, which started an upward trend in September last year, should rise gradually, he adds, driven primarily by the U.S. economic recovery. "On growth strategies, the government is likely to intensify its efforts after the ruling coalition won the Lower House election last December," he says. (bradford.frischkorn@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: FLS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0828 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ]--\f28 \uc2 \f2 Credit Suisse\f28 \uc2 \f2  strategists see little risk premium in the EUR price from the impending \f28 \uc2 \f2 European Court of Justice\f28 \uc2 \f2  opinion on the legality of the \f28 \uc2 \f2 European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's Outright Monetary Transactions program. A negative outcome would undermine the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by raising volatility further, the strategists said. (Christopher.whittall@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par markettalk@wsj.com \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0822 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ]--Germany is set to launch the new 0.50% February 2025-dated Bund at auction Wednesday, with a new record low borrowing cost that may well hit demand, says KBC Bank. "From an absolute point of view, the record low German yield isn't enticing," the banks' analysts say. They expect a difficult auction which they say "can only be saved" by the fact that demand tends to be relatively higher at the beginning of the year. Results of the EUR5 billion auction are due at 1030 GMT. Grey market levels show the new Bund at a yield of 0.51%. Any yield below 0.74% at an auction would represent a new record low German 10-year borrowing cost. (emese.bartha@wsj.com; @EmeseBartha) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par markettalk@wsj.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0816 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] China's government bonds are higher after a report saying the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  injected liquidity into commercial banks. The seven-year government bond yield is down 3 basis points at 3.52%. The Shanghai Securities News reported Wednesday that the \f28 \uc2 \f2 PBOC\f28 \uc2 \f2  injected 280 billion yuan ($45 billion) into the Chinese banking system through its medium-term lending facility, and lenders including \f28 \uc2 \f2 China Citic Bank Corp\f28 \uc2 \f2 . (601998.SH) and \f28 \uc2 \f2 Shanghai Pudong Development Bank Co\f28 \uc2 \f2 . got infusions of cash on Tuesday. The finance ministry also auctioned one-year government bonds at 3.14% versus an expectation of 3.23%, which also lifted interest in the bonds. But Shenyin Wanguo Securities says the MLF injection doesn't signal further easing of \f28 \uc2 \f2 PBOC\f28 \uc2 \f2  policy. "We need to pay attention to the net injection of MLFs...Tuesday's MLF is simply a renewal of maturing ones and is designed to maintain liquidity at normal levels," it adds. (wynne.wang@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0815 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ]--Russia's economy minister Alexei Ulyukayev is rather pessimistic about the country's sovereign rating. On Wednesday, he said that the probability of a rating downgrade to junk is "rather high," Russian news agencies reported. \f28 \uc2 \f2 S&P\f28 \uc2 \f2  warned late last year it may cut Russia's rating below investment grade and the revision is likely either this or next week. (andrey.ostroukh@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par markettalk@wsj.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0804 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] China's economy likely grew 7.2% in the fourth quarter of 2014, down from 7.3% in the third, according to the median forecast of 17 economists surveyed by \f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 . Gross domestic product growth for the year as a whole will likely come in at 7.3%, the same poll indicates. That would make for the lowest quarterly figure since 2009, and the lowest annual one since 1990. "Both production and investment have slowed to their weakest levels in at least five years, as China enters its 'new norm' of economic growth," says Jianguang Shen of Mizuho Securities. "We believe China is ready for additional monetary easing amid deflation concerns." The GDP data are due to be released, along with a clutch of others - industrial production, fixed-asset investment and retail sales--at 0200 GMT on Tuesday. (richard.silk@dowjones.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0803 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] The \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of Japan\f28 \uc2 \f2  will likely tone down its language on prices in a monthly economic assessment due at a January 20-21 policy meeting, people close to the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  said. First, it will moderate its "around 1%" language in a sentence that currently says the core consumer price index is rising at a pace of "around 1%,"--adjusted for a sales tax increase--to something more bearish. Second, the BOJ will change another sentence on prices that talks about the likely path of the core consumer price index for the coming several months; it currently says "The year-on-year rate of increase in the CPI is likely to be at around the current level for the time being." Making those changes may be natural considering a falling inflation rate driven down by oil prices, but such a move would add to speculation of additional easing, which many economists think will come sometime this year. (Takashi.nakamichi@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 January 14, 2015 04:07 ET (09:07 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020150114eb1e000zc\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
