\par\par \b \uc2 HSBC\uc2  Sees French Deflation In December -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,389 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 Januar 2015\par 08:31\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2015, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 0731 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] The slump in the euro-denominated price of Brent, and the lack of any Christmas-associated price rises for food, manufactured goods and services should result in the national measure of French consumer prices falling 0.1%, both on the month and on he year, says \uc2 HSBC\uc2 . If so this would be the first negative yearly reading since October 2009. The bank forecasts HICP at flat on-year in December from +0.4% in November. Data due 0745 GMT. gary.stride@wsj.com \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par markettalk@wsj.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 0656 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] China may not be green enough for \uc2 Tesla Motors\uc2  (TSLA), says equity research firm JL Warren Capital after the electric-car company said its China sales fell last quarter. "China still lacks a popular green movement like in the West," the firm said, adding that its survey of Tesla buyers there show they bought the car because it is trendy or cool rather than for environmental reasons. That puts it in competition with other brands and with other types of luxury products, JL Warren says. The firm also cited signs of a gap between the number of Teslas imported to China and the number registered to drive, as well as turnover in Tesla's China management. (carlos.tejada@wsj.com; Twitter: @CRTejada) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0637 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] As oil markets tumble, U.S. power generators are usually pretty quick to switch their energy supply to natural gas from coal, says \f28 \uc2 \f2 Whitehaven Coal\f28 \uc2 \f2  (WHC.AU) chief executive \f28 \uc2 \f2 Paul Flynn\f28 \uc2 \f2 . But, he says, Asia's thermal-coal market doesn't face the same pressures. "The price of gas is a lot higher in Asia" and would have to fall much more sharply here to displace coal as the cheapest energy commodity, thereby prompting substitution, says Flynn. "And I wouldn't expect to see that happen in the short term," he says. Asian benchmark gas prices are around three times higher than U.S. natural gas. Flynn also doesn't think surplus U.S. coal will make its way into Asia--which would add to an oversupply and weigh on prices--even with declining freight costs. "It is just not a viable alternative," he says. (rhiannon.hoyle@wsj.com; Twitter: @RhiannonHoyle) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0633 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] There's been winners and losers from the sliding Australian dollar. For \f28 \uc2 \f2 Whitehaven Coal\f28 \uc2 \f2  (WHC.AU), which exports coal to countries including Japan, it's undoubtedly eased some of the pressure of weak energy markets, according to chief executive \f28 \uc2 \f2 Paul Flynn\f28 \uc2 \f2 . "For us, it is a benefit," says Flynn. "Australian-dollar coal is much better off than it was six-to-12 months ago," he says. Exactly how much of a boost to earnings it's providing will become clear when the miner reports its half-year results later this month. To be sure, Citi earlier Wednesday cautioned that depreciating exporter \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  may lower the cost of production, thus lowering the natural price support level for coal, and Flynn agrees it could prolong an oversupply in the market. However, he argues the surplus isn't as big as it used to be and that Whitehaven is better equipped to deal with soft prices after sharply lowering its operating costs. (rhiannon.hoyle@wsj.com; Twitter: @RhiannonHoyle) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0610 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Malaysia's ringgit has chalked up a 5.5-year low of 3.5950 to the U.S. dollar as the price of Brent crude oil fell 1.1% to $46.08 a barrel. The Malaysian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has lost 2.8/% against the dollar this year. In the last two months of 2014, the ringgit depreciated 5.9% due to oil prices collapsing. The USD/MYR psychological trading barrier of 3.6000, if breached, could trigger stoploss orders to sell more ringgit. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Malaysia\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  might \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to prevent a sharp fall in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , but according to one onshore trader, this seems less likely given \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank Negara Malaysia\f28 \uc2 \f2  has not been active in the spot market recently. The USD/MYR is now at 3.5950 versus its Tuesday close of 3.5920. (ewen.chew@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: FLS \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0557 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] The Hong Kong government's plans to start talks with joint venture partner \f28 \uc2 \f2 Walt Disney\f28 \uc2 \f2  (DIS) on the Phase Two expansion of \f28 \uc2 \f2 Hong Kong Disneyland\f28 \uc2 \f2  come just months before the scheduled opening of \f28 \uc2 \f2 Disney\f28 \uc2 \f2 's Shanghai theme park, which will be bigger and will draw tourists away from the Hong Kong resort. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Hong Kong Disneyland\f28 \uc2 \f2 , which opened in 2005, remains the smallest of \f28 \uc2 \f2 Disney\f28 \uc2 \f2 's global theme parks, and struggled in its early years partly because attendance was lower than expected. It turned its first profit in fiscal 2012, as newer attractions helped boost attendance. A large plot of reclaimed land adjacent to the theme park--that's been earmarked for Disneyland's expansion--still sits vacant. (jeffrey.ng@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0556 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] The USD/JPY falls below 117, briefly touching 116.99 on EBS amid Nikkei weakness (now down 1.8%) and tumbling copper prices, says a Japanese bank dealer. "There are no fresh cues but (the pair) also lacks buying incentives," says the dealer. Focus is now on whether the pair can stay above 117. If the pair falls well below that mark, as possible option-related stop loss orders just below 117 kick in, there may be no downside support until 115.50, the dealer says. The USD/JPY is now at 117.10. (hiroyuki.kachi@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: PJK \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The name of South Korean President Park Geun-hye was misspelled. "South Korea's Employment Goal Difficult To Meet: HI Investment -- Market Talk," at 0122 GMT, misspelled the name as Park Guen-hye.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0525 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] The plunge in copper prices in Asia is "red meat to Aussie dollar bears," says Sean Callow, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Westpac\f28 \uc2 \f2 . Callow says there is a significant risk that the Australian dollar will extend its losses to below US$0.8000 soon. Pair now US$0.8091. (james.glynn@wsj.com; Twitter @JamesGlynnWSJ) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0524 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] The Aussie's stubbornness relative to falling copper prices in the North American time zone, gave way in Asia as copper continued to plummet, says David Forrester, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Macquarie Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2 . While copper is a small weight in Australia's commodity export basket (about 2%) it punches above this weight when it comes to influencing the Australian dollar, given its importance as an indicator for global commodity demand from global manufacturing and construction activity. (james.glynn@wsj.com; Twitter @JamesGlynnWSJ) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0522 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] The Australian dollar has been sold in Asia as a proxy for the weak world commodity prices," said Richard Grace, global head of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategy at the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Commonwealth Bank of Australia\f28 \uc2 \f2 . Grace said copper makes up just 2.6% of Australia's exports, which suggests the move down by the Australian dollar is exaggerated to a degree. Pair now US$0.8097, up from earlier low of US$0.8080. Day's high was near US$0.8185. (james.glynn@wsj.com; Twitter @JamesGlynnWSJ) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0521 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] USD/IDR is stable at around 12,600 versus its Tuesday close of 12,595-- which was also Monday's closing level--as stocks on the main Indonesia exchange trade around par. The low volatility may be due to the daily USD/IDR chart suggesting consolidation and the conflicting factors of overnight U.S. dollar index strength coupled with low \f28 \uc2 \f2 U.S. government\f28 \uc2 \f2  bond yields. Fundamentally, betting on the high-yielding rupiah and its bonds is a no-brainer in an environment of low global yields. But general risk sentiment has been shaky with the collapse of oil prices, thus deterring high-risk strategies involving emerging market assets. If oil prices stabilize at these multi-year lows, countries such as Indonesia--which is a net oil importer despite its use of natural coal resources--will be more attractive to investors. (ewen.chew@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 January 14, 2015 02:31 ET (07:31 GMT)\f28 \par \