\par\par \b \uc2 Global Turmoil Makes Rupiah Vulnerable -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,809 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 Januar 2015\par 03:33\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2015, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 0233 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] USD/IDR rallies even higher within the daily Bollinger uptrend channel--which now supports at 12,630--and targets the 13,000 level that is the top of the monthly Bollinger uptrend channel and also a major psychological trading barrier. Risk aversion has been driving investors away from emerging market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and toward the safe-haven U.S. dollar. Another overnight drop in U.S. stocks coupled with the rally of safe-haven assets such as gold, government bonds and the Japanese yen is ominous for financial markets. In times of major risk aversion, the rupiah is one of the more vulnerable \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  due to the perception that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is less able to defend its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the account of low \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves. USD/IDR is now 12,740 from its Tuesday close of 12,655. (ewen.chew@wsj.com) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 0229 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] Thailand's baht has been unperturbed by recent financial market distress that has boosted the U.S. dollar and other safe-haven assets broadly. USD/THB is still stuck in a technical no-man's land between the lower and upper Bollinger bands (standard deviation 1). Unless the pair ends Wednesday outside of the 32.84-32.94 range, consolidation may continue in the days ahead. Overnight risk aversion--as seen in U.S. stocks dropping again--sent most investors toward safe haven \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  such as the U.S. dollar and the Japanese yen. Generally, the baht is less reactive to external factors but if regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  exhibit bigger moves, USD/THB might get dragged along by proxy. USD/THB is now 32.87 from its Tuesday close of 32.85. (ewen.chew@wsj.com) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0221 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] The sharp fall of the ringgit in recent months--hitting a 5.5-year peak of 3.5840 Wednesday--is unlikely to taper off, traders say. The only force that can counter the ringgit's depreciation is \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  but aggressive action by \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank Negara \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Malaysia\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  seems unlikely, said one offshore trader. The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has been \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  intermittently by selling U.S. dollars in the spot market, but has not been aggressive, hence the ringgit continues to weaken. BNM may be concerned about whittling away foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves too quickly through market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 -- particularly at a time when oil export revenues are declining due to the collapse of crude oil prices. Malaysia is a net oil exporter and its trade balance is threatened by persistently soft oil prices that show no sign of rebounding. Malaysia releases external trade data at 1001 GMT today. (ewen.chew@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0218 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] The benchmark 10-year bond yield bounces back slightly after earlier sliding to a fresh all-time record low of 0.265%, following tumbles in U.S. and \f28 \uc2 \f2 German government\f28 \uc2 \f2  bond yields overnight. It is now at 0.280%. "For now, the decline in the yield is coming to a halt as the Nikkei stock index looks to be stabilizing after Tuesday's heavy losses," says Satoshi Yamada, senior quantitative analyst at SMBC Nikko Securities. The BOJ offered to buy Y2.5 trillion in short-term Treasury bills and a total of Y1.2 trillion in JGBs with remaining maturities of less than 10 years in three \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . Mr. Yamada says the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  are in line with expectations and will help keep market conditions tight. Key March futures are up 0.02 at 148.12. (tatsuo.ito@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: PJK \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0210 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] The online marketplace \f28 \uc2 \f2 Glassdoor Inc\f28 \uc2 \f2 ., which enables employees to anonymously critique their current employers and connect with potential new ones, raised $70 million in a funding round led by Google Capital and is now valued at close to $1 billion. Glassdoor has plans to go public but has not set a date, according to co-founder and Chief Executive \f28 \uc2 \f2 Robert Hohman\f28 \uc2 \f2 . The $1 billion valuation for private companies has become an attention-getting figure, though some companies have seen volatility around the number. Software companies \f28 \uc2 \f2 Hortonworks Inc\f28 \uc2 \f2 . and \f28 \uc2 \f2 New Relic Inc\f28 \uc2 \f2 . last month lowered their valuations to well below $1 billion prior to going public, but were able to recover once they started trading. They now have market caps of $1.08 billion and $1.72 billion respectively. (deborah.gage@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0207 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Malaysia's ringgit falls for a fourth straight day as the collapse of crude oil--which fell below $48/barrel overnight after breaking the psychological barrier of $50/barrel the day before--worsens. The impact of falling oil prices on Malaysia's trade balance--due to its oil exports--is negative for the ringgit. USD/MYR may now be targeting the 3.6000 round-figure trading barrier. That's a level last seen in July 2009 which, if breached, could trigger more buying of U.S. dollars due to stop-loss orders being activated. Traders say the Malaysian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has been \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  intermittently by selling U.S. dollars in the spot market, but has not been aggressive, hence the ringgit continues to weaken. USD/MYR is now 3.5825 from its Tuesday close of 3.5555. (ewen.chew@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0203 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] USD/SGD has a bullish short-term technical bias as long as it stays above 1.3293 and thereby inside the daily Bollinger uptrend channel. The immediate target for USD/SGD is the top of the channel at 1.3383 followed by the round-figure trading barrier of 1.3400. The overnight continuation of risk aversion--which saw more investors sell stocks for safer assets such as government bonds--has led to a broadly stronger U.S. dollar. The greenback is likely to rise in Asia despite the overnight fall in \f28 \uc2 \f2 U.S. government\f28 \uc2 \f2  bond yields--triggered by safe-haven buying--because the dollar is still the top safe-haven asset among Asia investors. But the pace of the dollar's rise may be dampened now that the yield on the benchmark 10-year \f28 \uc2 \f2 U.S. Treasury\f28 \uc2 \f2  bond is below 2.00%. USD/SGD is now 1.3365 from its Tuesday close of 1.3327. (ewen.chew@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 USD/KRW's Tuesday close was 1,098.8 "Conflicting Factors Trap USD/KRW in Narrow Range," at 0146 GMT, misstated in the last line that the USD/KRW pair closed on Monday at 1,098.8. In fact the pair closed on Tuesday at that level.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0146 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] USD/KRW may linger around within a 1,095-1,102 range as the conflicting factors of risk aversion and the fall in \f28 \uc2 \f2 U.S. government\f28 \uc2 \f2  bond yields keep the U.S. dollar in limbo. Generally, risk aversion sends investors away from emerging market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and toward the safe haven U.S. dollar, but the depressed yield on the 10-year \f28 \uc2 \f2 U.S. Treasury\f28 \uc2 \f2  note--due to investors buying more bonds overnight--is a negative factor for the greenback. If USD/KRW ends the day below 1,095, the daily Bollinger downtrend channel would be activated and could encourage short-term USD/KRW selling. USD/KRW is now 1,100.9--its Tuesday close was 1,098.8. (ewen.chew@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Corrections and Amplifications\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 This item was corrected at 02:01 GMT. The original misstated in the last line that the USD/KRW pair closed on Monday at 1,098.8. In fact the pair closed on Tuesday at that level.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0146 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] USD/KRW may linger around within a 1,095-1,102 range as the conflicting factors of risk aversion and the fall in \f28 \uc2 \f2 U.S. government\f28 \uc2 \f2  bond yields keep the U.S. dollar in limbo. Generally, risk aversion sends investors away from emerging market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and toward the safe haven U.S. dollar, but the depressed yield on the 10-year \f28 \uc2 \f2 U.S. Treasury\f28 \uc2 \f2  note--due to investors buying more bonds overnight--is a negative factor for the greenback. If USD/KRW ends the day below 1,095, the daily Bollinger downtrend channel would be activated and could encourage short-term USD/KRW selling. USD/KRW is now 1,100.9--its Monday close was 1,098.8. (ewen.chew@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0137 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Amid a global falloff in long-term interest rates, the decline in Japan's bond yields remains relatively moderate compared with falls elsewhere, says \f28 \uc2 \f2 J.P. Morgan\f28 \uc2 \f2  in a morning note. The narrowing long-term interest rate gaps between Japan and many major countries is providing "a factor helping support the JPY, in addition to risk-off developments" in financial markets that are fueling safe-haven buying of the Japanese unit. \f28 \uc2 \f2 J.P. Morgan\f28 \uc2 \f2  says global stock market weakness has sent the stocks of each nation close to their risk-off lows in mid-December. "We would like to monitor whether the stock indexes of each nation fall below these levels to gauge how long ongoing risk aversion will persist." (hiroyuki.kachi@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: PJK \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0132 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] The resurgent U.S. dollar is not good news for Asian companies who have greenback denominated bank loans. As the U.S. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  appreciates, it makes it harder to service debt based on a local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  revenue stream. While a lot of the risk will have been hedged in advance and analysts play down any comparisons with the 1997 Asia crisis, there will still likely be billions of loans under pressure, weighed further by slower economic growth in the region, experts say. "A stronger U.S. dollar and higher U.S. rates would make it more difficult for corporate borrowers to service U.S. dollar bank debt from local revenues and to roll over maturing debt," said \f28 \uc2 \f2 Hung Tran\f28 \uc2 \f2 , executive managing director at the Washington, D.C.-based \f28 \uc2 \f2 Institute of International Finance\f28 \uc2 \f2 , a banking trade group. (enda.curran@wsj.com; \f28 \uc2 \f2 Twitter\f28 \uc2 \f2 : @endacurran) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0124 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] The AUD/NZD cross resumed its two-month long downtrend from the October 31 high of 1.1303 Tuesday, hitting a post-float low of 1.0383, as a 3.6% rise in Fonterra's GDT Price Index at the overnight GlobalDairyTrade auction boosted demand for the New Zealand \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . The weekly AUD/NZD chart is bearish with the five- and 15-week moving averages declining, suggesting there's scope for the cross to decline to the psychological 1.0000 in coming weeks. Resistance is at Monday's reaction high of 1.0568. Spot AUD/NZD is at 1.0404 versus 1.0415 late Tuesday. (jerry.tan@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 January 06, 2015 21:33 ET (02:33 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020150107eb17000e8\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
