\par\par \b \uc2 EM ASIA  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2 -Ringgit near 5-yr low as oil plunges; Asia  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  down in Nov\b0\par\par\uc2 805 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 November 2014\par 06:07\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2014 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par * Ringgit may head to 3.4295/dollar\par     * \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Malaysia intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  spotted to curb ringgit loss-traders\par     * Won to see longest monthly losing streak since 2008 crisis\par \par  (Adds text, updates prices)\par     By Jongwoo Cheon\par     SINGAPORE, Nov 28 (Reuters) - Malaysia's ringgit led losses\par among emerging Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , hitting a near five-year low on\par Friday as oil prices tumbled, while regional units were poised\par to suffer depreciation in November.\par     The ringgit lost as much as 0.8 percent to 3.3720\par per dollar, its weakest since March 2010, as offshore funds\par dumped the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  on fears that lower oil prices may hurt\par Malaysia, a net oil exporter and major palm oil producer.\par     Brent crude fell close to a four-year low touched in\par the previous session after \f28 \uc2 \f2 OPEC\f28 \uc2 \f2  decided not to cut oil output to\par support prices. \par     "The sharp drop in oil prices will weigh on the fiscal\par position and the trade balance position," said Jonathan\par Cavenagh, senior \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist with \f28 \uc2 \f2 Westpac\f28 \uc2 \f2  in Singapore.\par     "Only \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  stands in the way of further\par USD/MYR strength."\par     \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Malaysia\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  was spotted \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to limit the\par ringgit's losses, traders said. Still, spot ringgit is seen\par weakening to 3.4295, the 61.8 percent Fibonacci retracement of\par its appreciation from 2009 to 2011, analysts said.\par     The country narrowed the fiscal deficit to 3.9 percent of\par gross domestic product in 2013, and Prime Minister \f28 \uc2 \f2 Najib Razak\f28 \uc2 \f2 \par wants to further trim the gap to 3.5 percent this year and to 3\par percent in 2015, heading toward a balanced budget by 2020.\par     Malaysia's exports in September rose 2.0 percent from a year\par earlier, missing market expectations, government data showed on\par Nov. 7. \par     The ringgit was the No. 3 worst-performing emerging Asian\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  with a 2.8 percent loss against the dollar so far this\par year, according to Thomson Reuters data.\par     "We remain unconstructive on \f28 \uc2 \f2 MYR\f28 \uc2 \f2 ," said Sacha Tihanyi, a\par senior \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist for Scotiabank, said in a research\par note.\par     "Though USDMYR has traded through our end of year target of\par 3.30 rather rapidly in November, we continue to see further\par weakness and will likely be revising up our USDMYR forecasts for\par 2015 and 2016."\par     South Korea's won and Taiwan's dollar \par underperformed the ringgit on concerns that a weaker yen \par will reduce competitiveness of those countries' exporters.\par     \par     WON DOWN FOR 3RD MONTH\par     The won has fallen 3.5 percent so far in November, a third\par consecutive monthly depreciation and the longest monthly losing\par streak since the November 2008 global credit crisis, Thomson\par Reuters data shows.\par     Adding to the gloomy outlook, South Korea's industrial\par output in October unexpectedly fell, data showed on Friday.\par  \par     The South Korean \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  led a monthly slide in emerging\par Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par     "Given very weak October data and our expectation of a fall\par in November exports, we cannot rule out the possibility of a\par 25bp rate cut on 11 December, though this is not our base case,"\par Nomura economist Young Sun Kwon wrote in a research note.\par     The ringgit has lost 2.4 percent so far in November, which\par would also be a third straight monthly slide. The Taiwan dollar\par has eased 1.5 percent.\par     Singapore's dollar has fallen 1.3 percent, which\par would be a fifth consecutive monthly depreciation, the longest\par monthly losing spree since March 2013.\par     The Indonesian rupiah has lost 1.0 percent, while\par the Indian rupee has slid 0.9 percent.\par     The Chinese yuan has fallen 0.6 percent, which\par would be the largest monthly loss since April.\par \par     \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CURRENCIES\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  VS U.S. DOLLAR\par     Change on the day at 0425 GMT\par   \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid   Previous day    Pct Move\par   Japan yen       118.29         117.79       -0.42\par   Sing dlr        1.3026         1.2980       -0.35\par   Taiwan dlr      30.948         30.951       +0.01\par   Korean won     1107.30        1098.40       -0.80\par   Baht             32.80          32.78       -0.05\par   Peso             44.89          44.85       -0.09\par   Rupiah        12200.00       12176.00       -0.20\par   Rupee            61.89          61.88       -0.02\par   Ringgit         3.3700         3.3465       -0.70\par   Yuan            6.1479         6.1392       -0.14\par  \par   Change so far in 2014\par   \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid  End prev year    Pct Move\par   Japan yen       118.29         105.28      -11