\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Malaysia FX Intervention \highlight\uc2  Likely 'Limited': AmResearch -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,646 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 September 2014\par 08:43\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0643 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] AmResearch expects "limited \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 " from \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Malaysia\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market, noting that the \f28 \uc2 \f2 MYR\f28 \uc2 \f2  has been fluctuating in an "orderly manner" though it fell 0.6% against the USD Tuesday. AmResearch economist Patricia Oh notes that Malaysia's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves only increased 0.1% from end-July despite the relatively strong \f28 \uc2 \f2 MYR\f28 \uc2 \f2  during August, suggesting that its movement in recent months was mainly driven by markets forces following July's interest rate increase. "Going forth, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank Negara Malaysia\f28 \uc2 \f2  will continue to maintain a commendable reserves level to ensure an orderly fluctuation of the ringgit," Oh says, adding that inflows through trade could have shown a slight increase in August as overall reserves picked up during the month. (jason.ng@wsj.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0641 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ]--US Treasurys continue to sell-off ahead of next week's FOMC meeting with the 10-year back at levels last seen at the start of August. This prevailing soft tone has already cast a shadow over the Bund market, and, with both 10-year and 30-year \uc2 UST\uc2  supply still ahead this week, "we look for USTs to remain on the back foot, at least in today's session," says \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2 . 10-year Bunds yield 0.997% and 10-year USTs yield 2.505%. (nick.cawley@wsj.com) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par markettalk@wsj.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0640 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Australia's S&P/ASX 200 closed down 0.6% at 5574.3, with a limited recovery from a four-week low of 5557.4, as Asian markets were under pressure after the S&P 500 fell 0.7% on concern that U.S. interest rates could rise earlier than expected. Low-interest-rate plays underperformed, as \f28 \uc2 \f2 Telstra\f28 \uc2 \f2  (TLS.AU), Scentre Group (SCG.AU), Westfield (WFC.AU) and \f28 \uc2 \f2 Transurban\f28 \uc2 \f2  (TCL.AU) are all down. Banks are surprisingly resilient given the pressure on yield stocks; CBA (CBA.AU) is flat. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Deutsche Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2  upgraded banks to overweight from underweight. Iron ore miners suffered, with Fortescue (FMG.AU) down 2.7% after spot iron ore hit a fresh five-year low. Offshore-income earners including CSL (CSL.AU), Computershare (CPU.AU) and Resmed (RMD.AU) outperformed after the exchange rate hit a six-month low. "Medium term, the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is going down, and people should position themselves in companies that have U.S. dollar earnings and U.S. economic exposure," Macquarie Private Wealth investment adviser James Rosenberg says. (david.rogers@wsj.com; Twitter: @DavidRogersWSJ)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0632 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Foxconn's plans to build factories in Indonesia remain stalled over unrealistic demands from the Taiwanese electronics assembler, Indonesia's finance minister says in an interview with The Wall Street Journal. Foxconn, formally \f28 \uc2 \f2 Hon Hai Precision Industry Co\f28 \uc2 \f2 . (2317.TW), is "used to doing business in China" where it can get land for free, Chatib Basri says in an interview. "Here you cannot do it, because this is not" the government's land, he says. He says other sticking points include differences over the length of a tax holiday and the imposition of import protections. Foxconn has been eyeing investment in Indonesia for years, and analysts say it may be likelier to make a move once President-elect Joko Widodo, a former entrepreneur who has won kudos from Foxconn's CEO, takes power in October. (ben.otto@wsj.com; @benottoWSJ)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0629 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Despite sitting on an estimated $1.4 trillion in untapped mineral resources, the Philippines has a poor track record when it comes to attracting foreign mining companies, due partly to burdensome regulation and security risks. But foreign mining companies could desert the country altogether, industry figures are warning, if the government pushes through a tax hike. Extractive industries pay to Manila about a 60% tax on revenue over the lifetime of a mine's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , but this would rise to 79% under the proposed regime, according to an IMF study, rendering the Philippines uncompetitive. "Mining companies go where they can earn money most easily," commented Julian Payne, president of the Canadian \f28 \uc2 \f2 Chamber of Commerce of the Philippines\f28 \uc2 \f2 . "It's clear to us that, under the proposed regime, mining companies will not invest in the Philippines." (trefor.moss@wsj.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0626 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Investors should stay "in the belly of the curve" on Malaysian government securities because it "has not priced in" potential increases in the country's benchmark interest rates or higher yields on 10-year U.S. Treasurys, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Affin Investment Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2  says in a note. It says most investors will likely sit on the sidelines until after the U.S. and Malaysian central banks meet this month. Some analysts expect \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank Negara Malaysia\f28 \uc2 \f2  to raise interest rates further in September, following its last raise in July, and are keeping a close eye on the U.S. Federal Reserve for further guidance about possible interest rate rises. (jake.watts@wsj.com; @jmwatts_)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0619 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ]--Talks between Saudi Arabian conglomerate Algosaibi and its creditors over the company's $6 billion debt restructuring have been in a stalemate since the group defaulted in 2009, but the establishment of a steering committee marks a step in the right direction. The company and its creditors sat around the table in May and with the steering committee formation, hopes are revived that a resolution can be found. The family group's biggest challenge now, however, is to engage its Saudi lenders who are still not part of any discussions. "We are encouraging the Saudi-based financial institutions to join as well," said Algosaibi's acting chief. (nicolas.parasie@wsj.com; Twitter: @wsjmideast) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par markettalk@wsj.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0601 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Profit-taking in \f28 \uc2 \f2 Apple\f28 \uc2 \f2  (AAPL) suppliers, following the release of a pair of new iPhones and a smartwatch, pushes Taiwan shares to end 0.8% lower at 9357.61. But a late-session rebound in some of them, such as casing-maker Catcher (2474.TW), indicates an optimism for the new products and a stellar 4Q sales for Taiwanese component makers, a local trader says. Catcher (2474.TW) ends up 0.8% at NT$305.50 and Largan (3008.TW) finishes up 0.2% at NT$2,445.00; both stocks were down in the morning. TSMC (2330.TW) also narrows earlier losses and closes down 1.6% at NT$125.00, while key assembler Hon Hai (2317.TW) drops 1.0% to NT$101.00. The trader says an upbeat 4Q is largely priced in the Taiex's recent run-up, although the optimism will help contain the index's downside. He tips the index to consolidate in 9350-9450 range later this week. (aries.poon@wsj.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0531 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Shares of \f28 \uc2 \f2 Toshiba\f28 \uc2 \f2  (6502.TO) are up 2.5% at Y483 following news Tuesday that the firm had christened a new NAND memory-producing factory in Mie Prefecture, southern Japan. The firm has collaborated with U.S. memory maker \f28 \uc2 \f2 SanDisk\f28 \uc2 \f2  (SNDK) on the facility, investing a total of Y500 billion, or nearly $4.7 billion. "Toshiba CEO \f28 \uc2 \f2 Hisao Tanaka\f28 \uc2 \f2  has stressed that the emphasis of the project will be not on fighting a battle for market share per se, but rather on profits, which investors like to hear," says \f28 \uc2 \f2 SBI Securities\f28 \uc2 \f2  market analyst Nobuyuki Fujimoto. (bradford.frischkorn@wsj.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0515 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Indian consumers may prefer to upgrade to older versions of \f28 \uc2 \f2 Apple Inc\f28 \uc2 \f2 .(AAPL) iPhone 5S/5C, rather than to the new iPhone 6/6 Plus due to high price of the new device, says Tarun Pathak a New Delhi-based telecom analyst at Counterpoint Technology Market Research, a Hong Kong-based data tracker. He says a majority of Indian \f28 \uc2 \f2 Apple\f28 \uc2 \f2  smartphone users still use the 4/4S models and would be willing to upgrade as prices of its older iPhone 5C/5S version have been reduced. However, Pathak says, first time buyers of smartphones may prefer to take the new version. (krishna.jai@wsj.com; Twitter: @jaikrishna)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0514 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] The Tokyo Metropolitan Government should be able to invest some of its Y4 trillion reserves in the stock market, says Tokyo governor Yoichi Masuzoe at a Tokyo investment conference. "(Public) pensions can invest in stocks. However, the public funds held by local governments--and the Tokyo Metropolitan Government has a lot of money--can't do that," he said. "I'm thiking about changing that." Local governments are bound by law to invest reserve fund money that isn't cash earmarked for short-term \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operational\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  expenses in a "reliable and efficient" manner. Under the current interpretation, that does not allow for stock investment. Mr. Masuzoe says he would push for a reinterpretation of the law. (eleanor.warnock@wsj.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0510 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] After a "blazing start to the year, there's no question that the New Zealand economy has lost some of its momentum in recent months," says \f28 \uc2 \f2 Westpac\f28 \uc2 \f2  Bank Senior Economist Michael Gordon. However, he expects the 2Q GDP figures to "show that the change in fortunes hasn't been dramatic--more like a slowing from above-trend growth to something closer to trend." \f28 \uc2 \f2 Westpac Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2  is forecasting the GDP to rise 0.7% in the June quarter in "a moderate slowdown after three straight quarters of above-trend growth." However, while interest rates and the NZD will likely react to the GDP release "the general election two days later is the bigger risk event for financial markets." The GDP data is due Sept 18. (rebecca.howard@wsj.com; Twitter: @FarroHoward)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 September 10, 2014 02:43 ET (06:43 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020140910ea9a00131\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
