\par\par \b \uc2 EM ASIA  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2 -China PMI, Fed relief support Asia  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2 ; baht firms on political hopes\b0\par\par\uc2 556 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 Mai 2014\par 10:34\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2014 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (Updates prices. For midday report, double-click\par  )\par     SINGAPORE, May 22 (Reuters) - Most emerging Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par rose on Thursday, underpinned by   improved Chinese factory\par activity and after the Federal Reserve signalled it would not\par raise interest rates any time soon.\par     The Thai baht gained as investors covered short\par positions on hopes of an end to a prolonged political crisis\par some economists fear could push the country into recession.\par     But investors have piled up the most bearish short bets on\par the baht in three months, according to a Reuters poll.\par  The country's political crisis intensified this\par week after Thailand's army imposed martial law on Tuesday.\par     An unexpected contraction in French business activity in May\par was also seen as supportive for emerging Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , as it\par raised the possibility of easing by the \f28 \uc2 \f2 European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par  \par     "If the ECB cuts, more European money will come to Asia,"\par said a senior Malaysian bank trader in Kuala Lumpur.\par     The ECB will cut what little it has left of its main\par interest rate and push the deposit rate below zero in an attempt\par to stop the euro from rising and inflation from easing any\par further, a Reuters poll showed. \par     Emerging Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  have been benefiting as \par accommodative policies by major central banks led investors to\par seek higher yields.\par     The minutes of the Fed's April meeting showed on Wednesday\par there was no sign of a rate hike soon, though policy makers had\par started laying the groundwork for an eventual exit from their\par easy stance. \par     The South Korean won rose on demand from\par exporters and continuous stock inflows, while caution increased\par over possible \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  authorities\par to stem its strength.\par     \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Malaysia\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's ringgit hit its strongest in near six\par months as offshore hedge funds bought non-deliverable forwards\par .\par     Some investors bought the Malaysian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  against the\par Singapore dollar.\par     The peso advanced as Philippine shares \par rose 1 percent. The 10-year government bond yield \par fell to 4.160 percent, the lowest since March 18.\par     \par     \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CURRENCIES\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  VS U.S. DOLLAR\par     Change on the day at 0800 GMT\par   \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid   Previous day    Pct Move\par   Japan yen       101.55         101.37       -0.18\par   Sing dlr        1.2505         1.2512       +0.06\par   Taiwan dlr      30.165         30.216       +0.17\par   Korean won     1024.10        1026.90       +0.27\par   Baht             32.37          32.47       +0.31\par   Peso             43.67          43.78       +0.26\par   Rupiah        11515.00       11505.00       -0.09\par   Rupee            58.48          58.78       +0.50\par   Ringgit         3.2060         3.2140       +0.25\par   Yuan            6.2344         6.2337       -0.01\par  \par   Change so far in 2014\par   \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid  End pre