\par\par \b \uc2 EM ASIA  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2 -Won leads Asia  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  gains on lower U.S. yields; baht dips before court rules on PM\b0\par\par\uc2 877 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 Mai 2014\par 07:00\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2014 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par * No S.Korea \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  spotted -traders\par     * Ringgit up on offshore funds, trade surplus\par     * Taiwan dollar gains on foreign financial inflows\par     * Thai court ruling could force PM from office\par \par  (Adds text, updates prices)\par     By Jongwoo Cheon\par     SINGAPORE, May 7 (Reuters) - The South Korean won hit a near\par six-year high on Wednesday, leading gains among emerging Asian\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , as \f28 \uc2 \f2 U.S. Treasury\f28 \uc2 \f2  yields fell ahead of Fed Chair\par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Janet Yellen\f28 \uc2 \f2 's congressional hearings later this week.\par     The baht, however, dipped as Thailand's\par Constitutional Court is believed almost certain to rule against\par Prime Minister \f28 \uc2 \f2 Yingluck Shinawatra\f28 \uc2 \f2  on an abuse of power charge\par later in the day. \par     The won rose on catch-up plays after holidays.\par Traders said the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  authorities have not yet been\par spotted \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par     \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Malaysia\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's ringgit advanced on offshore funds'\par demand in non-deliverable forward (NDF) markets and a\par wider-than-expected trade surplus in March. The Taiwan dollar\par  gained on capital inflows.\par     The U.S. dollar stayed around its lowest since\par October against a basket of six major \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  as Yellen is\par widely expected to maintain a dovish stance at congressional\par hearings on Wednesday and Thursday.\par     "As it stands no radical departure from the script is\par expected by the market," \f28 \uc2 \f2 OCBC Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2  said in a client note.\par     "Coupled with net portfolio inflows, we note that a wobbly\par dollar has continued to afford relative resilience for the Asian\par units," the bank added.\par     By contrast, Asian shares fell as Ukraine's worsening\par chances of slipping into civil war dented risk sentiment.\par     Regional \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  have not been hurt much yet by mounting\par geopolitical tension there as some traders saw its impact on\par Asia as limited.\par     Some big global investors are riding out the\par stomach-churning drops seen in Russian assets this year,\par refusing to join a stampede to the exits on the belief that any\par retaliation against Moscow for its role in Ukraine will fade and\par prices will rebound. \par     \par     WON\par     The won rose as much as 0.4 percent to 1,026.0 per dollar,\par its strongest since August 2008.\par     Most of its NDFs to the greenback strengthened\par on Monday and Tuesday when South Korea's financial markets were\par closed for holidays.\par     Trade was subdued on growing caution over possible\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  authorities to stem the\par second-best performing emerging Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  so far this year.\par     Some traders took profits as Seoul shares fell on\par foreign investors' continuous selling.\par     The dollar/won's 14-day relative strength index also fell to\par 29.4, below the 30-threshold, indicating dollar/won is oversold.\par     \par     RINGGIT\par     The ringgit gained as much as 0.4 percent to 3.2400 against\par the dollar, its strongest since April 21.\par     Malaysia reported a 9.6 billion ringgit ($3.0 billion) trade\par surplus in March, higher than a forecast of a 7.4 billion\par ringgit. \par     Some offshore funds earlier bought the Malaysian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in\par one-month NDFs, traders said.\par     "I prefer to sell (dollars)," said a foreign bank trader in\par Singapore.\par     "As long as U.S. rates continue to stay low, dollar/Asia are\par more in tandem to this," the trader added, referring to overall\par dollar/emerging Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  pairs.\par     Domestic corporates such as importers, however, bought\par dollars for payments on dips, limiting the ringgit's upside,\par traders said.\par     The ringgit is seen having chart resistance at 3.2384, the\par 61.8 percent Fibonacci retracement of its depreciation in April.\par     \par     TAIWAN DOLLAR\par     The Taiwan dollar advanced on inflows from foreign financial\par institutions but the flows were slowed by softer local shares\par  - limiting the Taiwan dollar's gains.\par     Caution increased over potential \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central\par bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to cap the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's further appreciation as it threatens\par to strengthen past 30.100 per U.S. dollar, a "warning zone,"\par traders said.\par     Authorities have has not been spotted buying the greenback\par yet, the traders added.\par     \par     \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CURRENCIES\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  VS U.S. DOLLAR\par     Change on the day at 0420 GMT\par   \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid   Previous day    Pct Move\par   Japan yen       101.59         101.68       +0.09\par   Sing dlr        1.2482         