\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Malaysia \highlight\uc2  Ringgit Steadies on Possible  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,869 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2014\par 04:40\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 0340 GMT [Dow Jones] The Malaysia ringgit is weaker at 3.3105 to the U.S. unit from its Thursday close of 3.2950 after an overnight U.S. dollar rally triggered by aggressive buying from macro hedge funds. In the nondeliverable forward market on Thursday evening, the dollar had rallied to around 3.3230 versus the ringgit (implied spot rate via the 1-month NDF contract). The local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  appears to have stabilized Friday around 3.3070 due to possible \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Malaysian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . An offshore trader said the authorities were suspected of capping USD/MYR around 3.2930-3.2950 on Thursday, preventing the dollar from climbing any higher. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may now have shifted its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  level due to the broadly stronger greenback. Yet, the break of the 3.3000 round-figure trading barrier is bound to be making investors who are still holding the ringgit jittery. The dollar could take on renewed bullish momentum if USD/MYR rises past the top of the Ichimoku Cloud resistance zone at 3.3110. (ewen.chew@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0336 GMT [Dow Jones] The NZD/USD continues to hold its own "despite the fact that the past 36 hours have been almost universally positive for the U.S. dollar," says Chris Hunter, corporate dealing manager for Western Union Business Solutions. "The Kiwi is holding its ground quite well," he says. Firmness in the Kiwi has been apparent since the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  raised interest rates last week and signaled more rate increases to come. The pair is at 0.8536 and "is in a very tight trading range," says Hunter, adding the next major event for the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  will be the \uc2 HSBC\uc2  report Monday on Chinese manufacturing activity. He tips a short-term range of 0.8500 to 0.8570 for the pair. (rebecca.howard@wsj.com; @FarroHoward)\par \par \uc2 0325 GMT [Dow Jones] There's a risk of weakness in the Australian dollar if the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's historically tight correlation with Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reasserts itself, especially given the prospect of continued yuan volatility, says \uc2 RBC Capital Markets\uc2 . The AUD has been something of a liquid proxy for a number of less liquid emerging-market Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , such as the CNY. But RBC says that has broken down and the AUD has been largely impervious to CNY volatility of late after \uc2 Australia's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  shifted to a neutral bias in early February from an easing bias, causing an adjustment in interest rate expectations. Any further gains in the AUD off the back of this change should be limited, it adds. "The risk is that yuan volatility persists for much longer and spills over into the rest of Asia--and by proxy, AUD," it says. (robb.stewart@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0316 GMT [Dow Jones] Taiwan government bonds fall amid rising concerns over foreign fund outflows, says a local trader. "The local bond market is focusing on the depreciation of the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which hit a more-than-three-year low, as foreign investors are remitting their funds abroad," the trader says. This adds to worries over the ongoing protest against a cross-Strait service trade pact and the Federal Reserve's possible rate hike by mid-2015, triggering recent foreign fund outflows. The market is less worried about the government's 2Q bond issuance plan (due around 0600GMT), he adds. The most-traded five-year bond yield rises to 1.1025% from 1.0950% late Thursday; the trader tips a 1.0900%-1.1100% band for the session; the 10-year bond yield is at 1.5908% vs 1.5865% late Thursday. (fanny.liu@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0305 GMT [Dow Jones] The Indonesia rupiah, which fell 1.2% on Thursday, appears to be stabilizing ahead of the 11,500 mark, which is both a technical chart resistance and a round-figure trading barrier. USD/IDR will likely maintain its near-term bearish bias as long as it closes Friday below 11,500. \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders have were spooked into buying back U.S. dollar shorts on the unexpectedly hawkish tone of the U.S. Federal Reserve at its Wednesday policy meeting. Fed chief Janet Yellen said that interest rates could rise as soon as six months after the Fed's bond-buying program is completely removed, sparking a two-day surge in the U.S. dollar. Knee-jerk optimism for the rupiah that arose early this week from the nomination of presidential candidate Joko Widodo - which also rallied local stocks - appears to be fading for now. USD/IDR is now 11,440 from its Thursday close of 11,445. (ewen.chew@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0224 GMT [Dow Jones] China's yuan hits another lowest level in over one year in the morning, but the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's downside is capped as the yuan fixing is set at a stronger-than-expected level. The USD/CNY is at 6.2265 vs 6.2275 at Thursday's close, after hitting 6.2370, the weakest level for the yuan since late February, 2013. "The central parity is below market expectations. Many institutions expected it to be just below 6.1500 to reflect the dollar index's strength overnight," says a Hong Kong-based bank trader. The central parity is set at 6.1475 vs 6.1460 Thursday. "I doubt the 6.2370 level was an error as the pair is largely trading below 6.2300," says a Beijing-based bank trader. (wynne.wang@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0216 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/PHP has burst through the top of the daily Ichimoku Cloud resistance zone and entered the Bollinger uptrend channel, triggering two bullish technical signals for the U.S. dollar. There is a strong likelihood that the peso could soon fall to 45.47 where the USD/PHP Bollinger uptrend channel ceiling awaits. The aftershock of the U.S. Federal Reserve's hawkish tone at its Wednesday policy meeting is triggering a tsunami of dollar-buying across Asia. Overnight, medium-term investors--who typically hold larger positions--were said to be buying USD/Asia broadly. In order not to drive the dollar up too high too fast--which raises their average cost of buying the dollar--these macro funds usually spread out their orders over a few sessions. This turnaround in positioning could thus continue for a few more days. USD/PHP is now 45.18 from its Thursday close of 45.10. (ewen.chew@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0214 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/Asia is higher across the board Friday after an overnight rally triggered by aggressive dollar-buying from macro hedge funds, says a trader. Macro hedge funds apparently bought large amounts in USD/MYR and USD/KRW late Thursday via the nondeliverable forward market (which trades round-the-clock), possibly to cover existing short positions or perhaps to even turn long the dollar. The Malaysian ringgit and the South Korea won at their overnight troughs were down by 0.5%-0.8% versus the U.S. dollar. The size and the intensity of the dollar-buying reveals a shift in perspective of these mid-term investors toward the U.S. dollar. Macro hedge funds typically sit on larger positions for longer holding periods. When the time comes to reverse these bets, it may take more than one session to complete the turnaround. (ewen.chew@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0205 GMT [Dow Jones] The Australian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  continues to act as if it's walking on sunshine since the incredibly strong February jobs report last week, \uc2 Rabobank\uc2  notes. However, given ongoing developments in China, it may yet prove to be hovering in thin air for some time before gravity finally kicks in, the bank says. \uc2 Rabobank\uc2  thinks a trigger for the Aussie's fall could possibly be "a bad set of Aussie trade numbers given what iron ore has been doing of late." The fall in China's yuan to its lowest level against the USD in more than year has deepened investor worries that a weaker CNY will crimp the appetite of Chinese manufacturers for base metals. Spot AUD/USD was recently at 0.9051, which is 0.94% below the three-month high of 0.9137 hit Wednesday. (jerry.tan@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0201 GMT [Dow Jones] \uc2 UBS\uc2  is of the opinion Australian unemployment will peak around the current 6% mark, but admits this is in contrast to the rise to 6.5% some others forecast. Its analysis identifies a potential 170,000 jobs that would delivery almost 1.5% year-on-year growth to stabilize the jobless rate. Of the new jobs, it says about 20% should come from retail, wholesale and hospitality and another 9% will be in construction. The services sector, not including accommodation and food, should account for 57% of the jobs growth, it says. It points out the majority of current jobs are still in mainly unlisted small and medium-sized businesses, not the large businesses that tend to have much more highly publicized job losses. (robb.stewart@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0154 GMT [Dow Jones] The PBOC's daily USD/CNY benchmark rate was set higher again, implying a weaker yuan, to reflect another jump in the U.S. dollar index overnight. The yuan is likely to keep falling now that both the CNY and the offshore CNH have crossed the 6.2000 round-figure trading barrier. In the USD/CNY nondeliverable forward market, the widening gap between the benchmark 1-month contract and the 12-month contract hints that investors are now more bearish on the yuan. The 12-month NDF contract trades at 6.2325 versus the spot rate of 6.2288 per dollar, indicating the yuan is expected to be weaker in one year's time than it is now. Traders are pricing in mild depreciation this year versus initial expectations for 1.0%-1.5% appreciation. USD/CNY is now 6.2288 from its Thursday close of 6.2275. (ewen.chew@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 20 Mar 2014 23:40 EDT  Malaysia Ringgit Steadies on Possible -2-\par \par \uc2 0152 GMT [Dow Jones] Taiwan shares have recouped some of Thursday's losses, but concerns that the U.S. could hike interest rates earlier than expected are still sidelining investors. The Taiex is up 0.2% at 8616.72, after falling 1.1% in the previous session. Moderate rebound is seen among tech names: TSMC (2330.TW) rises 0.5% to NT$112.50, Hon Hai (2317.TW) is up 1.0% at NT$85.20 while \uc2 MediaTek\uc2  (2454.TW) gains 0.3% to NT$445.00. Capital Securities institutional-investor division vice president Andrew Teng says that as the greenback is gaining strength on rate-hike expectations, foreign institutional investors are set to switch out from Asia and emerging markets, but local funds are likely to buy shares ahead of the month-end window dressing and limit the downside. He tips the index to hover in a tight 8600-8650 band for the rest of the session. (aries.poon@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 March 20, 2014 23:40 ET (03:40 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020140321ea3l000ll\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
