\par\par \b \uc2 EM ASIA  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2 -Won leads Asia  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  losses on China, Fed worries\b0\par\par\uc2 725 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 Februar 2014\par 05:50\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2014 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par * Won down on offshore funds, stocks; exporters relieved\par     * Philippine peso lower on dollar short-covering\par     * Ringgit falls; Malaysia Jan inflation at 27-month high\par \par  (Adds text, updates prices)\par     By Jongwoo Cheon\par     SINGAPORE, Feb 20 (Reuters) - The South Korean won led\par slides among emerging Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  on Thursday, hit by\par worries about a slowing Chinese economy and as the U.S. Federal\par Reserve is seen scaling back its stimulus at the current pace.\par     The won fell on continuous stock selling by\par foreign investors and worries over China's economy as the\par country is South Korea's top export market.\par     The Philippine peso weakened on selling from\par offshore funds and dollar-short covering.\par     Activity in China's factories tumbled again in February as\par employment fell at its fastest pace in five years, a private\par survey showed earlier, indicating a mild slowdown in the world's\par second-largest economy continues. \par     The minutes of the Fed's Jan. 28-29 policy meeting showed\par several policymakers wanted to emphasize that its asset-buying\par programme would be trimmed in predictable, $10-billion steps\par unless the economy's performance surprises them. \par     "It is time for Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to see more corrections as\par China's numbers are inconsistent, increasing uncertainties over\par the economy," said Yuna Park, a \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and bond analyst at\par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Dongbu Securities\f28 \uc2 \f2  in Seoul.\par     "Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will fall further if U.S. data improves\par after the impact of the cold weather wanes," Park added.\par     Most emerging Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  have risen so far in February\par as some solid data from China earlier this month eased concerns\par over the country's economic slowdown. The U.S. dollar also eased\par on the recent sluggish U.S. data.\par     \par     WON\par     The won slid as offshore funds sold the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  amid\par continuous foreign selling in Seoul's main stock market.\par     A senior executive at Moody's Investors Service said a\par sharper-than-expected slowdown in China's growth poses a greater\par threat to South Korea and the ongoing tapering by the Fed.\par  \par     Local exporters, however, chased the won for month-end\par settlements when the South Korean unit was weaker than 1,070 per\par dollar, traders said.\par     It has the 50.0 percent Fibonacci retracement at 1,074.1 of\par its appreciation in February.\par     "The won is likely to stay weaker than 1,070 for now, but it\par will depend on how much exporters sell the dollar above the\par level," said a foreign bank trader in Seoul.    \par     \par     PHILIPPINE PESO\par     The peso fell as offshore funds and local banks covered\par short positions in the dollar.\par     Investors expect dollar demand from local companies for\par month-end payments.\par     "As long as dollar/peso stays above 44.69-71, I will try to\par go long targeting 44.85," said a Philippine bank trader in\par Manila.\par     \par     RINGGIT\par     The ringgit eased against the U.S. dollar in thin trade\par after data on Wednesday showed Malaysia's inflation in January\par hit a 27-month high. \par     The Malaysian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  also fell against the Singapore\par dollar.\par     Still, investors stayed wary of possible \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Malaysia\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to stem its weakness.\par     \par     \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CURRENCIES\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  VS U.S. DOLLAR\par     Change on the day at 0420 GMT\par   \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid   Previous day    Pct Move\par   Japan yen       101.88         102.31       +0.42\par   Sing dlr        1.2647         1.2632       -0.12\par   Taiwan dlr      30.298         30.332       +0.11\par   Korean won     1072.40        1065.50       -0.64\par   Baht             32.60          32.52       -0.25\par   Peso             44.75          44.62       -0.29\par   Rupiah        11805.00       11765.00       -0.34\par   Rupee            62.41          62.20       -0.34\par   Ringgit         3.3025         3.2945       -0.24\par   Yuan            6.0831         6.0764       -0.11\par  \par   Change so far in 2014\par   \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid  End prev year    Pct Move\par   Japan yen       101.88         105.28       +3.33\par   Sing dlr        1.2647         1.2632       -0.12\par   Taiwan dlr      30.298         29.950       -1.15\par   Korean won     1072.40        1055.40       -1.59\par   Baht             32.60          32.86       +0.80\par   Peso             44.75          44.40       -0.79\par   Rupiah        11805.00       12160.00       +3.01\par   Rupee            62.41          61.80       -0.98\par   Ringgit         3.3025         3.2755       -0.82\par   Yuan            6.0831         6.0539       -0.48\par  \par \par  (Additional reporting by Kyoungho Lee in Seoul; Editing by\par Sunil Nair)\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Released: 2014-2-23T04:50:51.000Z   \par \par \uc