\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Malaysia \highlight\uc2  Ringgit Losses Capped by Fear of  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,783 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 Januar 2014\par 02:45\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0144 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/MYR is back inside the daily Bollinger uptrend channel, indicating further gains for the dollar are likely; but fear of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Malaysia central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  may limit near-term losses for the ringgit. The ringgit is weaker Thursday as the greenback rebounded against the emerging market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  overnight after the U.S. Federal Reserve again reduced its monthly bond buying program by $10 billion, sparking a bout of risk aversion in global stock markets. The daily chart suggests USD/MYR might rally toward 3.3590 where the top of the daily Bollinger uptrend channel awaits. Onshore traders are however wary of Bank Negara stepping in to stem the ringgit's decline as the Bank are suspected of having done so earlier this week when the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  approached its current level. USD/MYR is now 3.2460 from its Wednesday close of 3.3365. (ewen.chew@wsj.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0145 GMT [Dow Jones] PREVIEW: The Philippine economy likely slowed in 4Q, dragged by destruction wrought by natural calamities, particularly Typhoon Haiyan. The median forecast of 11 economists polled by the Wall Street Journal projects 4Q gross domestic product growth at 6.0% on year, a slower pace than the 7.0% posted in 3Q. "Private consumption and public spending should remain supportive of overall growth," says Bernard Aw, research analyst at Forecast Pte. The government is confident GDP growth will be at the higher end of the 6.0%-7.0% growth target, with the strong performance of the economy in the first three quarters cushioning the weakness in the last three months of the year. Full-year and 4Q GDP data are due 0200 GMT Thursday. (cris.larano@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0139 GMT [Dow Jones] The rout in emerging markets is a bit of a misnomer, DBS says. The bank notes that the \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Turkey, Latin America and Russia are being hit, but Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  for the most part are not. "On the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  front, for example, the Turkish lira, Argentine peso and Russian ruble are down (against the dollar) by 29%, 10% and 6.5% from average Dec 2013 levels. Most Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  are off by 1% to 2% over the same time frame. In the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  world, that's practically nothing; just noise (market price vibrations), the bank says. DBS also pointed out that the Canadian dollar, hardly an emerging-market \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , is down by 4.5%, while China's yuan--the biggest emerging market \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  of all--is stronger against the dollar than it was back in December. (jerry.tan@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0135 GMT [Dow Jones] Crude-oil futures are slightly higher in early Asian trade Thursday as investors weigh Fed tapering and a rise in U.S. oil stockpiles. Oil inventories rise by 6.4 million barrels to 357.6 million barrels in the week ended Jan. 24, EIA data show, compared to an expected gain of 2.2 million barrels. "The advance in stocks was despite the opening of the southern leg of the Keystone pipeline which allows improved access to Cushing stocks. The rise in crude inventories was balanced by a large decline in distillate inventories, which declined 4.6 million barrels, more than double forecasts," ANZ says. Oil markets also have support from the U.S. Federal Reserve's decision to cut bond purchases by another $10 billion. "The decision to taper was unanimous and it shows the confidence Fed officials have towards the sustainability of their country's economic recovery," Philip Futures says. Nymex crude is up 25 cents at $97.62/bbl, ICE Brent is up 1 cent at $107.86/bbl. (eric.yep@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0131 GMT [Dow Jones] BMD CPO futures are headed for a choppy session on Thursday as investors may square off positions ahead of the Lunar New Year holiday. Market participants say negative technical cues point to more downside for the tropical oil that is used to make everything from chocolates to cookies and lipsticks. CPO's downside target remains at MYR2,505/ton, says AmInvestment Bank. Benchmark April CPO ended up MYR15 at MYR2,544/ton Wednesday. (shie-lynn.lim@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0127 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/SGD rises as the U.S. dollar rebounds after the U.S. Federal Reserve announces a second consecutive reduction in bond buying, which brought back a tone of risk aversion in global markets. The Fed's removal of another $10 billion in monthly bond purchases is pummeling stocks in Asia, where Japan's Nikkei is down 3.1%. The downturn of the Turkish lira overnight, which had recovered after emergency interest-rate hikes in Turkey on Tuesday, also added to jittery investor sentiment. If USD/SGD ends Thursday above 1.2772, the daily Bollinger uptrend channel could resume its bullish effect on the greenback and lead the way to 1.2823. A mitigating factor that could help the Singapore dollar hold up is the safe-haven status of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  relative to regional emerging-market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . If risk aversion escalates, the Singapore dollar could see more demand as investors seek stability and USD/SGD could thus be weighed lower. USD/SGD is now at 1.2768 from its Wednesday close of 1.2767. (ewen.chew@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0125 GMT [Dow Jones] Malaysian shares are lower in early trade tracking Wednesday's losses on Wall Street after the U.S. Federal Reserve decided to reduce its monthly bond buyback program further. The benchmark KLCI is down 0.3% at 1783.57. "There might be some profit taking going on ahead of long Chinese New Year holiday," brokerage house JF Apex says, and tips support at 1775. The share market will close today after the mid-day break on account of the Lunar New Year and resume trading only on Tuesday. Among the index shares, MISC (3816.KU) sheds 1.2% at MYR5.80, \uc2 Petronas Dagangan\uc2  (5681.KU) falls 11.6% at MYR30.00 while \uc2 Petronas Gas\uc2  (6033.KU) gains 0.3% at MYR23.04 and PPB Group (4065.KU) is up 0.1% at MYR15.32. (abhrajit.gangopadhyay@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0117 GMT [Dow Jones] The Nikkei is down 3.0% at 14,927.09, reacting to a sharply weaker dollar after the U.S. Fed decides to continue scaling back its bond-buying program. USD/JPY is now at 102.17 against 103.33 before the Fed announcement. "The Wednesday 2.7% market rise was overdone given prevailing global market conditions, as the turmoil in emerging markets does not look like it's close to dying down," says Yoshihiro Okumura, general manager at Chibagin Asset Management. "While the continuation of the Fed's tapering program implies higher U.S. interest rates, a stronger dollar and a weaker yen--all of which are fundamentally positive for Japan stocks--the 'risk-off' investor mood and jolt to the world's growth markets is trumping these facts." All 33 subindexes are lower, with SoftBank (9984.TO) off 5.6% at Y7,387, extending its losing streak to seven days. \uc2 Fast Retailing\uc2  (9983.TO) is also down 3.4% at Y38,010. Canon (7751.TO) falls 1.6% despite posting a net-profit increase Wednesday of 5% for Apr-Dec. Sumitomo Mitsui FG (8316.TO) falls 5% despite announcing a 28% rise in Apr-Dec. net profit. Nintendo (7974.TO) rises 0.9% at Y13,000 after announcing a share buyback. (bradford.frischkorn@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0022 GMT [Dow Jones] Worries about emerging markets could start to weigh on base-metal prices as China heads into its week-long Lunar New Year holidays that starts Friday. "Unwinding the US Federal Reserve's quantitative easing programme was never going to be easy and it has barely started and some emerging market countries are already running into trouble," Justin Pugsley, markets analyst at MahiFX, says in a report. The two big external factors as to whether recent emerging-market \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  volatility is just a wobble or the beginning of a bigger crisis will be the pace of global economic growth and China, the world's biggest consumer of industrial metals. Slowing China growth has already triggered a fall in copper prices in the past six sessions on the Comex. LME 3-month copper ends 0.2% lower at $7,127/ton Wednesday, while aluminium closes 0.7% lower at $1,741/ton after touching $1,735.25/ton--the lowest price since July 2009. (arpan.mukherjee@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 2330 GMT - Concerns over a further emerging-market selloffs are likely to continue weighing on the USD/JPY, says a senior dealer at a major Japanese trust bank. He says USD/JPY may fall to 101.50 during Asian trading from 102.32 currently. There appear to be few ways to stave off another selloff now that rate hikes from the Turkish and South African central banks on Wednesday failed to restore stability. "This trend is likely to continue for a while," he says. Meanwhile, the Fed decided to cut its monetary stimulus more, which doesn't help riskier assets. (kosaku.narioka@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 17:05 EST - A 25 bps interest rate hike in March by the \uc2 Reserve Bank of New Zealand\uc2 , absent a significant escalation of emerging-market tensions, now looks like a strong bet, said Darren Gibbs, strategist at \uc2 Deutsche Bank\uc2 . He says while the market might toy with the possibility of a 50 bps hike in March, DB views this as exceptionally unlikely. Instead, firm sees authorities following through with three further 25 bps hikes, most likely in April, June and either September or December. Gibbs adds that given the present global environment, there is no need for more aggressive action by the RBNZ. (james.glynn@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 16:40 EST - Buying the Australian dollar against the New Zealand dollar might be the trade of choice now that the \uc2 Reserve Bank of New Zealand\uc2  has decided to hold interest rates steady, said Sean Callow, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at \uc2 Westpac\uc2 , based in Sydney. This trade might become even more attractive if the \uc2 Reserve Bank of Australia\uc2  removes its easing bias at a policy meeting on Tuesday. With recent 4Q inflation data in Australian surprising to the high side, there is a strong chance the RBA will change views, ditching plans to cut rates further. The Australian dollar was trading in early Asia at NZ$1.067, up from NZ$1.0575 before the RBNZ announcement. (james.glynn@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 January 29, 2014 20:45 ET (01:45 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020140130ea1u0002p\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw145\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2175\pichgoal600
