\par\par \b \uc2 Asia  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2  Fall as Fears Stoked by Argentine Peso -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 240 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 Januar 2014\par 04:41\par \uc2 Dow Jones Newswires Korean (English)\par \uc2 RTNWK\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 0155 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] Most Asia \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  are weaker on Friday as investors are spooked by the collapse of emerging-market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  elsewhere--particularly in Argentina where the peso fell more than 20% after an impromptu devaluation. The Turkish lira fell to a record on Thursday because of political instability, slowing growth and declining foreign investment. The risk of contagion in Asia is rising as investors may decide to reduce positions in other high-yielding but risky \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  like the Indonesia rupiah and India rupee. Investors appear to be taking shelter in safe havens like \uc2 U.S. government\uc2  bonds, which rallied overnight, or simply the U.S. dollar. The South Korea won went as low as 1,077.8 against the dollar early on Friday--a 0.4% fall versus Thursday's close. In \b \highlight22\uc2 Malaysia\b0 \highlight\uc2  suspected \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is staving off further ringgit weakness while the Singapore dollar--seen as a regional anchor--is mildly stronger than Thursday's closing.\par \par \uc2  Stock markets in Asia are mostly down on the 1.1% overnight fall in the \uc2 Dow Jones Industrial\uc2  Average--adding to a sense of a broader risk aversion brewing. (ewen.chew@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; MarketTalk@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 January 23, 2014 20:55 ET (01:55 GMT)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswir