\par\par \b \uc2 USD/Asia Buoyed Ahead of U.S. Nonfarm Payrolls -- Asia Daily  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2  Outlook\b0\par\par\uc2 1,844 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 Januar 2014\par 02:07\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 The following are projected trading ranges and outlooks for nine secondary \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  pairs in Asia today: \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par           Immediate Range  Larger Range \par USD/CNY   6.0500-6.0560   6.0400-6.0600 \par USD/TWD   30.000-30.200   29.990-30.350 \par USD/KRW    1,062-1,068     1,060-1,070 \par USD/SGD   1.2706-1.2750   1.2700-1.2792 \par USD/MYR   3.2630-3.2780   3.2500-3.2930 \par USD/THB    33.00-33.13     32.80-33.20 \par USD/PHP    44.50-44.87     44.37-45.00 \par USD/IDR   12,180-12,278   12,000-12,300 \par USD/INR    62.00-62.19     61.80-62.42 \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 (Ranges are calculated using recent highs and lows, information on the placement of option strikes, and technical analysis--Bollinger Bands, Fibonacci levels, trendlines and moving averages.)\par \par \uc2 USD/CNY--consolidation lower. Overnight, the U.S. dollar index ebbed slightly, implying the PBOC's daily USD/CNY benchmark rate could also move downwards a little. This could weigh spot USD/CNY back toward the record low of 6.0500. Today China releases its December exports data--expectations are for a 4.5% rise on year according to the median forecast of economists polled. There is a possibility that the number might be slightly below this mark given the recent spate of less-upbeat economic data out of China. Later in the global day the focus will be on the U.S. nonfarm payrolls data. After the surprisingly strong private payrolls report on Wednesday, expectations of a strong nonfarm payrolls data have increased. A beat on the consensus forecast of 200,000 new jobs for December could drive the U.S. dollar index up again. Dow Jones technical analysis suggests immediate support for spot USD/CNY is at 6.0500 (record low and psychological support), before 6.0400 (psychological support). Immediate resistance is at 6.0560 (week-high), then at 6.0600 (psychological resistance), before 6.0650 (top of daily Bollinger downtrend channel).\par \par \uc2 USD/TWD--upside bias. USD/TWD remains on a bullish trajectory toward the top of the weekly Bollinger uptrend channel at 30.280, propelled by more upbeat U.S. economic data. The dollar is likely to stay elevated due to suspected Taiwan \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  as well as the increased possibility of a strong U.S. nonfarm payrolls report later in the day. Expectations have been raised for a surge in U.S. job creation after U.S. private payrolls data on Wednesday was stronger than expected. The Taiwan \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  meanwhile has been actively \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  as well, said traders. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Thursday was suspected of buying the dollar as high as 30.230--higher than Wednesday's suspected support level of 30.090. Dow Jones technical analysis suggests immediate support is at 30.000 (psychological support), then at 29.990 (base of daily Bollinger uptrend channel), before 29.800 (psychological support). Immediate resistance is likely at 30.200 (psychological resistance), then at 30.220 (top of daily Bollinger uptrend channel), before 30.350 (Tuesday's 6-month high).\par \par \uc2 USD/KRW--supported. USD/KRW is currently propped up by the daily Ichimoku Cloud support at 1,062, indicating that further losses for the dollar may be less likely. Though the U.S. dollar index dipped overnight, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders in Asia are wary of a potentially strong U.S. nonfarm payrolls report later today--which could increase the likelihood that the U.S. Federal Reserve could further reduce its bond-buying program. If, however, the 1,062 technical support breaks, the next target for the won could be the 20-day Bollinger mid line at 1,057. On Thursday, high-volume selling of dollars by South Korea exporters sent USD/KRW tumbling to 1,062 from around 1,068. The exporters may have more dollars to sell again, which would cap USD/KRW near yesterday's highs. Dow Jones technical analysis suggests immediate support is at 1,062 (base of daily Ichimoku Cloud and base of Bollinger uptrend channel), then at 1,060 (psychological support), before 1,057 (20-day Bollinger mid). Immediate resistance is at 1,068 (top of daily Bollinger uptrend channel), then at 1,070 (psychological resistance), before 1,071 (top of daily Ichimoku Cloud resistance zone).\par \par \uc2 USD/SGD--upside bias. USD/SGD is still within the daily Bollinger uptrend channel despite an overnight dip in the U.S. dollar index. The likely theme for Friday is buying of the dollar on dips to position for a strong U.S. nonfarm payrolls report due later in the global day. Expectations for an upbeat jobs number were raised after better-than-expected U.S. private payrolls data on Wednesday. Continued improvement in job creation could spur the U.S. Federal Reserve to reduce bond-buying further--which would have the effect of pushing bond yields higher and thereby the dollar. Dow Jones technical analysis shows immediate support is at 1.2706 (base of daily Bollinger uptrend channel), then at 1.2700 (psychological support), before 1.2662 (20-day Bollinger mid). Immediate resistance is at 1.2750 (top of daily Bollinger uptrend channel), then at 1.2792 (top of weekly Bollinger uptrend channel).\par \par \uc2 USD/MYR--consolidation. USD/MYR could hover around the 20-day Bollinger mid as volatility seems to be dropping for the ringgit, judging by the shrinking intraday range over the last couple of days. If USD/MYR breaks the 3.2630-3.2930 range, more directional momentum could emerge. The main factors driving the ringgit now are the possibility of \b \highlight22\uc2 Malaysia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  near 3.3000 to stem ringgit weakness, and the likelihood of a very strong U.S. nonfarm payrolls data later today that could boost the U.S. dollar. Data released Thursday showed Malaysia's industrial production data for November beat expectations even though trade data for the same month disappointed. Analysts are suggesting that manufacturing will grow further this year. Dow Jones technical analysis suggests immediate support is at 3.2630 (daily Bollinger downtrend channel), before 3.2500 (psychological support). Immediate resistance is at 3.2780 (20-day Bollinger mid), then at 3.2800 (psychological resistance), before 3.2930 (daily Bollinger uptrend channel).\par \par \uc2 USD/THB--consolidation with upside bias. USD/THB is likely to keep climbing toward the top of the daily Bollinger uptrend channel at 33.24, driven by expectations for continued improvement in the U.S. economy and the political crisis in Thailand. The baht managed to recover slightly from the almost 4-year low of 33.13 notched on Monday, as stock investors were seen buying Thai equities recently--giving the benchmark stock index a boost of as much as 4.3% for the week. \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders are bullish the U.S. dollar, positioning for a forecast-beating U.S. nonfarm payrolls report later today on the heels of very strong U.S. private jobs data released on Wednesday. Dow Jones technical analysis suggests immediate support is at 33.00 (psychological support), then at 32.80 (psychological support), before 32.72 (base of daily Bollinger uptrend channel). Immediate resistance is at 33.13 (4-year high), then at 33.20 (psychological resistance), before 33.26 (top of daily Bollinger uptrend channel).\par \par \uc2 USD/PHP--consolidation. Signs of exhaustion in the dollar's rally have appeared after the last two consecutive bearish candlesticks. But the technical indicators may be overwhelmed by expectations that U.S. nonfarm payrolls might be a huge number due to the very strong private payrolls report on Wednesday, and this could keep the dollar buoyant. USD/PHP will likely retain its upside bias on the daily chart as long as it stays above 44.37. The peso may soon experience more volatility as the Philippines \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate decision meeting on Jan. 23 approaches. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has said monetary policy remains appropriate despite inflation rising to a 2-year high in December. Dow Jones technical analysis suggests immediate support is at 44.50 (psychological support), before 44.37 (base of daily Bollinger uptrend channel). Immediate resistance is likely at 44.87 (top of daily Bollinger uptrend channel), then at 45.00 (psychological resistance), before 45.43 (top of weekly Bollinger uptrend channel).\par \par \uc2 USD/IDR--upside bias. USD/IDR is likely to be supported by the base of the daily Bollinger uptrend channel at 12,180, and could even rally later as traders position for the U.S. nonfarm payrolls report due today. The technical bias is for further rupiah depreciation, even though the rupiah had rallied slightly late Thursday after the Indonesia \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  kept interest rates on hold--which gave the local stock market a mild boost. \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders are likely to look for dollar-buying opportunities on any dip as analysts think the U.S. nonfarm payrolls could post a big upside surprise--given the very strong private payrolls data on Wednesday. Dow Jones technical analysis suggests immediate support for spot USD/IDR is at 12,180 (base of daily Bollinger uptrend channel), before 12,000 (psychological support). Immediate resistance is at 12,278 (5-year high), then at 12,300 (psychological resistance), before 12,470 (top of daily Bollinger uptrend channel).\par \par \uc2 USD/INR--consolidation higher. USD/INR is likely to inch back up above the 20-day Bollinger mid line at 62.19 as investors price in expectations that today's U.S. nonfarm payrolls data could be stronger than expected. But a sustained rally for the dollar may not materialize due to strong technical resistance overhead. The daily Ichimoku Cloud which now spans 62.30-62.42 has proven to be a deterrent to dollar bulls this week. Consistently strong U.S. jobs growth would make it more likely that the U.S. Federal Reserve will again reduce its pace of monthly bond purchases--which would send bond yields higher and thereby lift the dollar as well. Looking slightly further ahead, the India elections in May will be the next driver of volatility. Dow Jones technical analysis suggests immediate support is at 62.00 (psychological support), before 61.80 (psychological support). Immediate resistance is likely at 62.19 (20-day Bollinger mid), then at 62.30 (daily Ichimoku Cloud resistance zone), before 62.42 (top of daily Ichimoku Cloud resistance zone).\par \par \uc2 9 Jan 2014 20:07 EDT  USD/Asia Buoyed Ahead of U.S. Nonfarm Payrolls -- -2-\par \par \uc2 Write to Ewen Chew at ewen.chew@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 (This article is general financial information, not personalized investment advice, as it does not consider the unique circumstances affecting an individual reader's decision to buy or sell a specific security. Dow Jones does not warrant the accuracy, completeness or timeliness of the information in this article, and any errors will not be made the basis for any claim against Dow Jones. The author does not invest in the instruments or markets cited in this article.)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 January 09, 2014 20:07 ET (01:07 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokumen