\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  CHAT: Resilience of North-Asia  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2  Continues; Won Gains.\b0\par\par\uc2 1,578 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 August 2013\par 08:46\par \uc2 Dow Jones Global Equities News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2013 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0646 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] The South Korea won bucks the trend of falling \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in Asia while China's yuan and the Taiwan dollar bob about. The won is up on Wednesday ending stronger at 1,115.4 versus its Tuesday close of 1,116.3 as the Kospi reverses its earlier 1.2% loss to close nearly flat. China's yuan is just a little weaker at 6.1228 to the dollar against its Tuesday close of 6.1217 while the Taiwan dollar is roughly unchanged. The tale of two cities continues to play out in Asia where the countries with current-account deficits suffer disproportionately in this bout of global risk aversion sparked by fear of the U.S. stepping into the Syria situation. India's rupee hit a new low of 68.75--3.6% weaker than its Tuesday record of 66.30. Malaysia and Indonesia continue to extend their multiyear lows as they remain under pressure owing to their deteriorating current-account deficits. (ewen.chew@wsj.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0614 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] Asia has a high exposure to oil, and there are numerous channels through which rising oil prices can impact Asian economies, including worsening trade balances, weakening fiscal positions, squeezed profit margins, higher inflation and weaker domestic consumption, analysts at Nomura say in a note. They say Thailand, South Korea, the Philippines and India are the largest net importers of oil and petroleum products as a percentage of GDP, and a rise in oil prices would hurt their current account balances the most. A $10/bbl rise in oil prices would lower Asian GDP growth by 0.4% and weaken Asia's current account positions by 0.6% of GDP, Nomura says, citing the IMF. Nymex WTI crude is up $2.64 at $111.65/bbl, ICE Brent crude is up $2.02 at $116.38/bbl. (eric.yep@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 PREVIEW: South Korea's exports in August likely expanded 4.0% from a year earlier, following a 2.6% rise in July, a survey of seven economists polled by \uc2 Dow Jones\uc2  shows; imports likely increased a median 2.9% on-year in August, compared with a revised 3.2% gain in July. Daishin Economic Research Institute senior economist Kim Yoon-gee says growth in South Korean exports will likely accelerate in the second half on growing shipbuilding orders and economic recovery in the U.S. and Europe. The \uc2 Dow Jones\uc2  survey forecasts a trade surplus of $2.8 billion in August, compared with a revised surplus of $2.54 billion in July. The trade ministry is due to issue August data at 0000 GMT Sunday. (kwanwoo.jun@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 PREVIEW: South Korea's industrial production likely expanded 1.0% on-year in July, reversing the 2.6% on-year contraction in June, a median forecast of seven economists polled by \uc2 Dow Jones\uc2  shows; on a month-on-month basis, output likely grew 0.4% in July, remaining unchanged from the preceding month. \uc2 Hana Daetoo Securities\uc2  economist Kim Doo-un says the output growth likely had support from increased shipments of IT goods on new product releases as well as South Korea's shipbuilding industry picking up on Europe's economic rebound. July's output data will be released at 2300 GMT Thursday. (kwanwoo.jun@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0603 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] South Korea's year-on-year inflation likely remained at 1.4% in August, unchanged from the previous month, according to the median forecast of seven economists surveyed by \uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 . On a month-on-month basis, the consumer price index likely gained 0.4% in August after a 0.2% rise in July. \uc2 Hyundai Securities\uc2  economist Lee Sang-jae says the year-on-year inflation will likely remain subdued at a mid-1% level through the year. August's CPI data are due Sunday at 2300 GMT. (kwanwoo.jun@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0513 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] Fed tapering fears have taken a back burner Wednesday to concerns surrounding a possible U.S. military strike against Syria, although the question that has dominated markets for months surely isn't far from investor minds with a key meeting of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's policy makers just weeks away. Over at Daiwa, the firm says the Fed's verdict on whether or not to start winding down its easing program is a "close call for September" but it continues to believe the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will stick to its current program until at least October. "We suspect that officials will not have enough information one month from now to ease their concerns about the economic outlook," the firm adds, noting the impact of rising interest rates on housing activity as one area that may merit further review at the Fed. (mia.lamar@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0445 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] Australian shares are likely to rise as domestic political uncertainty abates after the Federal Election on Sept. 7, though investors may tend to wait for the Federal Reserve meeting on September 17/18 before significantly adding to their portfolios, says Martin Lakos, Macquarie Private Wealth division director. "Investors are cognizant of risks from Syria, but there's certainly no panic by any stretch of the imagination," he adds. "They are actually focusing more on what's happening domestically and what they should be doing after the Sept. 7 election. It's been a very long election campaign and I think the domestic market will benefit from a recovery in consumer and business confidence once that's out of the way." The S&P/ASX 200 index is down 1.1% at 5083.4. (david.rogers1@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 There still appears to be no end to the rupee's slide, as the dollar once again rises due to global risk aversion to a new record high of 67.63 versus Tuesday's high of 66.30. In addition to the dumping of risk assets on fear of the U.S. being drawn into the Syria conflict, the possibility of capital flight from India is also pushing the USD/INR higher. Though the government said last week there are no plans to install capital controls, India has a poor track record of consistency in its policy, hence rupee investors are still jittery and looking to take their money out while they can. The USD/INR is recently at 67.59. (ewen.chew@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0410 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] The Philippine economy likely slowed in the second quarter after 7.8% on-year growth in the first--the fastest in Asia for that period. The median forecast of 10 economists project gross domestic product growth in the April-June quarter at 7.5% on year. GDP data is due at 0200 GMT Thursday. Eternal trade weighed on growth in the second quarter, ANZ economist Eugenia Victorina says. "We expect consumption to have gotten a boost from election-related spending in the first two months of Q2," she adds. (cris.larano@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0359 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] Gold is little-changed in Asia after rising to a three-month high overnight as investors flee risky assets amid escalating geopolitical tensions in Syria. The metal may continue its upward trend in the coming sessions thanks to a combination of its safe-haven status amid Middle East instability and macroeconomic variables out of the U.S., traders say. Nonetheless, the decline in the rupee will almost certainly raise fresh doubts about Indian gold demand going into the wedding season, especially now that the impact of a weaker \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is being magnified by sharply higher gold prices, IntlFC Stone senior analyst Edward Meir says. In the physical markets, the SPDR gold fund reported another modest inflow as of last Friday and there are many more days where increases are taking place as opposed to withdrawals, perhaps an indication that fund money is finally wading back into the complex, Mr. Meir adds. Spot gold is at $1,417/oz. (yue.li@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 The NZD/USD is likely to remain under pressure as the market "is waiting with bated breath" to find out whether there will be military action in Syria, says \uc2 HiFX\uc2  dealer Michael Johnston. "The path of least resistance for it (the NZD) is lower." Over the last 24 hours there has been tough rhetoric from the U.S. and its allies on Syria, which has raised the specter of military action and seen investors move towards \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  perceived traditionally as safe. The pair is at 0.7788 vs 0.7795 this morning. Johnston says support around 0.7800 has been difficult to break convincingly, with further support sitting at 0.7750. (lucy.craymer@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0342 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] USD/MYR is a little higher at 3.3315 vs 3.3300 late on Tuesday in Asia supported by continued equity-related outflow; the benchmark KLCI is down 1.2% mainly on concerns over possible U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Syria. Comments from the \b \highlight22\uc2 Malaysia central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor on Tuesday could boost the pair towards 3.3500 "sooner than we thought," Maybank IB says. \uc2 Bank Negara Malaysia\uc2  governor says it doesn't focus on any specific level for the ringgit and \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  only when the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market is "highly disruptive and disorderly." (jason.ng@wsj.com) [ 28-08-13 0646GMT ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020130828e98s0006r\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichg