\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  CHAT: USD/MYR Little Higher; May Test 3.3500 -Maybank\b0\par\par\uc2 1,633 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 August 2013\par 05:42\par \uc2 Dow Jones Global Equities News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2013 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0342 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] USD/MYR is a little higher at 3.3315 vs 3.3300 late on Tuesday in Asia supported by continued equity-related outflow; the benchmark KLCI is down 1.2% mainly on concerns over possible U.S. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in Syria. Comments from the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Malaysia central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  governor on Tuesday could boost the pair towards 3.3500 "sooner than we thought," Maybank IB says. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank Negara Malaysia\f28 \uc2 \f2  governor says it doesn't focus on any specific level for the ringgit and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervenes\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  only when the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign-exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market is "highly disruptive and disorderly." (jason.ng@wsj.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 The NZD/USD is likely to remain under pressure as the market "is waiting with bated breath" to find out whether there will be military action in Syria, says \uc2 HiFX\uc2  dealer Michael Johnston. "The path of least resistance for it (the NZD) is lower." Over the last 24 hours there has been tough rhetoric from the U.S. and its allies on Syria, which has raised the specter of military action and seen investors move towards \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  perceived traditionally as safe. The pair is at 0.7788 vs 0.7795 this morning. Johnston says support around 0.7800 has been difficult to break convincingly, with further support sitting at 0.7750. (lucy.craymer@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0246 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] Brent crude oil prices may surge to $120-$125/bbl either in anticipation of an attack on Syria or in reaction to headlines that an attack has started, analyst Michael Wittner at \uc2 Societe Generale\uc2  says in a note. "In our base case, we assume an attack begins in the next week. If it takes longer, and there are no signals that an attack is imminent, the oil price uplift from the entire Syrian situation will start to fade," Wittner says, adding that an attack is likely within the next week, but not before the weekend. However if the conflict spills over and results in supply disruptions then Brent could briefly spike to $150/bbl, he says. The big worry is Iraq where attacks on oil installations may be moving south and disrupt the 2 million barrels a day of Basrah grade crude oil exported through the Basrah port complex on the Persian Gulf. \uc2 Nymex\uc2  WTI crude is up $1.46 at $110.46/bbl, ICE Brent crude is up $1.59 at $115.95/bbl. (eric.yep@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0223 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] USD/PHP, currently at 44.54, could be on the way to 45.00 as risk aversion shifts into higher gear following the overnight surge in demand for U.S. Treasuries as an investment safe harbor. High-yielding assets, such as emerging market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , are being dumped and money is being parked in U.S. dollars as risk aversion exacerbates due to the U.S. possibly entering the Syrian conflict. While the Philippines is not deemed as risky as say, Indonesia or India, the large speculative bets on the peso -- which accumulated earlier this year on expectations of the country getting an investment grade rating from all three major credit rating agencies -- could now potentially be reversing. (ewen.chew@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0151 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] USD/MYR hit a second consecutive 3-year high of 3.3340, topping Tuesday's 3.3300, as global risk aversion goes into full swing, marked by strong buying of U.S. Treasuries - typically considered the safest investment. USD/MYR may be on its way to the top of the weekly Bollinger uptrend channel at 3.3500 next. The greenback will likely remain well-bid despite the overnight slippage in the U.S. 10-year Treasury yield - which had a positive correlation with the dollar up till this week - simply because the flight to safe haven assets will send investors away from risky ones such as the Malaysia ringgit. USD/MYR is now 3.3290.(ewen.chew@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0148 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] Crude-oil futures extend gains in Asian trade Wednesday as tensions in Syria build up, with \uc2 Nymex\uc2  WTI trading at its highest level in 18 months and ICE Brent at its highest in six months. "While the events in Syria have little impact on oil prices in isolation, the potential impact flowing through to the rest of the region is high while sectarian violence continues in Iraq and supplies from Nigeria, Libya and Sudan continue to disappoint," ANZ said in a note this morning, adding that a larger-than-expected build in US crude inventories was largely ignored in the face of escalating Middle East tensions. The API, a trade group, said late Tuesday that its data showed crude oil stockpiles rose by 2.5 million barrels. \uc2 Nymex\uc2  WTI crude is up 71 cents at $109.72/bbl, ICE Brent crude is up 92 cents at $115.28/bbl. (eric.yep@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0145 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] Taiwan shares are down 0.6% at 7773.43, tracking losses on Wall Street, on concerns over escalating tensions in Syria, says Horizon Securities analyst Benson Huang. "There are too much uncertainties, such as the QE tapering, the development in Syria, and the impact from the government's decision to raise power prices in October," he says, but adds buying from government-backed funds is providing some support. He tips the Taiex to trade in a 7810-7760 band for the session. Hon Hai (2317.TW) falls 1.0% to NT$79.20 and HTC (2498.TW) is down 1.0% at NT$148.50; TSMC (2330.TW) rises 0.1% to NT$95.70. \uc2 MediaTek\uc2  (2454.TW) rises 0.5% to NT$369.00 after the company announced late Tuesday its merger with MStar (3697.TW) was conditionally approved by Chinese regulators. (fanny.liu@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 The Singapore dollar may weaken further, dragged by other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the vicinity, which continue to weaken against the U.S. dollar. USD/SGD is rising within the daily Bollinger uptrend channel, eyeing the top of the channel at 1.2876. Speculative traders have been buying the U.S. dollar broadly, in anticipation of further emerging market carnage, thus sending several Southeast Asia \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to multi-year lows. The Sing dollar, which notched a 1-year low of 1.2859 Tuesday, has reacted much less to risk aversion than its direct neighbors, Malaysia's ringgit and Indonesia's rupiah. But traders are now buying more USD/SGD, anticipating the pair to catch up with the rest of Southeast Asia in due course. USD/SGD is now at 1.2826. (ewen.chew@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 The dollar remains strong in Asia, despite U.S. yields slipping overnight because investors bought more U.S. Treasurys -- the ultimate safe-haven asset -- amid risk aversion related to the possibility the U.S. may enter the Syria conflict. While data Tuesday showed U.S. consumer confidence improved markedly and U.S. home prices rose -- though at a slower pace -- which suggest the Fed could indeed reduce its pace of bond purchases next month, the focus now is on dumping risky assets for safer ones, which could therefore see the \uc2 U.S. Treasury\uc2  yields continue to slip. (ewen.chew@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0120 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] Shares of Japanese exporters are leading the broader market down as the dollar and euro continue their descent against the yen, with the dollar last at Y97.15. Canon (7751.TO) and \uc2 Nikon\uc2  (7731.TO) hit new year lows, down 1.4% at Y2,959 and down 1.1% at Y1,667, respectively. "Investors see the risk in both issues, with the fall in the dollar and the euro and the pair's exposure to overseas markets for their digital cameras," says \uc2 Okasan Securities\uc2  Strategist Hideyuki Ishiguro. "The market may yet worsen for Japan shares as U.S. stocks look overbought against the background of Syria tensions, Fed tapering, and re-kindling debate over raising the U.S. national debt ceiling, which together pose a threat to stocks," says Tachibana Securities market advisor Kenichi Hirano. (bradford.frischkorn@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 USD/KRW falls to 1,118.40 from an earlier Wednesday high of 1,119.80, but is still higher than Tuesday's onshore close of 1,116.30. Lee Seung-sik, Hana Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst, says there is upward pressure on the pair due to traders selling risk \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  because of concerns the U.S. plans military \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the Syrian civil war but that force is tempered by expectations that South Korean firms will be making their usual end-of-the-month conversions of foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 -denominated assets into the won. Lee tips the pair in a 1,115-1,123 range Wednesday. (jeyup.kwaak@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0023 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] The recent copper rally may be about to run out of steam. After rising more than 6% this month, \uc2 Barclays\uc2  says it's skeptical the market's uptrend can continue. Its argument: the rally has been merely the result of short covering from a period of extreme bearishness, rather than any notable demand from market bulls on signs of economic stabilization in China. Base metals are "still facing a sluggish global growth environment with unimpressive demand trends," \uc2 Barclays\uc2  analysts say in a note. The bank forecasts copper to move into a clear surplus by 4Q13, as demand for copper cable for China's power-grid infrastructure slows and mine supply rises. "Our new trade recommendation," the \uc2 Barclays\uc2  analysts say, "is to short copper above US$7,200 a ton." LME three-month copper is trading US$1.25 lower at US$7,313.75 a ton. (rhiannon.hoyle@wsj.com) [ 28-08-13 0342GMT ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020130828e98s0002h\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
