\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  CHAT: USD/MYR Tad Lower; Watch 3.2770 Support - Maybank\b0\par\par\uc2 1,454 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 August 2013\par 06:34\par \uc2 Dow Jones Global Equities News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2013 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 USD/MYR is slightly lower at 3.2930 vs 3.3000 late Friday in Asia after weaker-than-expected U.S. new home sales data raised hopes that the Fed won't taper asset purchase program in September; "the pair is still overbought and 3.2770 is the next support to watch," says Maybank IB, tipping resistance at 3.3275 for the week. The house notes that one-month nondeliverable forwards retreat modestly to 3.3035. (jason.ng@wsj.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 USD/MYR is slightly lower at 3.2930 vs 3.3000 late Friday in Asia after weaker-than-expected U.S. new home sales data raised hopes that the Fed won't taper asset purchase program in September; "the pair is still overbought and 3.2770 is the next support to watch," says Maybank IB, tipping resistance at 3.3275 for the week. The house notes that one-month nondeliverable forwards retreat modestly to 3.3035. (jason.ng@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0331 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] The WTI-Brent spread is expected to average $4/bbl over the second half of this year, expanding to $6/bbl over 2014, \uc2 Barclays\uc2  says in its weekly energy outlook. "Opposing forces are shaping oil market fundamentals on both sides of the Atlantic, with the WTI-Brent spread reflecting this shift in balances," the bank said, adding that the factors boosting WTI crude are easing while large supply shortfalls and heightened geopolitical tension support crude prices outside North America. The house says Brent crude is also facing a shrinking pool of supplies in the North Sea implying better support in the long run. "The deep seated problems underlying recent export shortfalls from Libya look intractable in the short term and, in our view, the oil market will need to get used to increased variability in Libyan supply," \uc2 Barclays\uc2  says. WTI crude is up 55 cents at $106.97/bbl; ICE Brent is up 21 cents at $111.25/bbl. (eric.yep@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0331 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] The WTI-Brent spread is expected to average $4/bbl over the second half of this year, expanding to $6/bbl over 2014, \uc2 Barclays\uc2  says in its weekly energy outlook. "Opposing forces are shaping oil market fundamentals on both sides of the Atlantic, with the WTI-Brent spread reflecting this shift in balances," the bank said, adding that the factors boosting WTI crude are easing while large supply shortfalls and heightened geopolitical tension support crude prices outside North America. The house says Brent crude is also facing a shrinking pool of supplies in the North Sea implying better support in the long run. "The deep seated problems underlying recent export shortfalls from Libya look intractable in the short term and, in our view, the oil market will need to get used to increased variability in Libyan supply," \uc2 Barclays\uc2  says. WTI crude is up 55 cents at $106.97/bbl; ICE Brent is up 21 cents at $111.25/bbl. (eric.yep@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0320 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] The daily Bollinger uptrend channel could guide the euro, last at $1.3382, toward $1.3424. Support is at $1.3371. The euro has been firming up as economic data out of the region improves, particularly in Germany, and the \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  says there is no need for further rate cuts for now. If EUR/USD drops out of the uptrend channel - a possibility as dollar strength builds ahead of the September FOMC meeting - the euro could retreat to $1.3329 where the 20-day Bollinger mid-support lies. (ewen.chew@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0250 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] Singapore's industrial production likely grew 1.5% on year in July, helped by a lower base of comparison, according to the median estimate in a Dow Jones Newswires poll of seven economists. That compares with a 5.9% fall in June. Measured on month in seasonally adjusted terms, industrial production likely contracted 0.3% in July after falling 3.1% in June. Economists point to purchasing managers' index data from Singapore showing an improvement in the manufacturing sectors and a mild increase in industrial output could be "in line with our modest recovery theory," says Selena Ling, the head of treasury research at OCBC. The data are due at 0500 GMT on Monday. (gaurav.raghuvanshi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0215 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] The USD/CNY falls, in response to the \uc2 Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's guidance to lead the pair's fixing lower. A Shanghai-based local bank trader says corporate dollar selling may push the pair down further for the session, tipping a nearby support at 6.1100. Near-term, the pair may consolidate with a downward bias around 6.1200, the trader adds. The USD/CNY is at 6.1186, vs 6.1210 late Friday, while the central parity is set at 6.1680 vs 6.1710 Friday. Offshore one-year USD/CNY is at 6.2350/6.2400 vs 6.2440/6.2470 late Friday. (yue.li@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 USD/IDR NDF contracts continue to trade at an exorbitant premium to the spot contract, a sign that dollar liquidity in the onshore market is still thin and investor confidence is low. The 1-month NDF contract--the most liquid in the offshore market--trades at close to IDR600 over the spot price. In 'normal' market conditions, the premium over the spot price is around IDR30-IDR50. Once the NDF premium begins to decline, which traders see as a sign of improving investor confidence and a return of appetite for high-yielding assets, we could see the rupiah stage a more significant recovery. USD/IDR is now 11,380--IDR600 over the current spot price. (ewen.chew@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 USD/KRW falls for a second straight session, to 1,113.5 from 1,116.9 late Friday. Samsung Futures analyst Jeon Seung-ji says it is likely to be a directionless week for the pair amid a tug of war between month-end export settlement and a number of USD-supporting uncertainties; "Fear that the Fed could taper its stimulus measures soon, jitters over destabilizing emerging markets, and BOK's possible \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  all are providing a firm support." She tips a 1,110-1,117 band for Monday and 1,110-1,127 range for this week. (kanga.kong@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 The base of the daily Bollinger uptrend channel at 3.2850 is the level to watch in USD/MYR as the greenback retreats slightly on a pullback in the U.S. 10-year Treasury yield. A daily close below this mark could overturn the bullish technical signal that has sent the dollar up since late July. The USD/MYR slipped to an intraday low of 3.2835 earlier but is now at 3.2940. If the dollar ends Monday below 3.2870, the pullback lower may extend further to 3.2650, where the 20-day Bollinger mid lies. Traders could be trimming bullish-dollar bets, taking profit on some of their positions before the September U.S. FOMC meeting, where most expect the Fed to announce some form of QE tapering. Recent \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Malaysia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to slow the pace of ringgit depreciation has helped a little in deterring dollar-buying. USD/MYR is now 3.2940.(ewen.chew@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0137 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] USD/SGD slips to the bottom of the Bollinger uptrend channel at 1.2776 and then rebounds slightly, keeping the bullish chart signal in play. The pair could consolidate here for a bit, but as long as this key support is intact at the end of the day, further dollar upside looks likely. The greenback retreated as the U.S. 10-year Treasury yield subsided Friday, but buying on dips is expected, as the market is leaning toward a September Fed tapering of quantitative easing. Singapore releases industrial production data for July at 0500 GMT. USD/SGD is now 1.2794.(ewen.chew@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0133 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] Expect a sliding USD/KRW, last at 1,113.2, to be supported near the daily Bollinger downtrend channel of 1,113. The pair began the day's trading gapping down from its Friday close of 1,116.9. The reaction is partly due to a slight pullback in the U.S. 10-year Treasury yield Friday. Although most traders expect some form of tapering to occur after the Federal Reserve meets in September, the upside for the dollar may already be priced in to some extent. (ewen.chew@wsj.com) [ 26-08-13 0434GMT ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020130826e98q0002t\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
