\par\par \b \uc2 DJ ASIA DAILY  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2  OUTLOOK - Asia\b0\par\par\uc2 6,404 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 August 2013\par 02:48\par \uc2 Dow Jones Newswires\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2013, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE--The following are projected trading ranges and outlooks for nine secondary \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  pairs in Asia today: \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par          Immediate Range  Larger Range \par USD/CNY    6.1200-6.1224   6.1143-6.1306 \par USD/TWD    30.060-30.180   30.000-30.300 \par USD/KRW    1,112-1,117     1,110-1,120 \par USD/SGD    1.2774-1.2800   1.2750-1.2841 \par USD/MYR    3.2800-3.2870   3.2650-3.3000 \par USD/THB    31.80-32.00     31.72-32.17 \par USD/PHP          -               - \par USD/IDR    10,700-10,800   10,640-11,000 \par USD/INR    63.00-63.50     62.50-64.00 \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 (Ranges are calculated using recent highs and lows, information on the placement of option strikes, and technical analysis--Bollinger Bands, Fibonacci levels, trendlines and moving averages.)\par \par \uc2 USD/CNY - consolidation. USD/CNY looks likely to stabilize within a narrow range unless the PBOC central parity rate surprises higher, which is a possibility given that USD/JPY is climbing toward 99.00. Technical signals suggest a mild downside bias if the USD/CNY pair stays within the Bollinger downtrend channel that caps now at 6.1224. Traders have recently started speculating again that the Chinese authorities might implement a rate cut or reduce the reserve ratio requirement that banks must hold, in order to stimulate growth to meet the government's GDP target of 7.5%. Dow Jones technical analysis suggests immediate support for USD/CNY is at 6.1200 (psychological support), then at 6.1143 (base of Bollinger downtrend channel), before 6.1090 (record low). Immediate resistance is likely at 6.1224 (top of daily Bollinger downtrend channel), then at 6.1306 (20-day Bollinger mid), before 6.1387 (daily Bollinger uptrend channel).\par \par \uc2 USD/TWD - upside bias. USD/TWD appears bullish on the chart, hovering just above the Ichimoku Cloud zone which provides support at 30.060. The dollar is likely to stay in demand as a September withdrawal of U.S. monetary stimulus looms. Traders will be averse toward shorting the dollar due to the elevated U.S. 10-year Treasury yield. Taiwan industrial production data, released Friday, surprised much stronger and bodes well for 3Q growth, which could further insulate the Taiwan dollar from the selloff in emerging Asia \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Dow Jones technical analysis suggests immediate support is at 30.060 (daily Ichimoku Cloud support and 20-day Bollinger mid), then at 30.000 (psychological support), before 29.940 (daily Bollinger downtrend channel). Immediate resistance is likely at 30.180 (daily Bollinger uptrend channel), then at 30.300 (top of daily Bollinger uptrend channel).\par \par \uc2 USD/KRW - consolidation. USD/KRW may be supported near the daily Bollinger downtrend channel as the dollar opens lower Monday at 1,113, gapping down from its Friday close of 1,116.9. The reaction might be due to the slight pullback in the U.S. 10-year Treasury yield Friday. Although most traders expect some form of tapering to occur after the U.S. Federal Reserve meets in September, the upside for the dollar may already have been priced in to some extent and the greenback may hence give up some gains ahead of the FOMC. Dow Jones technical analysis suggests immediate support is at 1,112 (daily Bollinger downtrend channel), then at 1,110 (psychological support), before 1,108 (base of daily Bollinger downtrend channel). Immediate resistance is at 1,117 (20-day Bollinger mid), then at 1,120 (psychological resistance), before 1,121 (daily Bollinger uptrend channel).\par \par \uc2 USD/SGD - upside bias. USD/SGD has dipped to the bottom of the Bollinger uptrend channel at 1.2775 and may consolidate here for a bit, but as long as this key support is intact at the end of the day, further dollar upside looks likely. The greenback slipped as the U.S. 10-year Treasury yield subsided a little Friday, but there may be buying-on-dips expected as the market is leaning toward a September Fed tapering of QE. Singapore releases industrial production data for July at 0500GMT. Dow Jones technical analysis shows immediate support is at 1.2774 (base of daily Bollinger uptrend channel), then at 1.2750 (psychological support), before 1.2707 (20-day Bollinger mid). Immediate resistance is at 1.2800 (psychological resistance), then at 1.2841 (top of daily Bollinger uptrend channel), before 1.2892 (top of weekly Bollinger uptrend channel).\par \par \uc2 USD/MYR - pullback lower. USD/MYR turns down at the open, breaking through the immediate support of the Bollinger uptrend channel at 3.2870. If the dollar ends Monday below 3.2870, the pullback lower may extend further to 3.2650 where the 20-day Bollinger mid lies. Traders could be trimming some bullish dollar bets, taking profit on some of their positions before the September U.S. FOMC meeting, where most expect the Fed to announce some form of QE tapering. Recent \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Malaysia central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  did little to deter dollar-buying, though it did slow the pace of the move higher last week, which could be part of the reason traders are reducing their long-dollar positions. Dow Jones technical analysis suggests immediate support is at 3.2800 (psychological support), then at 3.2650 (20-day Bollinger mid support), before 3.2500 (psychological support). Immediate resistance is at 3.2870 (daily Bollinger uptrend channel), then at 3.3000 (major psychological barrier), before 3.3090 (top of daily Bollinger uptrend channel).\par \par \uc2 USD/THB - consolidation with upside bias. USD/THB may retreat slightly as the dollar eases against most other South East Asia \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The daily chart suggests a slightly overbought situation that might require a pullback to 31.72 to relieve some of the skew in market positioning. When speculative positioning is more balanced, there could again be more room to the upside. The pair could range within a 31.72-32.00 band for now. Dow Jones technical analysis suggests immediate support is at 31.80 (psychological support), then at 31.72 (daily Bollinger band standard deviation 2), before 31.50 (psychological support). Immediate resistance is at 32.00 (psychological resistance), then at 32.17 (Thursday's 3-year high), before 32.49 (monthly Bollinger uptrend channel).\par \par \uc2 USD/PHP - closed.\par \par \uc2 USD/IDR - pullback lower. USD/IDR could pullback toward 10,500 as the market reacts to the moves announced by the government Friday. The Indonesia finance ministry decided to temporarily scrap the mineral export quota, as well as to increase taxes on imported cars. Additionally, the government said it will open more industries to foreigners, all in an attempt to increase inflows, improve trade balance and therefore bring down the current account deficit. The rupiah has been under immense selling pressure due to its burgeoning deficit problem. The USD/IDR NDF contracts continue to trade at an exorbitant premium to the spot contract, a sign that dollar liquidity in the onshore market is still thin and investor confidence is low. Once the NDF premium begins to decline, we could see the rupiah stage a more significant recovery. Dow Jones technical analysis suggests immediate support for spot USD/IDR is at 10,700 (psychological support), then at 10,640 (daily Bollinger band standard deviation 2), before 10,500 (psychological support). Immediate resistance is at 10,800 (psychological resistance), before 11,000 (major psychological barrier).\par \par \uc2 USD/INR - reversal lower. Technical signs suggest the USD/INR could reverse lower, possibly to the base of the daily Bollinger uptrend channel at 61.74. The long-tailed doji candlestick Thursday followed by the bearish engulfing candlestick Friday confirms the reversal signal. The rupee has been able to salvage some of its losses after the government said there are no plans to install capital controls to stop the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's freefall. This calmed the market, which had been anticipating a larger exodus of investors taking flight in fear of capital controls to come. The multitude of problems facing India, beginning with its chronic current account deficit, have seen it collapse to an all-time low of 65.56 to the dollar. Dow Jones technical analysis suggests immediate support is at 63.00 (psychological support), then at 62.50 (psychological support), before 62.00 (psychological support). Immediate resistance is at 63.50 (psychological resistance), then at 64.00 (psychological resistance), before 65.00 (psychological resistance).\par \par \uc2 Write to Ewen Chew at ewen.chew@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 (This article is general financial information, not personalized investment advice, as it does not consider the unique circumstances affecting an individual reader's decision to buy or sell a specific security. Dow Jones does not warrant the accuracy, completeness or timeliness of the information in this article, and any errors will not be made the basis for any claim against Dow Jones. The author does not invest in the instruments or markets cited in this article.)\par \par \uc2 26 Aug 2013 20:51 EDT DJ  ASIA DAILY \b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2  OUTLOOK - Asia\par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE--The following are projected trading ranges and outlooks for nine secondary \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  pairs in Asia today: \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par           Immediate Range  Larger Range \par USD/CNY    6.1200-6.1224   6.1142-6.1305 \par USD/TWD    29.940-30.020   29.850-30.180 \par USD/KRW    1,113-1,118     1,110-1,120 \par USD/SGD    1.2800-1.2861   1.2788-1.2900 \par USD/MYR    3.3000-3.3200   3.2950-3.3500 \par USD/THB    32.00-32.17     31.80-32.49 \par USD/PHP    44.20-44.50     44.00-45.00 \par USD/IDR    10,800-11,000   10,700-11,500 \par USD/INR    64.00-64.50     63.50-65.00 \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Ranges are calculated using recent highs and lows, information on the placement of option strikes, and technical analysis--Bollinger Bands, Fibonacci levels, trendlines and moving averages.)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 USD/CNY - consolidation with downside bias. As the dollar softens a bit more overnight, weighed by the slippage in the U.S. 10-year Treasury yield, the PBOC dollar/yuan parity rate is also likely to fall. The USD/CNY has been stabilizing recently as investors remain confident that China is making the right moves to liberalize and restructure its economy, even if it means first swallowing the bitter pill of clamping down on massive shadow-bank lending. While other emerging \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in Asia have been getting routed in the past month, the yuan has been rock-steady. USD/CNY will likely stay biased lower within the Bollinger downtrend channel that caps now at 6.1224. Dow Jones technical analysis suggests immediate support for USD/CNY is at 6.1200 (psychological support), then at 6.1142 (base of Bollinger downtrend channel), before 6.1090 (record low). Immediate resistance is likely at 6.1224 (top of daily Bollinger downtrend channel), then at 6.1305 (20-day Bollinger mid), before 6.1387 (daily Bollinger uptrend channel).\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 USD/TWD - consolidation. USD/TWD is once again stuck inside the daily Ichimoku Cloud zone which spans 29.850-30.020. Unless the pair clears this area of technical support/resistance, USD/TWD will continue to drift sideways. The dollar may have lost some steam overnight due to a worse than expected U.S. Durable Goods Orders number, but improving jobs data from the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Dallas Fed\f28 \uc2 \f2  helped to mitigate some weakness. The dollar could get another boost closer to the September 17-18 FOMC where the Fed will decide whether to reduce monetary stimulus. Dow Jones technical analysis suggests immediate support is at 30.000 (psychological support), then at 29.940 (daily Bollinger downtrend channel), before 29.850 (base of daily Ichimoku Cloud). Immediate resistance is likely at 30.020 (top of daily Ichimoku Cloud and 20-day Bollinger mid), then at 30.180 (daily Bollinger uptrend channel), before 30.300 (top of daily Bollinger uptrend channel).\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 USD/KRW - consolidation. USD/KRW may range within 1,113-1,118 Tuesday as the dollar drifts sideways against the majors. Mixed U.S. data released overnight may motivate more traders and investors to sit on the sidelines till the Fed meeting Sept. 17-18. If, however, USD/KRW ends the day below 1,113, the daily Bollinger downtrend channel will come into play and could pull the dollar lower in the near term. Dow Jones technical analysis suggests immediate support is at 1,113 (daily Bollinger downtrend channel), then at 1,110 (psychological support), before 1,109 (base of daily Bollinger downtrend channel). Immediate resistance is at 1,118 (20-day Bollinger mid), then at 1,120 (psychological resistance), before 1,121 (daily Bollinger uptrend channel).\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 USD/SGD - upside bias. USD/SGD is on a path higher toward 1.2861 where the Bollinger uptrend channel top awaits. The dollar may be trading higher due to the bullish technical bias as the market lacks other cues for direction. Mixed U.S. data overnight did not make the Fed tapering picture any clearer, hence traders have to fall back on trading the chart signals. Singapore's manufacturing output rose 2.7% on-year in July, beating estimates for a 1.5% gain. But this improvement did nothing for the Sing dollar, as investors train their sights instead on what the U.S. dollar will do next. Dow Jones technical analysis shows immediate support is at 1.2800 (psychological support), then at 1.2788 (base of daily Bollinger uptrend channel), before 1.2750 (psychological support). Immediate resistance is at 1.2861 (top of daily Bollinger uptrend channel), before 1.2900 (top of weekly Bollinger uptrend channel and psychological resistance).\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 USD/MYR - upside bias. USD/MYR gaps higher to 3.3100 at the open from its previous close of 3.3035, setting a bullish tone immediately. The pair is on track to testing the 3-year high of 3.3225 notched last week. USD/MYR may be on its way to the top of the weekly Bollinger uptrend channel at 3.3500. Dollar dominance appears to be in full effect through South East Asia, bucking the relatively stable North Asia \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  such as the yuan, the won, and the Taiwan dollar. There will likely be \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  attempts by the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Malaysia central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  should the dollar keep rising, though it may not put a stop to ringgit depreciation; evidently, last week's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  only temporarily deterred dollar-buying. Dow Jones technical analysis suggests immediate support is at 3.3000 (psychological support), then at 3.2950 (base of daily Bollinger uptrend channel), before 3.2800 (psychological support). Immediate resistance is at 3.3200 (top of daily Bollinger uptrend channel), before 3.3500 (top of weekly Bollinger uptrend channel and psychological resistance).\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 USD/THB - consolidation with upside bias. USD/THB could be slightly overbought, the daily charts suggest, but the dollar continuing to rise against other South East Asia \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will likely keep USD/THB elevated as well. USD/THB may head for the monthly Bollinger uptrend channel at 32.49 in days ahead. Thailand's trade data released Monday showed a widening deficit caused by falling exports as well as increased imports, adding to concerns that Thai baht will be the next India rupee or Indonesia rupiah. Dow Jones technical analysis suggests immediate support is at 32.00 (psychological support), then at 31.80 (psychological support and daily Bollinger band standard deviation 2), before 31.50 (psychological support). Immediate resistance is at 32.17 (Thursday's 3-year high), before 32.49 (monthly Bollinger uptrend channel).\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 USD/PHP - upside bias. USD/PHP may gap higher Tuesday after reopening for the first time this week due to the Monday holiday. As the dollar continues to rally against South East Asia due to the region suffering from a recent decline in investor confidence, the peso will unlikely be exempt from another selloff. The USD/PHP charts do look slightly overbought ever since the pair made a 2.5-year high of 44.31, but this is more likely due to a lag in the technical indicators because of the recent string of Manila market closures. The dollar, which closed at 44.26 Friday, could test 44.50 soon - above which stoploss orders could be lurking. Dow Jones technical analysis suggests immediate support is at 44.20 (psychological support), then at 44.00 (psychological support), before 43.73 (base of daily Bollinger uptrend channel). Immediate resistance is likely at 44.50 (psychological resistance), then at 45.00 (psychological resistance), before 45.21 (monthly Ichimoku Cloud resistance).\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 USD/IDR - upside bias. The dollar is likely to continue rallying against the rupiah as investor confidence remains shaky, despite the measures announced last Friday by the Indonesia finance ministry to reduce trade imbalance and therefore improve the current account deficit. The rupiah has been under immense selling pressure since last month, hitting a 4-year low of 10,875 Monday due to its burgeoning deficit problem. The USD/IDR NDF contracts continue to trade at an exorbitant premium to the spot contract, a sign that dollar liquidity in the onshore market is still thin and investor confidence is low. Once the NDF premium begins to decline, we could see the rupiah stage a more significant recovery. Dow Jones technical analysis suggests immediate support for spot USD/IDR is at 10,800 (psychological support), then at 10,700 (psychological support), before 10,690 (daily Bollinger band standard deviation 2). Immediate resistance is at 11,000 (major psychological barrier), then at 11,500 (psychological resistance).\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 USD/INR - upwards bias. USD/INR looks likely to stay aloft and may range slightly higher between 64.00-65.56 for now. Aside from dollar demand ahead of the FOMC in September, where the Fed might announce a reduction in bond purchases, the fear of capital flight from India is also pushing USD/INR higher. Though the government said last week there are no plans to install capital controls, India has a poor track record of consistency in its policy, and hence rupee investors may not be consoled much. Dow Jones technical analysis suggests immediate support is at 64.00 (psychological support), then at 63.50 (psychological support), before 63.00 (psychological support). Immediate resistance is at 64.50 (psychological resistance), then at 65.00 (psychological resistance), before 65.56 (record high).\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Write to Ewen Chew at ewen.chew@wsj.com\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (This article is general financial information, not personalized investment advice, as it does not consider the unique circumstances affecting an individual reader's decision to buy or sell a specific security. Dow Jones does not warrant the accuracy, completeness or timeliness of the information in this article, and any errors will not be made the basis for any claim against Dow Jones. The author does not invest in the instruments or markets cited in this article.)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 27 Aug 2013 20:44 EDT DJ \par ASIA DAILY \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  OUTLOOK - Asia\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 SINGAPORE--The following are projected trading ranges and outlooks for nine secondary \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  pairs in Asia today: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par          Immediate Range  Larger Range \par USD/CNY    6.1221-6.1224  6.1139-6.1305 \par USD/TWD    29.940-30.000  29.920-30.180 \par USD/KRW    1,117-1,120    1,113-1,123 \par USD/SGD    1.2800-1.2876  1.2750-1.2900 \par USD/MYR    3.3300-3.3350  3.2800-3.3500 \par USD/THB    32.00-32.19    31.88-32.49 \par USD/PHP    44.20-44.50    44.00-45.00 \par USD/IDR    10,900-11,000  10,800-11,500 \par USD/INR    66.00-66.50    65.50-67.00 \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Ranges are calculated using recent highs and lows, information on the placement of option strikes, and technical analysis--Bollinger Bands, Fibonacci levels, trendlines and moving averages.)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 USD/CNY - downside bias. The yuan may be expected to gain as the U.S. dollar index slips - mainly due to USD/JPY tanking amid an intensifying scenario of global risk aversion. The PBOC yuan parity rate may come down toward 6.1600 from Tuesday's 6.1688, and could thus weigh spot USD/CNY down and back into the Bollinger downtrend channel that begins at 6.1221. While other emerging \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in Asia have been getting routed in the past month, the yuan has been rock-steady as investors begin to back the idea that China is indeed restructuring its economy for the better, even if it means enduring some short-term pain due to the clampdown on illegal bank lending. The yuan, currently up 1.8% for the year, is turning out to be Asia's best performer despite all the talk about the great China slowdown. Dow Jones technical analysis suggests immediate support for USD/CNY is at 6.1221 (daily Bollinger downtrend channel), then at 6.1200 (psychological support), before 6.1139 (base of Bollinger downtrend channel), ). Immediate resistance is likely at 6.1224 (top of daily Bollinger downtrend channel), then at 6.1305 (20-day Bollinger mid), before 6.1387 (daily Bollinger uptrend channel).\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 USD/TWD - consolidation. USD/TWD is still trapped inside the daily Ichimoku Cloud zone which now spans 29.920-30.000. Unless the pair clears this area of technical support/resistance, USD/TWD will continue to drift sideways. The dollar weakened a bit more overnight due to U.S. yields slipping because investors bought more U.S. Treasurys as a safe haven amid risk aversion related to Syria. While U.S. consumer confidence improved markedly and U.S. home prices rose - though at a slower pace - which suggest the Fed could indeed taper QE next month, the focus now is on dumping risky assets for safer ones, which could therefore keep the dollar supported. Dow Jones technical analysis suggests immediate support is at 29.940 (daily Bollinger downtrend channel), before 29.920 (base of daily Ichimoku Cloud). Immediate resistance is likely at 30.000 (top of daily Ichimoku Cloud and psychological resistance), then at 30.020 (20-day Bollinger mid), before 30.180 (daily Bollinger uptrend channel).\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 USD/KRW - possible upside bias. USD/KRW gaps up to open at 1,119 from its Tuesday close of 1,116.3, reflecting the escalation in risk aversion overnight. USD/KRW could break through the psychological barrier at 1,120 and then aim for the Ichimoku Cloud resistance at 1,123. Stocks are down 1.2% on the Kospi, led by Japan's Nikkei stumbling 2.4% after USD/JPY got dumped hard overnight, sending the yen's value spiking. Fear of the U.S. entering the fray in Syria is sending investors fleeing risky assets and opting for the safety of the U.S. dollar and also the U.S. Treasuries, which explains why the dollar is stronger in Asia Wednesday despite lower \f28 \uc2 \f2 U.S. Treasury\f28 \uc2 \f2  yields. Dow Jones technical analysis suggests immediate support is at 1,117 (20-day Bollinger mid), then at 1,113 (daily Bollinger downtrend channel), before 1,110 (psychological support). Immediate resistance is at 1,120 (psychological resistance), then at 1,121 (daily Bollinger uptrend channel), before 1,123 (daily Ichimoku Cloud resistance).\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 USD/SGD - upside bias. USD/SGD is tracking higher along the Bollinger uptrend channel, eyeing the top of the channel at 1.2876. Bullish technical signals aside, the fear of global risk aversion is hurting the Sing dollar as investors buy the U.S. dollar broadly in anticipation of further emerging market carnage. The USD/SGD pair has reacted much less to risk aversion than its direct neighbors USD/MYR and USD/IDR, but traders are now buying more USD/SGD, anticipating the pair to catch up with the rest of South East Asia in due course. Dow Jones technical analysis shows immediate support is at 1.2800 (psychological support and base of daily Bollinger uptrend channel), before 1.2750 (psychological support). Immediate resistance is at 1.2876 (top of daily Bollinger uptrend channel), before 1.2900 (top of weekly Bollinger uptrend channel and psychological resistance).\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 USD/MYR - upside bias. USD/MYR makes a second consecutive 3-year high of 3.3315, topping Tuesday's 3.3300, as global risk aversion goes into full swing, marked by strong buying in U.S. Treasuries - typically considered the safest investment. USD/MYR may be on its way to the top of the weekly Bollinger uptrend channel at 3.3500. USD/MYR will likely remain well-bid despite the slippage in the \f28 \uc2 \f2 U.S. Treasury\f28 \uc2 \f2  yield - which had a positive correlation with the dollar up till this week - simply because risk aversion will send investors away from risky assets such as the Malaysia ringgit. Dow Jones technical analysis suggests immediate support is at 3.3000 (psychological support and base of daily Bollinger uptrend channel), then at 3.2800 (psychological support), before 3.2750 (20-day Bollinger mid). Immediate resistance is at 3.3350 (top of monthly Ichimoku Cloud), before 3.3500 (top of weekly Bollinger uptrend channel and psychological resistance).\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 USD/THB - upside bias. USD/THB may climb to 32.49 - where the monthly Bollinger uptrend channel begins - as investors flee to the safety of the U.S. dollar amid an escalation of global risk aversion. Thailand is one of the South East Asia countries with a recently deteriorating outlook - trade data released Monday showed a widening deficit caused by falling exports as well as increased imports, adding to concerns that Thai baht could even be the next India rupee or Indonesia rupiah. Dow Jones technical analysis suggests immediate support is at 32.00 (psychological support), then at 31.88 (daily Bollinger band standard deviation 2), before 31.80 (psychological support). Immediate resistance is at 32.19 (Tuesday's 3-year high), before 32.49 (monthly Bollinger uptrend channel).\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 USD/PHP - upside bias. USD/PHP could make a beeline for 45.00 if stoploss orders, likely placed above 44.50 - are triggered. Emerging market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  are suffering across the board as risk aversion exacerbates due to the U.S. possibly entering the Syria conflict. While the Philippines is not deemed as risky as Indonesia or India, the large speculative bets on the peso - which accumulated earlier this year on expectations of the country getting an investment grade rating from all three major credit rating agencies, could now potentially be reversing. Dow Jones technical analysis suggests immediate support is at 44.20 (psychological support), then at 44.00 (psychological support), before 43.78 (base of daily Bollinger uptrend channel). Immediate resistance is likely at 44.50 (psychological resistance), then at 45.00 (psychological resistance), before 45.21 (monthly Ichimoku Cloud resistance).\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 USD/IDR - upside bias. The rupiah is likely to remain in the doldrums as risk aversion comes to a head. The U.S. dollar is rallying broadly even as \f28 \uc2 \f2 U.S. Treasury\f28 \uc2 \f2  yields subside - a sure sign that the flight to safe haven assets is real. USD/IDR could notch another 4-year high if it surpasses Tuesday's 10,905, despite the measures announced last Friday by the Indonesia finance ministry to reduce trade imbalance and therefore improve the current account deficit. The premium that the 1-month USD/IDR NDF contract commands over the spot price continues to widen, from around IDR600 to almost IDR800 late Tuesday, a sign that dollar liquidity in the onshore market is still thin and investor confidence is low. Dow Jones technical analysis suggests immediate support for spot USD/IDR is at 10,900 (psychological support), then at 10,800 (psychological support), before 10,740 (daily Bollinger band standard deviation 2). Immediate resistance is at 11,000 (major psychological barrier), then at 11,500 (psychological resistance).\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 USD/INR - upside bias. There still appears to be no end to the rupee's slide, as the dollar once again rose overnight in reaction to broad-based risk aversion. USD/INR looks likely to chalk up a new record high once it opens. In addition to the recent flight to safe haven assets - due to the fear of the U.S. being drawn into the Syria conflict - the possibility of capital flight from India is also pushing USD/INR higher. Though the government said last week there are no plans to install capital controls, India has a poor track record of consistency in its policy, hence rupee investors are still jittery and looking to take their money out while they can. Dow Jones technical analysis suggests immediate support is at 66.00 (psychological support), then at 65.50 (psychological support), before 65.00 (psychological support). Immediate resistance is at 66.50 (psychological resistance), before 67.00 (psychological resistance).\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Write to Ewen Chew at ewen.chew@wsj.com\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (This article is general financial information, not personalized investment advice, as it does not consider the unique circumstances affecting an individual reader's decision to buy or sell a specific security. Dow Jones does not warrant the accuracy, completeness or timeliness of the information in this article, and any errors will not be made the basis for any claim against Dow Jones. The author does not invest in the instruments or markets cited in this article.)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 28 Aug 2013 20:47 EDT DJ \par ASIA DAILY \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  OUTLOOK - Asia\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 SINGAPORE--The following are projected trading ranges and outlooks for nine secondary \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  pairs in Asia today: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par           Immediate Range  Larger Range \par USD/CNY    6.1200-6.1221   6.1135-6.1302 \par USD/TWD    30.000-30.060   29.950-30.180 \par USD/KRW     1,113-1,118     1,110-1,120 \par USD/SGD    1.2750-1.2791   1.2719-1.2800 \par USD/MYR    3.3045-3.3350   3.3000-3.3375 \par USD/THB     32.00-32.28     31.95-32.49 \par USD/PHP     44.50-44.80     44.20-45.00 \par USD/IDR    10,900-11,000   10,800-11,500 \par USD/INR     68.50-69.00     68.00-69.50 \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Ranges are calculated using recent highs and lows, information on the placement of option strikes, and technical analysis--Bollinger Bands, Fibonacci levels, trendlines and moving averages.)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 USD/CNY - downside bias. As long as the USD/CNY stays inside the daily Bollinger downtrend channel, which now caps at 6.1219, a technical downside bias will persist. However, the overnight bounce in the WSJ dollar index and USD/JPY could mean the PBOC will shift the USD/CNY parity rate higher accordingly - this could move the pair out of the downtrend channel. The yuan, curently up 1.8% for the year, is turning out to be Asia's best perfomer despite all the talk about China slowing down, and the deepening rout of emerging market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . Dow Jones technical analysis suggests immediate support for USD/CNY is at 6.1200 (psychological support), then at 6.1135 (base of Bollinger downtrend channel), before 6.1090 (record low). Immediate resistance is likely at 6.1221 (top of daily Bollinger downtrend channel), then at 6.1302 (20-day Bollinger mid), before 6.1386 (daily Bollinger uptrend channel).\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 USD/TWD - consolidation with possible upside bias. Signs of an upside break may be appearing in USD/TWD, which is on the edge of crossing the 20-day Bollinger mid and also above the daily Ichimoku Cloud resistance zone. A Thursday close above 30.060 might lend a bullish tone to USD/TWD and set it on a path toward the daily Bollinger uptrend channel at 30.180. The U.S. 10-year Treasury yield slipping a bit lower overnight could however mean that the dollar may trade with a slightly offered tone, and that could keep USD/TWD trapped within its recent range of 29.900-30.130. Dow Jones technical analysis suggests immediate support is at 30.000 (psychological support), then at 29.990 (daily Ichimoku Cloud support), before 29.950 (daily Bollinger downtrend channel). Immediate resistance is likely at 30.060 (20-day Bollinger mid), before 30.180 (daily Bollinger uptrend channel).\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 USD/KRW - downside bias. USD/KRW gaps lower open at 1,113 from its Wednesday close of 1,115.4, as investor sentiment improves overnight. USD/KRW could be headed lower if it ends Thursday below 1,113 and therefore within the bearish pull of the Bollinger downtrend channel. A break of the 1,110 psychological support could trigger more selling related to stoploss orders and push the dollar to the weekly Bollinger downtrend channel at 1,108. The change in risk appetite can best be seen in the Kospi, which is up 0.6% and leading the way for Asia thus far. Dow Jones technical analysis suggests immediate support is at 1,113 (daily Bollinger downtrend channel), then at 1,110 (psychological support), before 1,108 (weekly Bollinger downtrend channel). Immediate resistance is at 1,118 (20-day Bollinger mid resistance), then at 1,120 (psychological resistance), before 1,121 (daily Bollinger uptrend channel).\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 USD/SGD - consolidation. USD/SGD dropped out of the daily Bollinger uptrend channel, as the dollar eased on the back of the decline in the U.S. 10-year Treasury yield - which resulted from investors' flight to quality assets. USD/SGD could be consolidating in a lower range of 1.2718-1.2791 demarcated by the 20-day Bollinger mid support and the entrance to the Bollinger uptrend channel. The Singapore dollar, being a regional anchor, may also be benefiting from a reallocation of money from riskier South East Asia countries such as Indonesia and Malaysia, where their \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  have depreciated sharply over the last month, into Singapore. Dow Jones technical analysis shows immediate support is at 1.2750 (psychological support), then at 1.2719 (20-day Bollinger mid support), before 1.2700 (psychological support). Immediate resistance is at 1.2791 (daily Bollinger uptrend channel), then at 1.2800 (psychological resistance), before 1.2865 (top of daily Bollinger uptrend channel).\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 USD/MYR - pullback lower, upside bias to remain. USD/MYR is retreating to the base of the Bollinger uptrend channel as the dollar slips on improving investor sentiment. The dollar could hence find a base at 3.3045 but if it remains above this key level by the end of the day, the technical upside bias will remain. U.S. 10-year Treasury yields declining, as investors rush back into the traditional safe haven assets, may be pressuring the greenback slightly lower as well. Dow Jones technical analysis suggests immediate support is at 3.3045 (base of daily Bollinger uptrend channel), then at 3.3000 (psychological support), before 3.2800 (psychological support and 20-day Bollinger mid). Immediate resistance is at 3.3350 (top of monthly Ichimoku Cloud), then at 3.3375 (top of daily Bollinger uptrend channel), before 3.3500 (top of weekly Bollinger uptrend channel and psychological resistance).\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 USD/THB - consolidation, possible pullback lower. USD/THB could be running out of upwards momentum for now, noting the doji candlestick pattern formed Wednesday. The dollar is a tad weaker across the board due to improving sentiment sparked by the relief rally in equities. USD/THB may decline toward the psychological support at 32.00 but the larger uptrend is not likely to be negated unless the pair falls below 31.62 and therefore exits the Bollinger uptrend channel. Dow Jones technical analysis suggests immediate support is at 32.00 (psychological support), then at 31.95 (daily Bollinger band standard deviation 2), before 31.80 (psychological support). Immediate resistance is at 32.28 (Wednesday's 3-year high), before 32.49 (monthly Bollinger uptrend channel).\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 USD/PHP - possible pullback lower. USD/PHP may experience a profit-taking selloff as the pair now looks very much overbought on the daily chart - price action has been way above the upper Bollinger band (standard deviation 2) for the past four sessions. The dollar is weaker in Asia Thursday as a relief rally takes place in stocks and South East Asia \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . Dow Jones technical analysis suggests immediate support is at 44.50 (psychological support), then at 44.20 (psychological support), before 44.00 (psychological support). Immediate resistance is likely at 44.80 (psychological resistance), then at 45.00 (psychological resistance), before 45.21 (monthly Ichimoku Cloud resistance).\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 USD/IDR - consolidation lower. A pullback in dollar strength appears to be the theme of the day for South East Asia, as investor sentiment improves and stocks in Asia mount a relief rally. The rupiah could hence get a reprieve and recover toward 10,800 to the dollar. A key indicator to watch in order to gauge sentiment for the rupiah is whether the USD/IDR NDF premium subsides. The premium that the 1-month USD/IDR NDF contract commands over the spot price traded to almost IDR800 Wednesday, a sign that dollar liquidity in the onshore market is still thin and investor confidence is low. If this NDF premium falls to below IDR500, there could be more follow through in the rupiah recovery. Dow Jones technical analysis suggests immediate support for spot USD/IDR is at 10,900 (psychological support), then at 10,800 (psychological support), before 10,790 (daily Bollinger band standard deviation 2). Immediate resistance is at 11,000 (major psychological barrier), then at 11,500 (psychological resistance).\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 USD/INR - consolidation lower. The rupee could enjoy a brief relief rally as the dollar weakens broadly in Asia Thursday. The market is almost getting used to new record lows being set for the INR, especially with talk of a 100.00 USD/INR call option recently traded in the market - a bet that the rupee could fall to 100 to the dollar. The rupee last closed at 68.80 - the second consecutive record low this week. Investors have been especially bearish the rupee as the rapid depreciation has raised the possibility of capital flight from India. Though the government said last week there are no plans to install capital controls, India has a poor track record of consistency in its policy, hence rupee investors are still jittery and looking to take their money out while they can. Dow Jones technical analysis suggests immediate support is at 68.50 (psychological support), then at 68.00 (psychological support), before 67.50 (psychological support). Immediate resistance is at 69.00(psychological resistance), before 70.00 (major psychological resistance).\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Write to Ewen Chew at ewen.chew@wsj.com\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (This article is general financial information, not personalized investment advice, as it does not consider the unique circumstances affecting an individual reader's decision to buy or sell a specific security. Dow Jones does not warrant the accuracy, completeness or timeliness of the information in this article, and any errors will not be made the basis for any claim against Dow Jones. The author does not invest in the instruments or markets cited in this article.)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 August 28, 2013 20:47 ET (00:47 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020130826e98q00038\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
